window.PHARMA_BOOK_SEARCH_INDEX=[["content/c01.html#pdf-001",1,"EVOLUTION OF THE PHARMACEUTICAL MARKET","GUTSAGA SERZAS GUTSAGA EVOLUTION OF THE PHARMACEUTICAL MARKET How Functions, Technologies, and Data Change Competitive Power 2nd edition, revised and expanded PHARMA MANAGEMENT SOLUTIONS, UAB Kaunas, 2026"],["content/c02.html#pdf-002",2,"Publication details","Publication details Author Serzas Gutsaga Title Evolution of the Pharmaceutical Market Subtitle How Functions, Technologies, and Data Change Competitive Power Edition 2nd edition, revised and expanded Publisher PHARMA MANAGEMENT SOLUTIONS, UAB Place of publication Kaunas Year of publication 2026 Language of publication English © Serzas Gutsaga, 2026 All rights reserved. Bibliographic information is available on the portal of the Lithuanian Integral Library Information System (LIBIS), ibiblioteka.lt ISBN 978-609-96676-1-4 Print run: 100 copies. Printed by: REPRO, Kaunas, Lithuania, www.repro.lt"],["content/c03.html#pdf-006",3,"Contents","1/2 Contents Book map — from the market constraint to the next capability. BEFORE THE BOOK From the Author · 1 Who This Book Is For · 2 How to Read This Book · 3 PART I · MARKET, MONEY, AND POWER 01 Where Does the Market End? 4 02 How Can We Increase Cash Flow? 6 03 How Does the Pharmaceutical Family Divide the Money? 9 PART II · EVOLUTION OF THE PHARMACY CHAIN 04 Availability Wins the Customer 13 05 What Does a High Availability Level Cost? 19 TOOL Inventory Root Cause Audit 26 06 Can One Hundred Pharmacies Manage Inventory the Same Way? 31 07 Why Create Another Storage Location If We Want to Reduce inventory? 40 08 Does Buying Cheaper Always Mean Earning More? 51 09 When Does Inventory Become Excess? 63"],["content/c03.html#pdf-007",4,"Contents","2/2 Contents Book map — from the market constraint to the next capability. PART II · EVOLUTION OF THE PHARMACY CHAIN · CONTINUED 10 Do You Have Several Products—or Do You Have a Category? 73 11 Should the Entire Assortment Be Physically Stocked in Every Pharmacy? 83 12 Should People Walk to the Product? 93 13 What Exactly Are You Going to Scale? 105 14 What Happens When One Chain Systematically Performs Better Than Others? 115 Management Dashboard · Eight Numbers That Reveal the Quality of the System 124 PART III · CHOOSING A DISTRIBUTOR 15 When Do Customers Become Competitors? 137 16 Three Strategic Choices 145 17 Independent Pharmacies, Concentrated Power 151 PART IV · MANUFACTURER 18 The Manufacturer Beyond the Factory 159 19 Who Controls Market Access? 162 20 Routes to the Patient 166 PART V · IT AND GOVERNMENT 21 Who Controls the Digital Entry Point? 172 22 Government Changes the Game 181 PART VI · STRATEGIC FINALE 23 What Must Remain Under Our Control? 186 24 The Last Bottleneck 188 Finale · The Final Growth Check 197 PRACTICUM Where Is Your Market Now? 199"],["content/c04.html#pdf-003",5,"FROM THE AUTHOR","FROM THE AUTHOR The pharmaceutical market is one big family The pharmaceutical market is a close-knit environment: manufacturers, distributors, pharmacy chains, and technology companies work side by side for years, negotiate, and compete. Everyone depends on everyone else, yet each seeks a stronger position. Over time, not only company size changes, but functions do as well. A chain builds a warehouse, begins purchasing directly, manages assortment and data—and as those functions move to the chain, so do money, dependencies, and influence. Why do companies operating in the same market end up in completely different positions after a few years? How positions within the family change Constraint Remove the constraint Gain an advantage The market catches up New constraint One company grows and becomes stronger. Another grows—and merely scales its own problems. The difference starts with a new capability: a company removes a constraint better than others and gains an advantage. But the market learns, and yesterday’s advantage becomes the norm. Then the competition moves on—to capital, purchasing, assortment, data, decision speed, and scaling. I have worked with inventory and flow management systems since 2004 and with the pharmaceutical market since 2012. Projects I have participated in have covered 10,000+ pharmacies, including chains of up to 4,500 locations, from Mongolia to Argentina. In this book, I distinguish among project data, field observations, practical estimates, and teaching models. The goal is not to predict the market, but to show the mechanism of its evolution and a company’s next step. This book does not offer one right answer. It helps you see where to look for it."],["content/c05.html#pdf-004",6,"Owners and shareholders","This book is written for people who do more than simply work in the pharmaceutical market—people who want to understand how it is changing and where the next opportunity for growth is emerging. Owners and shareholders To see not only current financial results, but also which functions and capabilities will determine the company’s market power tomorrow. CEOs and executives To distinguish symptoms from systemic constraints, understand the consequences of growth, and determine which capability the company needs to build next. Functional leaders Purchasing, supply chain management, commercial management, logistics, IT, finance, category management, operations—to see your function not in isolation, but as part of the overall system for creating competitive advantage. Professionals who want to grow For those who already know their function well but want to move from executing individual tasks to understanding the business as a system and prepare for the next level of responsibility. It does not matter what position you hold today. What matters more is which decisions you want to be able to make tomorrow."],["content/c06.html#pdf-005",7,"First pass — follow the logic","You do not have to read this book like a business novel—from the first page to the last without stopping. It is designed as a working map: first see the overall logic, then return to the chapters and questions relevant to your company. First pass — follow the logic Do not try to retain every metric, formula, and detail. Focus on the transition: constraint → capability → economic result → change in power → next constraint. Within your function — go deeper When a chapter touches your area of responsibility, stop at the mechanics, metrics, and examples. For the other chapters, on the first pass it is enough to understand the principle. The “stage check” is not an exam These are questions not about your memory, but about your company. If they cannot be answered quickly with numbers and rules, or if no one can clearly name the person responsible, mark the page and return to it with your team. After reading — return to the book Use the relevant chapters before a strategy session, a process change, an investment, or a new stage of growth. What seems like someone else’s function today may become your constraint tomorrow. First reading — understand the map. Read again — work with a specific constraint. The main thing is to understand what the company needs to do next."],["content/c07.html#pdf-008",8,"Where Does the Market End?","CHAPTER 1 One question that changes how you see market size The room is full—more than three hundred people. Manufacturers, distributors, pharmacy chains, technology companies. If you have worked in the pharmaceutical market for a long time, many faces here are familiar. You take your seat and wait for the usual opening. Typically, the speaker comes out and shows numbers and charts. The audience listens. Questions come later. But this time, everything happens differently. What prevents the pharmaceutical market from earning more? The speaker falls silent. A few seconds of silence. You catch yourself already looking for the answer in your own company, your own negotiations, your own numbers."],["content/c07.html#pdf-009",9,"Where Does the Market End?","First—silence. Not the short pause after which a speaker answers their own question. He is actually waiting. Someone looks up from their phone. A slight smile moves through the rows—three hundred people are simultaneously trying to work out how they themselves would answer. …and then the room comes alive. Price. Government regulation. Competition. Low margin. We need to sell more. More pharmacies. Almost every one of these answers has already crossed your mind. They are all reasonable. But the speaker changes the scale of the question. Speaker: Tomorrow, manufacturers could produce twice as many medicines. Would the market automatically become twice as large? Audience: No. Speaker: What if we opened twice as many pharmacies? At the foundation of the market are people who need treatment and the money that they—or someone on their behalf—can direct toward that treatment."],["content/c08.html#pdf-010",10,"MECHANISM 1","CHAPTER 2 Three basic mechanisms—one of them changes the market boundary itself By now you already know: there will be no ready-made answer from the stage. Every next step goes back to the audience—and first passes through you. Speaker: Good. Then how can we increase the market’s cash flow? What options are there? Audience: Raise the price. MECHANISM 1 Price: more money for the same volume If a unit of product costs more for the same volume of treatment, more money flows through the market. The most direct mechanism. Speaker: Can we raise the price indefinitely? Audience: No. Audience: There is regulation. Audience: There are competitors. Audience: The patient simply will not be able to pay. There is less fear of getting it wrong now, and more excitement. You, too, find yourself testing each comment against your own company. Price can increase the flow, but it has a limit: purchasing power, competition, and regulation—often all at once. Speaker: How can we bring more money into the system without limiting ourselves to what the patient pays at the pharmacy today? Audience: Insurance."],["content/c08.html#pdf-011",11,"MECHANISM 2","MECHANISM 2 Insurance: people who are not sick today also pay Speaker: In an insurance model, money comes in up front. But something else matters more: the common pool is funded not only by those who already need treatment. People who are healthy today also make contributions. Audience: So insurance does not simply shift payment over time—it broadens the group of people who finance the system. HOW THE COMMON INSURANCE POOL GROWS PEOPLE WITH DIFFERENT NEEDS some are healthy today; others already need treatment COMMON INSURANCE POOL insurance premiums TREATMENT when a specific person needs it BEFORE ILLNESS money comes in up front BROADER BASE healthy people pay too MORE FUNDS the system collects more EXAMPLE: US HEALTH INSURANCE REFORM In the US system, the size of the subsidy for an insurance premium depends on household income: some participants receive support, while others pay the premium in full. In a common insurance pool, contributions from healthier participants help cover the costs of those who need treatment more often. Key effect: insurance broadens the funding base. The broader the common pool, the more money the system can collect before a specific illness occurs. Example sources: the official US health insurance portal; Congressional Research Service."],["content/c08.html#pdf-012",12,"How Can We Increase Cash Flow?","Speaker: Don't charge more money for existing demand. Don't merely change the timing of financing. Then what? Audience: Sell something else. Speaker: For example? Audience: Cosmetics. Audience: Baby products. Audience: Personal care products. Audience: Health and personal care products. And this creates another shift. To buy many of these products, a person does not necessarily need treatment. The pharmacy begins to work not only with money directed specifically toward a medical need. You look at the three basic mechanisms and notice that the question that seemed too large only a few minutes ago has already turned into a map. PRICE More money from existing demand. More money from existing demand. INSURANCE Money is collected in advance, and the payer base is broader: people who are not currently ill also pay. Money is collected in advance, and the payer base is broader: people who are not currently ill also pay. NOT JUST MEDICINES Spending enters the pharmacy channel even without a current illness. Spending enters the pharmacy channel even without a current illness. Price and insurance change the economics of meeting medical needs. A non-drug assortment expands the boundary of spending that can enter the pharmacy channel. Additional services can be a supporting element where permitted, but they are not a separate basic mechanism on this map. Good. The money is there. Who gets it?"],["content/c09.html#pdf-013",13,"Who will get this money?","Who will get this money? One market. Many participants. One system. We have already covered the size of the market. But the next turn brings the discussion much closer to everyone in the room. Speaker: Who is competing for the money that flows through the pharmaceutical market? Pharmacies Distributors Manufacturers Insurers Hospitals Technology and data Government Speaker: In other words, a significant part of this system is sitting in this room right now. You look around. Behind the familiar company names, a single system begins to emerge—participants in one market connected by a common flow of money. Each participant depends on the others, yet each seeks to increase its share of the market's overall money flow. We depend on one another. But who among us will get more?"],["content/c09.html#pdf-014",14,"A fair share or a strong position?","A fair share or a strong position? Speaker: We can imagine an ideal model. Everyone receives a fair share for the value they create. Manufacturer: I developed and produced the product. Here is my fair return. Distributor: I finance inventory, operate the warehouse, and provide logistics. Here is my share. Pharmacy: I provide access to the patient and the retail infrastructure. Here is my share. Speaker: Is that how the market works? From the audience: Of course not. Speaker: And who usually gets more? From the audience: Whoever is stronger. WHO HAS MORE POWER. Now the question is no longer about fairness. It is about what a participant actually controls and how difficult it is for others to bypass or replace it. Speaker: Then what exactly gives a participant more power?"],["content/c09.html#pdf-015",15,"Sources of power in pharma: some examples","Sources of power in pharma: some examples This is not a complete list. Below are several typical sources. Purchasing volume Exclusivity Access to the patient Data Influence on therapy choice Logistics Speaker: This is not an exhaustive list. Power emerges where a participant controls something important that others find difficult to bypass or quickly replace. Company scale can strengthen a position, but scale alone does not explain power. What matters more is which critical functions the participant controls. FUNCTION → MONEY → DEPENDENCE → INFLUENCE You look around the room differently now. You see not so much the companies as the functions they control. It is control over a critical function that becomes a source of power."],["content/c09.html#pdf-016",16,"LET'S START WITH AVAILABILITY.","Speaker: Which participant should we start with? From the audience: The pharmacy. Speaker: Why? From the audience: It is closest to the customer. The pharmacy is where the flow of money turns into an actual purchase. And if a chain wants to strengthen its position, the first condition seems almost obvious: the right product must be available at the right time. If several pharmacies are nearby, why should the customer choose one particular pharmacy? The presentation then moves along the entire chain. At every stage, the same question: which function is moving, who gains control, and how does power shift as a result? The conversation did not end on stage—it simply moved into the hallways. Manufacturers discuss chains. Chains discuss direct purchasing. Distributors discuss customer functions. Some debate data; others, access to the patient. In a short presentation, almost every market participant had recognized themselves in it—not only through the question “What does our company do?” but through a more direct one: how do we depend on one another, what are we competing for, and who is gradually taking over functions, money, and influence? What usually remains between the lines in negotiations had suddenly been said in public—in front of all sides of the market at once. COMPANIES → FUNCTIONS → CONTROL → POWER In the book, we can move more slowly. Now we will examine each transition in detail. LET'S START WITH AVAILABILITY. For a manufacturer, distributor, or technology company, this section is a map of the functions the customer gradually brings in-house, changing your own role in the process."],["content/c10.html#pdf-017",17,"An unavailable product can cost you more than one sale—it can cost you a future customer.","PART II · STAGE 1 An unavailable product can cost you more than one sale—it can cost you a future customer. Availability wins the customer: when the needed product is out of stock (OOS), the customer does not wait—they go where the probability of finding it is higher. 1. PHARMACY A the needed product is OOS The product is OOS. Today's sale is lost. 2. PHARMACY B product found The product is available. The customer's problem is solved. 3. REPEAT success repeats B starts to be perceived as the more reliable location. 4. NEW ROUTE the customer starts with B The next search now starts with B. Availability → successful visit → repeat choice → habitual route A stockout is not only about lost margin. It also creates the risk of losing the customer's future route. A missed sale today can change a future choice. On the first visit, a stockout can easily be dismissed as a one-off. The customer simply solves the problem at a nearby pharmacy, while the first location sees only one lost sale. If the situation repeats, more than today's basket changes: the customer starts the search where the probability of finding the needed product is higher. The competitor then gains an advantage in the next choice as well."],["content/c10.html#pdf-018",18,"A small difference in Availability Level can be accompanied by a large difference in sales.","THE COMPETITIVE GAP A small difference in Availability Level can be accompanied by a large difference in sales. A recurring field observation: an advantage of about 5 percentage points in Availability Level was repeatedly accompanied by a sales difference of around 20%. AVAILABILITY LEVEL 93% Out of 100 demand occasions, the customer finds the product in 93. 7 times, the customer leaves without making a purchase. +5 ADDITIONAL SUCCESSFUL OCCASIONS for every 100 purchase opportunities. 98% Out of 100 demand occasions, the customer finds the product in 98. Only 2 times does the customer leave without making a purchase. HOW 5 ADDITIONAL SUCCESSFUL OCCASIONS TURN INTO A LARGE SALES DIFFERENCE MORE COMPLETED PURCHASES The customer finds the product and buys it now. HIGHER SATISFACTION A positive experience builds trust. MORE REPEAT VISITS The customer returns more often and starts the search at their “usual” pharmacy. IN FIELD OBSERVATIONS—AROUND ~20% SALES DIFFERENCE The customer does not see a percentage. They see the outcome: the product is found or it is not. Taken together, these outcomes create a noticeable difference in sales. IMPORTANT NOTE ON THE METRIC We do not simply measure the number of SKUs on the shelf; we assess the economic risk of a missed sale. A stockout of a fast-moving product and of a product sold once a year should not carry the same weight. NOT A UNIVERSAL FORMULA ~20% is a recurring field observation, not a guaranteed result. The effect depends on the starting Availability Level, competition, and customer behavior."],["content/c10.html#pdf-019",19,"How do we measure Availability Level?","METHODOLOGY AND CASES How do we measure Availability Level? We do not treat all SKUs as equal. The metric weights the economic risk of a missed sale by estimated lost margin. FORMULA Potential margin = actual margin + estimated lost margin Availability Level = actual margin / potential margin × 100% Estimated lost margin = stockout days × average daily consumption × margin per unit DATA FROM SPECIFIC PROJECTS Siberia and the Urals Siberia and the Urals: 88% → 98% After inventory management was changed, Availability Level rose to approximately 98%. Customers increasingly perceived the chain as a place where the probability of finding the needed product was higher. AlphaPharm, Armenia 94% → 99.5% The company set a strict target: above 99%, ideally around 99.5%, even without improving inventory turnover. The target was achieved. A high Availability Level stops being merely an internal operating metric. It becomes part of the market position. THE METRIC THEREFORE ANSWERS TWO QUESTIONS: what share of potential margin the company did not lose because the product was available; how high the economic risk of unmet demand is. That is why I treat Availability Level as a financial metric that also serves as an approximate indicator of service. For AlphaPharm, the target of around 99.5% was deliberately set well above a merely “good” level. The point was to make a high probability of product availability a stable characteristic of the chain. The final 0.5 percentage points matter because they further reduce the number of situations in which a customer has to look for the product elsewhere."],["content/c10.html#pdf-020",20,"From Availability Level to competitive advantage.","THE DIGITAL BLIND SPOT From Availability Level to competitive advantage. A high Availability Level is no longer just an internal metric. Real management requires seeing not only today's stock balance, but its history. TODAY'S STOCK BALANCE IS NOT ENOUGH ERP/POS stores events sale receipt transfer document Management requires a history of inventory status SKU × PHARMACY × DAY Historical Availability Level requires a daily status for every item at every location—not merely a transaction log. Over years of work, I have not encountered an ERP/POS system in which the daily status history of every SKU and an estimate of lost margin were a fully fledged standard tool. I have seen another workable approach: companies saved daily stock balances in a separate repository and analyzed the history there. This creates a separate analytics layer on top of the operating system. MANAGEMENT NEEDS THE TREND After any management decision, there should be one simple question: did it get better or worse? 6 months → 90 days → 30 days → today WHAT WAS YOUR AVAILABILITY LEVEL 90 DAYS AGO?"],["content/c10.html#pdf-021",21,"One chain-wide percentage is not enough.","MANAGEMENT VIEW One chain-wide percentage is not enough. The overall chain metric shows the current state. But management action begins when a leader breaks it down into dimensions and immediately sees where performance is weaker. 1. Suppliers The supplier view shows which suppliers have a lower Availability Level and where the causes need to be investigated. Supplier Availability Level Biofarm 91% Medica Trade 93% PharmaPlus 95% NovaPharm 97% A low metric does not prove that the supplier is at fault, but it shows where to ask the next question. 2. Category managers This is not a description of their functions, but a management view: who is measured and by what outcome. Manager Kowalski 89% Shevchenko 92% Novak 94% Melnik When people know how they are measured, they manage the category differently. 3. Product categories It is especially important to see traffic-driving categories—deviations there affect the customer's route more quickly. Category Pain relief 90% Cold and flu Children's products Chronic therapy Traffic-driving categories require separate attention. 4. Pharmacies / locations The same KPI shows exactly where the problem appears at the individual-location level. Pharmacy Pharmacy No. 102 88% Pharmacy No. 103 Pharmacy No. 101 Pharmacy No. 118 This is how an overall metric turns into targeted action. A manager looks not only at the overall percentage, but at exactly who is pulling the result down."],["content/c10.html#pdf-023",22,"Availability Level affects four outcomes at once","MANAGEMENT VIEW Availability Level affects four outcomes at once The same metric matters to different functions across the company. It helps them speak the same language and make aligned decisions. SERVICE The customer finds the needed products and has a better experience. This increases loyalty and purchase frequency. ECONOMICS Lost margin declines. A stockout on the shelf means lost sales in monetary terms. AVAILABILITY LEVEL One metric. Multiple outcomes at once. MARKET It helps maintain competitiveness and protect the company's position. Customers are less likely to leave for competitors because of stockouts. MANAGEABILITY It provides objective control over results. It makes the effects of decisions visible and keeps the team focused on execution. Do not reward the team solely for a high Availability Level. The simplest way to improve this metric is to fill pharmacies with excess inventory. Service will indeed improve, but tied-up capital, write-off risk, and operating costs will rise with it. The economics may ultimately get worse. Managing availability alone can lead to: Availability rises Inventory increases Capital is tied up Write-offs increase Inventory turnover slows How do you maintain a high Availability Level without buying it with excess inventory? That requires a second metric. A high Availability Level is not a goal in itself. A second metric is needed."],["content/c11.html#pdf-024",23,"How much capital does a high Service Level cost?","PART II · STAGE 2 How much capital does a high Service Level cost? A high Availability Level improves service, but it requires inventory. Inventory is money that could be working in the business. Service and capital therefore cannot be optimized independently of each other. Availability Level is how we measure service inside the company: how much margin we lose when a product is missing. Service Level is how the customer and the market see it: how well we serve compared with competitors in the same area. FROM PRACTICE I have repeatedly seen a high Availability Level become an end in itself. A chain nearly eliminated stockouts, but held far too much inventory in the process. At that point, part of the service was effectively being paid for with working capital. That is why I always look at Availability Level and Days of Inventory together. Two metrics. Four scenarios. Availability Level × Days of Inventory The two metrics cannot be optimized independently. AVAILABILITY LEVEL high low TARGET High service and efficient use of capital target zone SERVICE BOUGHT WITH CAPITAL Good service, but excess inventory CAPITAL SAVED AT THE CUSTOMER'S EXPENSE Risk of losing sales and loyalty WORST-CASE SCENARIO Low service and tied-up capital DAYS OF INVENTORY Availability wins the customer. Faster inventory turnover releases capital for growth. The question is not how to raise Availability Level at any cost. The question is what Service Level we can afford and what it costs."],["content/c11.html#pdf-025",24,"Inventory is capital waiting for a sale.","CAPITAL ON THE SHELF Inventory is capital waiting for a sale. When we purchase a product, cash becomes inventory. While the product sits on the shelf, that capital is not working. When the product is sold, it turns back into cash that can be used for another purchase, opening a pharmacy, expanding the assortment, or investing. A SIMPLE CASH CYCLE The faster the cycle, the more business the same amount of capital can support. CASH Purchase product INVENTORY Product in pharmacies SALE Product with the customer Funds returned Days of inventory is a simple measure of capital velocity. It shows how many days of current sales the average inventory balance can cover. Formula: Days of Inventory = Average inventory at purchase cost / Average daily cost of goods sold The fewer days of inventory at the same Service Level, the faster capital returns to cash. Example: how one metric releases capital. Assume average daily cost of goods sold is €1 million. 35 days of inventory = €35 million in inventory 20 days of inventory = €20 million in inventory Difference = €15 million of capital that can be used for growth The example is simplified, but it shows the order of magnitude of the effect at a comparable level of customer service. Author's observation For an owner, this formula becomes especially important during growth. One extra day of inventory, multiplied across hundreds of pharmacies and thousands of SKUs, no longer looks trivial. That is capital that has to come from somewhere. This is why I read days of inventory not as a warehouse KPI, but as the speed at which money returns to the business. Faster inventory turnover while maintaining Availability Level allows the same capital to support a larger scale of business."],["content/c11.html#pdf-026",25,"Fast inventory turnover turns supplier payment terms into a financial resource.","TIME RESOURCE Fast inventory turnover turns supplier payment terms into a financial resource. If a product is sold before the supplier payment is due, the cash returns to the business and works for it for a period of time. This creates a window of financial flexibility and allows the chain to expand while reducing the need for additional external financing. How the financial window arises Day 0 Product received from supplier Day 20 the product is sold and the cash returns to the business 40-day financial window: the money works in the business—new purchases, pharmacy openings, other opportunities Day 60 Supplier payment due date TURNOVER FASTER THAN PAYMENT: cash returns before the supplier has to be paid 20 days of turnover < 60 days of payment terms = 40-day financial window Capital works in the business; the need for additional credit falls; financial flexibility increases; more growth opportunities become available TURNOVER SLOWER THAN PAYMENT The supplier must be paid before the cash from sales returns. 70 days of turnover > 60 days of payment terms = a shortfall in own funds. The company has to pay with its own cash or borrow; the financial burden rises; flexibility falls; and the risk of cash shortfalls increases. MANAGER CHECKLIST Reducing inventory genuinely improves the business if: 1 Availability Level (service) has not deteriorated; 2 days of inventory have decreased; 3 inventory turns before the supplier payment is due. Important to remember Supplier payment terms are not free money. Every delivery has its own due date, while the business still has taxes, payroll, and other obligations. The financial window is a resource that must be managed responsibly. Reducing inventory is not worthwhile for the metric's sake. The goal is to release capital without losing Service Level."],["content/c11.html#pdf-027",26,"When money starts working again, the business can grow.","DATA FROM A SPECIFIC PROJECT When money starts working again, the business can grow. In one project, the chain's days of inventory fell from approximately 35–40 days to below 20 days while maintaining a high Availability Level. BEFORE: 35–40 days of inventory FROM A CONVERSATION WITH AN OWNER I remember this call because, for the first time, the financial effect was expressed not as percentages and formulas, but as a very simple question. “I suddenly have a lot of money in the account. What should I do with it?” “Open pharmacies.” AFTER THE PROJECT Later, this chain grew from about 70 to 1,000 pharmacies. I do not attribute that growth to a single metric: it required strategy, a team, locations, purchasing, acquisitions, and many other decisions. But faster inventory turnover released working capital and was one of the factors that made faster expansion possible. Capital today means pharmacies tomorrow. WHAT WAS THE RESULT? AFTER: <20 days of inventory RESULT; Less capital tied up in inventory; High Availability Level maintained; Resources became available for the next stage of growth KEY TAKEAWAY: Reducing days of inventory releases capital. That capital can be directed to business development."],["content/c11.html#pdf-028",27,"How the manager uses inventory turnover","MANAGEMENT VIEW How the manager uses inventory turnover Inventory turnover is not just one number for the whole chain. It becomes more useful when a manager breaks it down by management dimensions and looks for persistent deviations. 1. Manufacturer / supplier If comparable products sell at similar rates but one supplier requires more days of inventory, that is a reason to investigate. Supplier “VectorPharm”—52 days Supplier “Polmed”—44 days Supplier “NovaPharm”—36 days Why does its assortment tie up more capital? Possible causes: large minimum batches · long lead time · purchasing above need. 2. Category manager / buyer If five portfolios run at 34–38 days of inventory while one consistently holds 50, that is already a management deviation. Shevchenko—50 days Koval—41 days Novak—36 days Causes and incentives: seasonality · large batch · purchasing for a discount · individual purchasing terms. 3. Pharmacy / location. If the chain averages 35 days while a specific location holds 47, there is no need to investigate the entire chain. Pharmacy No. 214—47 days Pharmacy No. 108—42 days Pharmacy No. 305—34 days Drill into this pharmacy. Look for: local excess · weak customer traffic · ordering errors. 4. Category / sales pattern. Different categories and demand patterns carry different inventory, so they need to be compared separately. Cold and flu—49 days Gastrointestinal—43 days Dermatology—31 days Special attention: traffic-driving products and seasonal tails. The point is not suspicion. The point is transparency. The metric itself proves nothing. A strong and persistent deviation shows where to start the investigation. The metric does not deliver a verdict. It shows where to look next."],["content/c11.html#pdf-029",28,"How to find a deviation and drill down to a specific SKU","FROM THE METRIC TO A SPECIFIC CASE How to find a deviation and drill down to a specific SKU The metric is the beginning, not the end, of the analysis. Its value is that it quickly shows where to look for the cause. Your task is to move systematically from the overall level to a specific case. STEP-BY-STEP DRILL-DOWN: FROM GENERAL TO SPECIFIC 1. CHAIN 35 days of inventory. Where is the deviation? 2. PHARMACY 48 days of inventory. Which pharmacy? 3. MANAGER / SUPPLIER contribution to the deviation. Who is responsible? 4. SKU unusually high stock balance. Which product? 5. EVENT 5 units bought when demand required 1. What happened? EXAMPLE FROM PRACTICE On Sundays, the owner reviewed purchases for individual pharmacies. He saw that one pharmacy had bought five units of a product where demand required one. On Monday, the person responsible had to explain: “Why did we buy five when we needed one?” This kind of transparency changes behavior quickly: unusual purchases stop disappearing into overall turnover—they remain in the history and are visible to everyone. The metric has brought us to a specific case. How to prove the root cause and determine which process needs to change is the next tool: root-cause audit."],["content/c11.html#pdf-031",29,"How much business can the available capital support?","STAGE CHECK · FOR MANAGEMENT How much business can the available capital support? Inventory is not a goal; it is a tool. It should provide a high Service Level and be efficient enough to release capital for business growth. 1. SERVICE Are customers getting the products they need? Availability Level is on target; lost sales are not increasing; complaints are not increasing. 2. CAPITAL Is inventory becoming more efficient? days of inventory are decreasing; more sales are generated with the same capital; cash is being released for growth. 3. TRANSPARENCY Can you see where the capital is tied up? It is clear which pharmacies / managers account for it; SKUs with excess inventory are identified; trends and accountability are visible. 4. SCALE What can we do with the capital we have released? Open new pharmacies; expand the assortment in key categories; support marketing and promotions; strengthen our market position. THE MAIN TRAP Reduce inventory so far that sales disappear with it. Inventory that is too low reduces Availability Level, leads to lost sales, and causes customer loss. Our goal is not minimum inventory, but the right balance between service and turnover speed. THE MANAGER'S KEY QUESTION “How much additional business can we support with the same capital if we accelerate inventory turnover?” This question shifts the discussion from stock balances to real opportunities for business growth. KEY TAKEAWAY FROM THIS STAGE The goal is not minimum inventory. The goal is high service at a turnover speed that releases capital for the next stage of growth. MORE SALES. NEW OPPORTUNITIES. NEXT → ROOT-CAUSE AUDIT"],["content/c12.html#pdf-032",30,"MANAGEMENT TOOL","MANAGEMENT TOOL Why is the product unavailable — and why is there too much of it? Zero inventory and excess inventory are opposite symptoms. But they may have the same source: the process by which the company makes inventory decisions. ZERO INVENTORY ↘ Lost sales × Lost customer ONE DECISION-MAKING PROCESS Rules · Parameters · Data · Accountability EXCESS INVENTORY € Frozen capital ! Write-off risk SYMPTOM ≠ CAUSE Different consequences. Potentially the same cause. WRONG QUESTION — Who is to blame? This question leads to defensiveness, not a solution. RIGHT QUESTION — Which process will create this deviation again? This question leads to facts and a sustainable solution. You can eliminate a specific shortage or reduce a specific excess. But if the rule, parameter, or process does not change, the problem will return — with the same SKU or another one. Looking for someone to blame degrades the data. When an audit is perceived as an investigation against a person, people begin defending decisions. When the process is investigated, facts emerge. An audit begins not with “who made the mistake?” but with “what in the system allowed this mistake to recur?” NEXT → HOW TO MOVE FROM A DEVIATION TO THE ROOT CAUSE"],["content/c12.html#pdf-033",31,"How do you move from a deviation to the root cause?","PRACTICAL TOOL How do you move from a deviation to the root cause? Do not stop at the first answer. Go deeper until you find the process, rule, or parameter that created the problem. STEP-BY-STEP APPROACH DEVIATION Record a specific fact. Example: Zero inventory for an SKU in the pharmacy. FIRST ANSWER Obtain an explanation. The order was not placed in time. NEW FACT Verify it and ask “why?” Why was the order not placed in time? Demand was high. ANOTHER “WHY?” Look for the next level of cause. Why was high demand not taken into account? The reorder point was not configured. ROOT CAUSE Identify the process / rule / parameter. Replenishment parameters are incorrectly configured for this category in pharmacies of this type. WHERE THE INVESTIGATION MAY LEAD DATA forecast inaccuracy data delay master-data errors PARAMETERS incorrect thresholds outdated rules obsolete settings PROCESSES approval delays manual operations lack of execution control ORGANIZATION unclear roles insufficient authority no accountability for the result The same symptom can have several causes. Verify the facts. Do not draw conclusions without data. Look for a systemic cause, not an isolated case. KEY IDEA Reaching the root cause means finding what will create this deviation again if it is not changed. FACTS INSTEAD OF ASSUMPTIONS. SYSTEM INSTEAD OF ISOLATED CASES. NEXT WHO SHOULD CONDUCT THE AUDIT"],["content/c12.html#pdf-034",32,"Who should conduct the audit — and what authority do they need?","PRACTICAL TOOL Who should conduct the audit — and what authority do they need? An audit crosses organizational units. Knowledge of the company alone is not enough. An independent perspective alone is not enough either. Method, access, and a real mandate are required. INTERNAL TEAM Strong in: context data decision history process knowledge Risk: habit established relationships “we have always done it this way” INDEPENDENT PERSPECTIVE fresh perspective experience with similar cases capability to challenge established practice persistent “why?” Constraint: without an internal mandate, recommendations may remain just recommendations. INDEPENDENT METHOD + EXECUTIVE WITH A REAL MANDATE WORKING AUDIT MODEL One helps reveal what the company has become accustomed to. The other ensures access, answers, and change. FIVE RIGHTS OF THE AUDIT LEAD DATA Obtain the necessary data without separate approval for every request. ACCESS Engage any organizational unit involved in the causal chain. QUESTIONS Return with another “why?” if the first answer is not supported by facts. DEADLINE Obtain a specific answer and action deadline, not “we’ll look into it.” ESCALATION Escalate a systemic cause to an executive who can change the process. Many people may participate in the investigation. There must be one person accountable for the change. Without a mandate, an audit collects observations. With a mandate, it changes the system. NEXT THREE DAYS TO SEE THE CAUSE MAP"],["content/c12.html#pdf-036",33,"ROOT CAUSE AUDIT","ROOT CAUSE AUDIT THREE DAYS TO SEE THE FULL PICTURE Why should you not immediately quantify every cause in monetary terms? Until the cause map is visible, the team risks arguing about methodology and data accuracy instead of understanding the system of problems. FIRST — THE CAUSE MAP. PRECISE CALCULATIONS ARE THE SECOND STAGE. DAY 1 CASES zero inventory excess inventory abnormal charts manual intervention DAY 2 CAUSE MAP recurring causes system points processes / rules / parameters DAY 3 DECISIONS priorities one accountable owner first action deadline result verification IN MY EXPERIENCE — PROVIDED THERE IS ACCESS TO THE SYSTEM AND KEY EMPLOYEES In most companies, an initial map can be built in two to three working days. Validation continues on new SKUs until most new cases begin to fall into the categories already identified. In three days, a company does not eliminate every cause. In three days, it understands exactly what must be eliminated. The result of the audit is not a long report. It is a cause map and an agreed program of changes. Day 3 turns the analysis into an agenda: cause · change · one accountable owner · action · deadline · verification."],["content/c12.html#pdf-037",34,"CONTINUOUS IMPROVEMENT MECHANISM","CONTINUOUS IMPROVEMENT MECHANISM WHY IS ONE AUDIT NOT ENOUGH? What happens after the dominant causes are eliminated? The company moves to a different level of operating maturity — and precisely for that reason begins to see causes that were previously hidden by larger problems. After the agreed changes are implemented, the system does not become “error-free.” The dominant causes simply stop obscuring the others. Part of the next level existed before but was hidden; part emerges after previous constraints are removed and the process begins to operate differently. NOT A ONE-TIME CHECK, BUT A CYCLE AUDIT CAUSE MAP ELIMINATION OF DOMINANT CAUSES NEW OPERATING LEVEL THE NEXT CAUSES BECOME VISIBLE NEXT AUDIT It is like climbing: from a new height, the next summit becomes visible. THE CALENDAR IS SECONDARY It is useful to review the full map at least once a year. But if the main changes have been implemented and the result stabilizes earlier, there is no need to wait for the calendar date — the next audit can be conducted immediately. Eliminating root causes does not complete improvement. It moves the company to a higher level and makes the next layer of causes visible."],["content/c13.html#pdf-038",35,"ONE POLICY. ONE HUNDRED LOCAL DECISIONS.","FROM STRATEGY TO A REPEATABLE DECISION ONE POLICY. ONE HUNDRED LOCAL DECISIONS. Why does the right direction for the company not always produce the same result locally? Management sets a clear direction, for example: “high availability with fewer days of inventory.” At the strategy level, everything looks logical. But locally, it turns into dozens of different interpretations. THE CENTER SETS THE POLICY “High availability with fewer days of inventory” DIFFERENT INTERPRETATIONS AT LOCAL LEVEL Better to play it safe; let's order more. Excess inventory Let's reduce the order to lower inventory. Risk of shortage Let's buy for the discount — it's a good deal. Unplanned purchasing We've always done it this way. Working “the way we're used to” THE RESULT: DIFFERENT OUTCOMES Different Service Levels Capital tied up in inventory Lost sales Difficult to manage High variation in performance ONE POLICY — TWO REALITIES WITHOUT A MECHANISM 1 policy 100 interpretations 100 outcomes The same policy is executed differently, and the company gets different business results. WITH A MECHANISM Common rules and parameters Repeatable decision The policy is converted into specific rules, and every unit makes the same decision under the same conditions. KEY TAKEAWAY A company direction does not yet constitute a system. For a strategy to produce the same result, it needs an execution mechanism — common rules, parameters, and process. Scale does not begin when a company opens its hundredth pharmacy. Scale begins when the right decision can be repeated the same way one hundred times."],["content/c13.html#pdf-040",36,"STANDARD DECISION — TO THE SYSTEM. EXCEPTION — TO THE PERSON.","FROM POLICY TO EXECUTION STANDARD DECISION — TO THE SYSTEM. EXCEPTION — TO THE PERSON. Where should human attention go? A company makes thousands of recurring decisions. Most can and should be formalized. Human attention is a valuable and limited resource, and it should be directed to exceptions rather than routine tasks. STANDARD — TO THE SYSTEM RECURRING DECISIONS Clear rules. Clear parameters. High frequency. Standard assortment replenishment Routine demand forecasting Regular supplier orders Application of product rules and parameters Daily recurring decisions THE SYSTEM EXECUTES CONSISTENTLY AND ACCORDING TO THE RULES. EXCEPTION — TO THE PERSON RARE AND COMPLEX DECISIONS Experience required. Context. Flexibility. New product (no demand history) Major promotions and local campaigns Supplier problems and supply disruptions Unusual spikes or drops in demand Local events (seasonality, epidemics, etc.) Conflicting data and nonstandard situations PEOPLE APPLY EXPERIENCE AND CONTEXT WHERE THERE IS NO UNAMBIGUOUS RULE. MORE TIME FOR WHAT REALLY MATTERS WHY DOES THIS MATTER FOR THE BUSINESS? LOWER COSTS People's expertise is used where it is truly needed. HIGHER DECISION QUALITY Fewer errors in standard cases, faster response to deviations. GREATER SPEED The system processes thousands of decisions simultaneously. HIGHER TEAM ENGAGEMENT People work on interesting tasks instead of checking the same thing every day. IMPORTANT If an employee can routinely override the system-generated order without justification, manual management has merely acquired a recommendation screen. A rule works only when the system is applied consistently. KEY TAKEAWAY A company should not spend expensive expertise on decisions it already knows how to formalize."],["content/c13.html#pdf-041",37,"WHO MAKES THE STANDARD DECISION IN YOUR COMPANY TODAY?","FROM EXECUTION TO MANAGING THE SYSTEM WHO MAKES THE STANDARD DECISION IN YOUR COMPANY TODAY? Five levels of maturity: from manual management to rule management. As the company matures, it stops managing every order and starts managing the decision-making mechanism — rules, parameters, permissions, and exceptions. 1 THE PERSON DECIDES EVERYTHING Each employee makes decisions independently. High variability in outcomes. Strong dependence on individual experience and personality. Low repeatability 2 THE SYSTEM RECOMMENDS The system proposes a decision; the person accepts or changes it. Results depend on how recommendations are used. First steps toward consistency. Partial repeatability 3 THE SYSTEM CREATES THE DECISION; THE PERSON ADJUSTS IT The system creates a draft order. A person regularly reviews and makes adjustments. Quality improves, but the outcome depends on review discipline. Increasing stability 4 THE SYSTEM HANDLES THE STANDARD DECISION; THE PERSON HANDLES EXCEPTIONS The system automatically creates and sends orders according to standard rules. A person works only with exceptions: new products, promotions, unusual demand, supply problems. Expertise is used where it is truly needed. High stability 5 THE COMPANY MANAGES RULES AND PERMISSIONS Management controls rules, parameters, permissions, and exceptions. Changes are rolled out quickly across the entire chain. Transparency, manageability, and scalability. Organizational capability More manual work and variability More automation, control, and scalability IMPORTANT Automating the wrong rule scales the error faster than a person can. First make sure the rule reflects reality — as we discussed in the root-cause audit section. KEY TAKEAWAY A mature company manages not every decision, but the decision-making mechanism. This is what makes it possible to execute the right decision consistently across the chain and scale the business without losing quality. As maturity increases, the person does not disappear. Their attention shifts from recurring decisions to exceptions, strategic tasks, and improving the rules."],["content/c13.html#pdf-042",38,"Automating the order is not enough","FORECAST-CENTRIC APPROACH Automating the order is not enough On the previous page, we answered the question of who makes the standard decision: a person or the system. But there is another question: what logic does the system use to decide how much product should be in the pharmacy? Automation amplifies the logic we build into it. FORECAST-CENTRIC LOGIC SALES HISTORY DEMAND FORECAST DAYS OF COVER + SAFETY STOCK ORDER First we estimate future consumption. Then we convert the forecast into a calculation of the required inventory and order. Forecasting can be improved by accounting for seasonality, trends, promotions, day of the week, and other factors. The more accurate the forecast, the better the decision within this model. sharp acceleration or deceleration in demand is reflected in the calculation after new data changes the estimate of future demand; the higher the required service level, the more the system has to protect against forecast error. But the principle does not change: first estimate future demand, then convert that estimate into inventory. A more accurate forecast improves the result — but within the same overall paradigm it does not necessarily create a meaningful advantage over the strongest competitors."],["content/c13.html#pdf-043",39,"Management by inventory state","A DIFFERENT INVENTORY-MANAGEMENT ARCHITECTURE Management by inventory state The forecast-based approach starts with the question: how much product do we expect to sell? Management by inventory state starts with a different question: is current inventory adequate relative to the system's target state? The inventory state is what the system manages. The target level is adjusted in response to that state. LOGIC OF ADEQUATE INVENTORY ACTUAL INVENTORY ADEQUACY ASSESSMENT TARGET INVENTORY ORDER First we assess the current inventory state. Then we change the target level and the order depending on the deviation from the required state. HIGH INVENTORY TARGET INVENTORY ↓ reduce / do not replenish ADEQUATE TARGET INVENTORY → UNCHANGED keep within the normal operating zone LOW INVENTORY TARGET INVENTORY ↑ accelerate replenishment In practice, forecasting and state-based management can be combined; the difference lies in the primary control signal. IMPLEMENTATION IN HORIZON · DATA FROM SPECIFIC PROJECTS This principle is implemented in Horizon by Gutsaga Technology. In specific projects, Availability Level rose above 99%, and in some cases to approximately 99.5%, while days of inventory remained under continuous control. These are results from specific implementations, not a universal guarantee. Competitive advantage is sometimes created not only by forecast accuracy, but by a different feedback architecture."],["content/c13.html#pdf-044",40,"Automation does not eliminate management thinking","CENTRAL POLICY Automation does not eliminate management thinking There is a dangerous illusion: if a machine places the order, people no longer need to think. In practice, mature automation does the opposite. Automation should remove recurring decisions, not management thinking. SYSTEM standard order thousands of recurring decisions common rules policy execution across the chain The point of automation is not to remove people from the process. The system should take over what can be repeated consistently thousands of times. The standard order is the system's task. PERSON works with the patient notices a real exception explains the cause reports a local event changes a parameter, process, or policy Human value begins where a standard answer is no longer enough: someone must identify the exception, understand the cause, and change the rule. When the same cause keeps creating exceptions, it becomes a management task. True automation transfers the company's rules into the system. Management can change a policy once — and see it executed in every pharmacy where those rules should apply. The machine executes what is repeatable. People manage causes, parameters, and exceptions. CONTROL SHIFTS FROM INDIVIDUAL PHARMACIES TO THE CHAIN-WIDE MANAGEMENT SYSTEM"],["content/c13.html#pdf-045",41,"Why is automatic ordering sometimes perceived as a threat?","RESISTANCE TO CHANGE Why is automatic ordering sometimes perceived as a threat? Manual ordering is not just work. It is also the authority to decide which product enters the pharmacy and in what quantity. Automation changes that authority. Resistance does not prove that the algorithm is poor. WHAT CHANGES right to make a local decision familiar autonomy visibility of manual changes common rules instead of personal practice WHAT MAY INFLUENCE BEHAVIOR supplier representatives promotion programs established relationships local arrangements personal incentives Even without financial motivation, a person may defend familiar autonomy and dislike a system that makes their decisions visible. In practice, resistance is especially useful to analyze through recurring manual interventions: who changes the decision, for what reason, and what then happens to Availability Level and days of inventory. The debate should therefore be moved from opinions to a measurable question. WHAT HAPPENED TO AVAILABILITY LEVEL AND DAYS INVENTORY?"],["content/c13.html#pdf-046",42,"350 pharmacies: when about twenty locations said the system was wrong","DATA FROM A SPECIFIC PROJECT 350 pharmacies: when about twenty locations said the system was wrong In a pharmacy chain of approximately 350 locations, pharmacy-to-warehouse orders were generated fully automatically for about nine months. Later, around twenty pharmacies began actively claiming that automatic ordering was managing them poorly. We compared the data. Availability Level in those pharmacies remained very high, as in the rest of the chain. But days of inventory differed substantially. COMPARABLE SERVICE. DIFFERENT CAPITAL. Availability Level — comparably high ABOUT 50 DAYS INVENTORY pharmacies that resisted ABOUT 35 DAYS INVENTORY rest of the chain For me, the important point is not the difference between 50 and 35 days itself. The important point is that, at a comparable Availability Level, the same chain can use noticeably different amounts of capital. That means the debate over “who knows better” can be moved from opinions to a measurable economic result. The problem was not that customer service could not be delivered through automation. These locations wanted to hold noticeably more product than their actual demand required. For the owner, the debate changed: instead of “Who is right — the system or the pharmacy?” a different question emerged. If service is comparable, why does one group require nearly 50% more days of inventory?"],["content/c13.html#pdf-047",43,"Automation means moving the decision into the system","STAGE CHECK · FOR MANAGEMENT Automation means moving the decision into the system Automatic ordering becomes a true management system when a single policy operates across the entire chain and human intervention becomes a managed exception. THE MANAGER SHOULD ASK Who makes the default decision — the system or a person? How often do employees change the calculated order, and is the reason recorded? What happens to Availability Level and days of inventory after manual changes? Which reasons for intervention recur? Are we correcting an individual order — or the process that keeps creating the same exception? If these questions cannot be answered quickly from the data, automation has not yet become a management system. It merely generates an order. A system begins where the rules, interventions, and their consequences are visible. HOW POWER SHIFTS BETWEEN PLAYERS The right to manage inventory shifts from the individual location to the shared system. The company gains the ability to scale its own logic rather than individual people. POLICY common rules for the entire chain SYSTEM standard decision by default EXCEPTION a person investigates the cause, not the routine A common decision already exists. But who controls how quickly the product physically returns to the pharmacy? AUTOMATION MANAGES THE DECISION. THE WAREHOUSE BEGINS TO MANAGE REPLENISHMENT TIME."],["content/c14.html#pdf-048",44,"Why add a warehouse to reduce inventory?","PART II · STAGE 4 Why add a warehouse to reduce inventory? Replenishment time, aggregation, and centralized supply At first glance, a company's own warehouse looks like a contradiction. A distributor can already deliver product directly to the location. Why add a building, people, handling, transportation, and another place where inventory will sit? You can add a warehouse — and reduce total inventory across the system. The answer becomes clear when we stop looking at a warehouse as merely a building. A warehouse is not needed to store more. It is needed to manage replenishment time and the system's total inventory. ADDED A WAREHOUSE. WHY DID INVENTORY DECREASE? WITHOUT CENTRALIZATION MANY LOCAL SAFETY STOCKS WITH A CENTRAL WAREHOUSE ONE COMMON REPLENISHMENT RHYTHM ANOTHER STORAGE LOCATION — OR A NEW WAY TO MANAGE FLOW?"],["content/c14.html#pdf-049",45,"When does not having a warehouse start to cost more than the warehouse itself?","STRUCTURAL THRESHOLD When does not having a warehouse start to cost more than the warehouse itself? For a small chain, direct deliveries from distributors may be economically optimal. As the chain grows, another cost increases — the cost of decentralized supply. WHEN A STRUCTURAL THRESHOLD EMERGES safety stock is duplicated across dozens of locations supplier schedules force the chain to hold additional safety stock large batches fit local demand poorly surplus at one pharmacy is difficult to turn quickly into product availability at another receiving many external deliveries consumes pharmacy time aggregate demand already supports large-volume purchasing, but the chain cannot receive and distribute the volume The comparison should not be warehouse cost versus zero, but warehouse cost versus the cost of the entire decentralized supply system. In my experience, many chains postpone this transition and remain for a long time in the range of roughly 10–20 pharmacies. This is not a universal threshold: geography, pharmacy density, distribution model, and market all differ. WHAT ACTUALLY DETERMINES THE REQUIRED INVENTORY? REPLENISHMENT TIME ORDER FREQUENCY  DELIVERY TIME (UNTIL AVAILABLE FOR SALE) Replenishment time runs from the moment a sale creates the need to restore inventory until the replenishment is available for sale again. In practice, this measure is often underestimated. Reports usually show delivery lead time, but inventory depends on the entire cycle: when the need arose, when the order was created, when it was picked, delivered, received, and when the product was back on the shelf. A few extra hours at each stage turn into additional inventory across the entire chain."],["content/c14.html#pdf-050",46,"The longer the replenishment cycle, the more inventory has to be financed","TIME AND THE LONG TAIL The longer the replenishment cycle, the more inventory has to be financed Inventory does not protect the calendar. It protects the time until the next reliable replenishment. SIMPLE MATH 1 DAY 3 units 2 DAYS 6 30 DAYS 90 With stable demand, three units per day is the minimum without additional safety stock against demand and supply variability. THE LONG TAIL OF THE PHARMACY ASSORTMENT A sale once every two or three weeks is not unusual in a pharmacy assortment. For a large share of items, this is a normal frequency. AUTHOR'S PRACTICAL ESTIMATE Based on my practical estimate, about 20% of product items have at least one sale in a typical week; about 80% sell less frequently. The ratio depends on format and market. This refers to the share of SKUs, not the share of revenue or the number of units sold. If an external supplier arrives once a week, a location may hold a second unit so it does not run out after the first sale. If its own warehouse replaces the sold unit the next morning, this duplicate local safety stock is often no longer necessary. FOR ONE SKU, THE DIFFERENCE IS SMALL. ACROSS THOUSANDS OF SKUS AND HUNDREDS OF PHARMACIES, IT BECOMES WORKING CAPITAL."],["content/c14.html#pdf-051",47,"What changes if the product returns to the shelf the next morning?","OPERATING CYCLE What changes if the product returns to the shelf the next morning? One possible cycle: in the evening the system receives current inventory and sales data; overnight it calculates requirements; early in the morning the warehouse picks the orders; in the morning or before noon the product is available in the pharmacy again. The difference versus an external supplier is sometimes not weeks, but a few hours or one ordering cycle. Yet that cycle can determine how much additional inventory must be held locally. The chain's own warehouse allows it to design the replenishment cycle around pharmacy needs rather than the schedules of different external suppliers. HOW MANY HOURS FROM A REPLENISHMENT DECISION TO PRODUCT BACK ON THE SHELF? EVENING Sales and inventory NIGHT Requirements calculation EARLY MORNING Warehouse picking MORNING / BEFORE NOON Product back on the shelf THE WAREHOUSE BEGINS TO MANAGE TIME."],["content/c14.html#pdf-052",48,"Why do one hundred pharmacies require less total safety stock than one hundred independent inventories?","AGGREGATION Why do one hundred pharmacies require less total safety stock than one hundred independent inventories? At an individual pharmacy, demand for a specific product may be uneven: zero today, one or two units tomorrow, then nothing for several days. If every location independently protects itself against this uncertainty, safety stock is duplicated across the chain. A central warehouse sees the aggregate demand of many locations. The effect is especially strong when local fluctuations are at least partly asynchronous. If all locations experience the same spike or shortage at the same time, risk pooling helps less. A warehouse pools not only volume. It also partially pools local uncertainty. AGGREGATION TEACHING MODEL These are not data from a specific client and not a promise of results for every chain. Consider a simple example. 100 pharmacies one product sells in each pharmacy about once a week without an own warehouse, replenishment arrives about once a week each location holds two units so it does not run out after one sale BEFORE THE CENTRAL WAREHOUSE 2 UNITS of local safety stock × 100 PHARMACIES = 200 UNITS in the system The same safety unit is held one hundred times."],["content/c14.html#pdf-053",49,"WITHOUT CENTRALIZATION","WITHOUT CENTRALIZATION Each pharmacy holds its own local safety stock. 100 LOCAL SAFETY STOCKS Pharmacy 1 2 units Pharmacy 2 Pharmacy 3 Pharmacy 4 Pharmacy 5 Pharmacy 6 Pharmacy 7 Pharmacy 8 Pharmacy 9 Pharmacy 10 … (100 pharmacies in total) 100 pharmacies × 2 units 200 UNITS IN THE SYSTEM WITH A CENTRAL WAREHOUSE Part of the safety stock is consolidated in one location. CENTRAL WAREHOUSE · 29–43 units 1 unit 100 pharmacies × 1 unit + 29–43 units in the warehouse 129–143 Teaching model. The result depends on supply, replenishment time, demand, pack size, and the required Availability Level."],["content/c14.html#pdf-055",50,"When does the mechanism work in a real chain?","MODEL AND PRACTICE When does the mechanism work in a real chain? WHAT MATTERS IN THE MODEL the warehouse replenishes pharmacies daily or the next morning the central warehouse itself is replenished sufficiently frequently and reliably the effect depends on delivery frequency, demand, correlation, pack size, and the required Availability Level What matters is not the percentage but the mechanism: duplicated safety stock across individual pharmacies is replaced by a much smaller pooled safety stock higher in the supply chain. PRACTICE · “GODOVALOV” In one project, we redesigned the supply architecture: instead of independent large deliveries to regional warehouses, product flowed to a central warehouse and from there to the regions in small, frequent batches. 30% TOTAL WAREHOUSE INVENTORY As I recall, total warehouse inventory fell by at least approximately 30%. The reduction was especially noticeable at regional warehouses. At the same time, space requirements decreased, receiving became more even, and the need for warehouse labor declined. This is a historical observation from a specific project, not a universal benchmark. If the central warehouse itself is replenished infrequently or unreliably, it will require more inventory and the effect will be different. For me, the important point in this case is not the 30% result itself. The important point is the mechanism: inventory stopped being duplicated across dozens of locations, while a fast replenishment rhythm made it possible to hold less safety stock locally. If the delivery rhythm becomes infrequent or unstable, this effect quickly diminishes."],["content/c14.html#pdf-056",51,"Not everything has to go through your own warehouse","WHICH PART OF THE FLOW SHOULD BE BROUGHT UNDER OUR OWN CONTROL? Not everything has to go through your own warehouse A similar effect was later reported by another client, Farmakopeika / Medexport: after moving to a centralized architecture, inventory fell by more than 30%, according to the client, while the warehouse's operating requirements decreased. Two projects do not establish a universal percentage, but they do confirm the mechanism itself. Aggregation and more frequent replenishment can reduce system-wide inventory even when another warehouse is added to the chain. ALL THROUGH OUR OWN WAREHOUSE? DIRECT DELIVERY OR THROUGH THE WAREHOUSE Which parts of the flow create greater overall economic value when controlled by the chain itself? THE WAREHOUSE GIVES PHARMACISTS BACK THEIR TIME EXTERNAL DELIVERY full receiving process for an external supplier OWN WAREHOUSE exception control with reliable scanning, picking, and shipping Where processes and regulations allow, repeated checking can be substantially reduced. This frees up pharmacists' time. A GOOD WAREHOUSE DOES MORE THAN REDUCE INVENTORY. IT GIVES PHARMACISTS BACK THEIR TIME."],["content/c14.html#pdf-057",52,"The market has already run out. The chain still has time.","SHORTAGE The market has already run out. The chain still has time. There is another function that becomes especially visible during a shortage. External distributors can no longer supply the product. A pharmacy that depends solely on external replenishment cannot restore availability once its local inventory is depleted. But the chain may still have inventory at its central warehouse. The product has already disappeared from the market. It is still available within the chain. CENTRAL INVENTORY BUYS THE CHAIN ADDITIONAL TIME EXTERNAL MARKET no more supply CENTRAL WAREHOUSE inventory still remains CHAIN still selling WHEN SUPPLY IS CONSTRAINED the chain can manage distribution priorities within its own rules and obligations This is not unlimited protection. But it is additional time during which the chain can continue serving customers after other market participants have already run out of the product. If the same warehouse also operates as a wholesale supplier to external pharmacies, the chain's own pharmacies may become a distribution priority during a shortage, subject to contractual obligations and the rules of the specific market. THE WAREHOUSE BECOMES A MECHANISM FOR ALLOCATING A CONSTRAINED RESOURCE."],["content/c14.html#pdf-058",53,"What else does a central warehouse provide?","SYSTEM-WIDE ECONOMICS What else does a central warehouse provide? Additional product handling costs money. But the relevant comparison is not the cost of a single warehouse operation; it is the economics of the entire system. 1 · SYSTEM-WIDE ECONOMICS inventory + service + capital + labor + delivery When I assess a central warehouse, I am not interested in the price of one warehouse operation. What matters is what happens to the entire system: inventory, service, capital, labor, and delivery frequency. The key is not to minimize one local operation at the expense of the entire system. 2 · BREAKING DOWN LARGE LOTS manufacturer case pack -> quantities needed by each pharmacy A large factory pack may be cheap at the point of purchase but expensive for the chain if the excess product then sits in dozens of pharmacies. A warehouse allows the chain to buy in bulk while distributing exactly as much as each location needs. Local picking savings can tie up more working capital across the entire chain. 3 · PURCHASING POWER 100 small orders -> one consolidated purchasing volume When one hundred small orders are consolidated into a single purchasing volume, more than logistics changes. The supplier sees the chain's aggregate demand, and the chain negotiates as a single large buyer. A warehouse concentrates more than product. It concentrates purchasing power. FROM THIS POINT, THE CHAIN TAKES OVER NOT ONLY THE DISTRIBUTOR'S LOGISTICS FUNCTION, BUT ALSO PART OF ITS NEGOTIATING POSITION."],["content/c14.html#pdf-059",54,"Is the warehouse the heart of the chain?","STAGE CHECK · FOR MANAGEMENT Is the warehouse the heart of the chain? The heart does not determine strategy, but it keeps things moving. In this sense, a strong central warehouse becomes the infrastructure through which the chain manages replenishment, local inventory, distribution, assortment, and purchasing volume. Before establishing its own warehouse, the chain depends to a significant extent on the rhythm of external distribution. Afterward, it begins to build its own supply system. THE MANAGER SHOULD ASK What is the full replenishment lead time until the product is available? After centralization, did total inventory across 'warehouse + pharmacies' decrease? What share of the flow makes economic sense to control through the chain's own warehouse? Can receiving work in pharmacies be reduced? How accurately does the warehouse pick orders and break large lots down to actual demand? How much additional time does the chain gain during a market shortage? WHAT EXACTLY THE CHAIN BRINGS UNDER ITS OWN CONTROL DISTRIBUTOR part of the function used to be external CHAIN time · aggregation · distribution · purchasing volume THE CHAIN BRINGS UNDER ITS CONTROL its own supply system Part of the function that used to be external moves inside the chain. This raises the next question: If demand is already aggregated and the supplier can deliver the product to one location, why continue buying only through a distributor? The next stage is to move up the chain and buy directly from the manufacturer."],["content/c15.html#pdf-060",55,"Does buying cheaper always mean earning more?","PART II · STAGE 5 Does buying cheaper always mean earning more? Direct purchasing, private label, and parallel trade An in-house warehouse has already changed the chain's position. Demand is aggregated, volumes are visible in one place, and the chain can distribute the product onward itself. If we are already large enough, why buy only from a distributor? The manufacturer may offer a lower price. But that often comes with a larger lot, a longer replenishment lead time, less flexibility, more days of inventory, and more capital tied up in product. Buying cheaper is not the same as earning more. A lower price is only one line of the economics PURCHASE PRICE LOT becomes larger INVENTORY may increase CAPITAL stays tied up longer RISK shifts to the chain A DISCOUNT IS NOT THE SAME AS AN ECONOMIC OUTCOME Buying cheaper makes sense only when the entire function delivers a better economic outcome for the company. FROM THE AUTHOR One of the most common purchasing mistakes is to treat a discount as income before calculating the full cost of inventory. The price is visible immediately. Capital, excess inventory, and lost flexibility show up later. A good purchasing contract therefore begins not with the question 'how much did we save?' but with 'what did the company earn over the full cycle?'"],["content/c15.html#pdf-061",56,"When does a manufacturer begin to see a pharmacy chain as a direct customer?","DIRECT CONTRACT When does a manufacturer begin to see a pharmacy chain as a direct customer? A single pharmacy is rarely of interest to a manufacturer as a major buyer. Even a chain may still look like many small locations if each orders separately and requires separate delivery. HOW RETAIL DEMAND BECOMES A DIRECT CONTRACT MANY LOCAL LOCATIONS AGGREGATED DEMAND · ONE ORDER · ONE RECEIPT THE MANUFACTURER SEES ONE CUSTOMER DEMAND — from all pharmacies ONE RECEIPT — at one location VOLUME — by product TO PHARMACIES — using the chain's own resources CONTRACT — larger Aggregation turns retail demand into purchasing power. But a direct contract is attractive only when the chain can take over part of the distributor's function in an economically justified way. IMPORTANT A manufacturer sees a major customer not when the chain has many pharmacies, but when their demand becomes one manageable order."],["content/c15.html#pdf-062",57,"If the manufacturer offers a lower price than the distributor, does that mean a direct contract is more advantageous?","TWO SOURCES OF SUPPLY If the manufacturer offers a lower price than the distributor, does that mean a direct contract is more advantageous? Not necessarily. The two sources have different economics. Parameter Distributor Manufacturer Purchase price Usually higher May be lower Replenishment time Usually shorter Often longer Minimum order quantity Lower Order flexibility Higher Required inventory Usually lower Often higher Capital requirement Excess inventory risk WHAT THE CHAIN BUYS FROM A DISTRIBUTOR Not just product: a buffer, financing for part of the cycle, order splitting, mixed assortment, and small deliveries. WHAT A DIRECT CONTRACT CHANGES The price may fall, but batch size, lead time, capital, and risk more often shift inside the chain. Price shows the cost of one unit of product. Inventory shows what the chain has to carry to obtain that price. A manufacturer’s low purchase price often looks like obvious savings only because part of the cost is not yet visible in the price line. The distributor already holds inventory, splits orders, finances part of the cycle, and allows the chain to buy more frequently and in smaller volumes. With a direct contract, these functions do not disappear—they move inside the company. That is why two sources of supply should be compared not by the price per unit, but by the cost of the entire system that makes it possible to buy, store, and sell that unit on time."],["content/c15.html#pdf-063",58,"What margin is actually good?","MARGIN ON INVENTORY INVESTMENT What margin is actually good? A high margin percentage by itself says nothing about the quality of a decision. One product may deliver a moderate margin and turn quickly. Another may deliver twice the percentage but require a large lot and sit for months. You need to look not only at the margin on one sale, but also at how much capital is continuously tied up in inventory to generate that margin. MARGIN ONLY MAKES SENSE ALONGSIDE THE CAPITAL IT REQUIRES GROSS MARGIN over 12 months ÷ AVERAGE INVENTORY at purchase cost = GMROI GMROI shows how much gross margin, in monetary terms, each €1 of average inventory generates over 12 months. In this book: gross margin over 12 months / average inventory at purchase cost. A PRACTICAL BENCHMARK FOR A PHARMACY CHAIN <100% — weak return 100-200% — moderate level 200-300% — high performance >300% — strong economics These ranges are the author's practical benchmarks for annual GMROI in the pharmacy business, not universal market standards. A high GMROI should not be considered a good result if it is achieved through chronic shortages. Evaluate it together with the Availability Level."],["content/c15.html#pdf-064",59,"What does the chain actually take over from the distributor?","WHEN THE FUNCTION MOVES INSIDE THE CHAIN What does the chain actually take over from the distributor? Direct purchasing changes more than price. The chain begins to aggregate demand itself, place a large order, receive the product at its own warehouse, finance a longer cycle, break down and distribute the product to pharmacies, and maintain the required Availability Level. WHAT MOVES INSIDE THE CHAIN WITH THE FUNCTION The chain does not simply capture the distributor's margin; it takes over the functions, costs, and risks behind it. PREVIOUSLY, PART OF THE FUNCTION WAS PERFORMED BY THE DISTRIBUTOR Holds buffer inventory Receives large lots and breaks them down Consolidates manufacturers' assortments Organizes deliveries to individual pharmacies Partially absorbs demand fluctuations NOW THE CHAIN TAKES ON Holds and finances its own inventory Receives a large lot and distributes it to pharmacies Manages its purchasing assortment independently Manages its own replenishment cycle Takes on the risk of excess inventory, shortages, and availability FUNCTION ECONOMICS + RISK + RESPONSIBILITY The chain should assess the migration of a function based on its full economics and strategic value: margin, capital, service, risk, access, and control. Sometimes a function is justified not because it is cheaper to perform today than an external provider, but because it increases resilience or reduces dependency. We have taken over the function, and now we must prove that its total economic and strategic value exceeds its cost, complexity, and risk."],["content/c15.html#pdf-065",60,"What should you do if getting a good price requires buying more than your own chain needs?","PARALLEL TRADE What should you do if getting a good price requires buying more than your own chain needs? A manufacturer may offer a lower price at a higher volume. The chain has several options. HIGHER VOLUME, LOWER PRICE. WHAT SHOULD WE DO WITH THE EXCESS? 1 · DECLINE if the full economics do not work 2 · BUY AND HOLD more inventory, capital, and risk 3 · BUY AND RESELL PART only if it is legal, licensed, and there are buyers MARKET PERMITS LICENSES BUYERS TRACEABILITY CAPITAL / RISK In some European markets, cross-border circulation of original medicinal products, when conducted in accordance with established rules, may constitute parallel trade. For medicines, requirements governing distribution, traceability, packaging, and labeling are particularly important. A large purchase alone does not turn the chain into a wholesale operator. It requires a lawful resale channel, buyers, and the capability to manage additional inventory, working capital, and risk. SCALE CAN TURN PURCHASING INTO A WHOLESALE FUNCTION — BUT ONLY IF THERE IS A LAWFUL CHANNEL AND ECONOMIC RATIONALE."],["content/c15.html#pdf-066",61,"Why can private label create more economic value for the chain?","PRIVATE LABEL Why can private label create more economic value for the chain? At sufficient scale, a chain can commission production under its own private label. The manufacturer produces the product to an agreed specification. WHAT THE CHAIN CAN GAIN higher gross margin a differentiated product control over pricing architecture its own brand asset WHAT SHIFTS TO THE CHAIN choice of manufacturer quality and specification consistency volume forecasting and excess-inventory risk reputational risk attached to its own name Private label does not create profit automatically. The chain itself controls the product's place in the assortment, price positioning, volume, its own brand, and the product's route to pharmacies — and it bears the consequences of mistakes itself. The economic value of private label comes not only from higher margin. The chain gains the ability to manage the offer itself: choose the manufacturer, define the specification, build the pricing architecture, and develop its own brand. But every new element of control simultaneously becomes a new area of responsibility. Private label therefore works best when the chain can manage not an individual product, but its entire economics — from volume forecasting and the production batch to its place in the category, availability in pharmacies, and customer response. The more functions remain inside the chain, the fewer mistakes can be shifted to an external supplier. The larger the share of the value-chain economics the chain retains, the more responsibility it must be able to carry."],["content/c15.html#pdf-067",62,"How many months of demand are in one batch?","FACTORY PHYSICS How many months of demand are in one batch? Convert the production batch from units into months of demand. Chain-wide demand per month 1,000 units Minimum production batch 24,000 24,000 ÷ 1,000 = 24 ≈24 months of demand one minimum production batch equals 24 months of demand One production batch covers: 1 mo. · 2 mo. · 3 mo. · 4 mo. · 5 mo. · 6 mo. · 7 mo. · 8 mo. · 9 mo. · 10 mo. · 11 mo. · 12 mo. · 13 mo. · 14 mo. · 15 mo. · 16 mo. · 17 mo. · 18 mo. · 19 mo. · 20 mo. · 21 mo. · 22 mo. · 23 mo. · 24 mo. CAPITAL how much money is tied up in inventory SHELF LIFE will the batch sell in time DEMAND what happens if the category changes Before agreeing on the price, convert the batch into months of demand. High margin does not compensate for inventory that the chain cannot turn over in time."],["content/c15.html#pdf-068",63,"Can a high-margin SKU be a poor decision?","FACTORY PHYSICS Can a high-margin SKU be a poor decision? Yes. If the minimum production batch contains two years of inventory, a high margin on a single unit may be almost meaningless. You cannot negotiate against factory physics. But you can decline the purchase if production constraints do not fit the economics of your chain. THE PRIVATE-LABEL TRAP The problem begins when the economics of a single SKU become more important than the customer's need. At the level of one item, the economics may look better. At the level of the customer relationship, the chain may lose more. THE HIGHEST-MARGIN PRODUCT CAN STILL BE A POOR DECISION IF IT CAUSES THE BUSINESS TO LOSE THE CUSTOMER. Private label should strengthen the category and customer choice, not force the customer to conform to the chain's internal economics. THE CATEGORY MANAGER LOOKS AT demand price and margin positioning place in the category THE FACTORY LOOKS AT minimum economic batch line changeover production frequency production and quality-control lead times Batch 24,000 units ÷ 1,000 units/month = 24 months of demand PRACTICAL CONCLUSION A high margin on an individual SKU does not necessarily mean a good category decision. If private label causes the chain to increase inventory, displace a product the customer needs, or restrict choice, the economics of one item may improve while the economics of the entire category become worse. Private label should be evaluated not in isolation, but together with its impact on turnover, availability, and customer behavior."],["content/c15.html#pdf-069",64,"Moving upstream in the chain is economics, not a discount","STAGE CHECK · FOR MANAGEMENT Moving upstream in the chain is economics, not a discount Direct purchasing and private label become strategic when their total value — margin, capital, service, resilience, access, and control — justifies the risk and complexity of the function. THE MANAGER SHOULD ASK How do days of inventory change when moving to a direct source? How much capital does the minimum order quantity require? How many months of demand does one mandatory production batch cover? What gross-margin return does average inventory generate for each source? What excess-inventory and expiration risk does the chain take on? Which distributor functions can the chain genuinely perform better itself? Are we increasing private-label margin at the expense of customer choice and trust? A DIRECT SOURCE SHOULD BE COMPARED NOT BY THE DISCOUNT, BUT BY THE FULL ECONOMICS DAYS OF INVENTORY What happens to inventory turnover? LOT AND CAPITAL How much money does the mandatory volume require? MARGIN ON INVENTORY What return does average inventory generate? RISK Excess inventory, expiration risk, complexity of the function. A direct contract is not a reward for scale; it is a new operational responsibility. If the chain cannot manage inventory, capital, and risk better than under the previous model, the discount merely moves part of the economics and complexity inside the company. Direct purchasing becomes a strategy when the entire function creates a better overall outcome, both economically and strategically."],["content/c15.html#pdf-070",65,"The chain stops being merely a buyer of ready-made distribution.","HOW MARKET POWER CHANGES The chain stops being merely a buyer of ready-made distribution. It can bring the purchasing function in-house, capture part of wholesale economics, develop its own product, and take over part of the manufacturer and distributor functions. A larger discount may already be an expression of negotiating leverage. Additional lasting power from a new function appears when the chain performs it efficiently enough to justify the added cost and risk. BUYER OF READY-MADE DISTRIBUTION BRINGS THE FUNCTION IN-HOUSE GAINS NEW LEVERAGE Direct purchasing does not itself create the problem of excess inventory in pharmacies. Excess inventory arises regardless of whether the chain buys from a distributor or directly from a manufacturer. Demand changes. A promotion ends. Sales slow down. The volume purchased may have been too large. Commercial terms may have encouraged the chain to hold additional inventory. NEW CAPABILITY — SEEING INVENTORY NOT AS HUNDREDS OF SEPARATE BALANCES, BUT AS A SINGLE NETWORK-WIDE RESOURCE. An in-house warehouse and centralized supply make this function technically possible. Here, the object of management itself changes. While the chain was focused on purchasing, the main questions were price, terms, and source of supply. But once the product is already inside the system, those decisions are in the past. The outcome now depends on how quickly the chain detects changes in demand and how well it can manage existing inventory as a single resource. The next stage of the book is therefore not a consequence of direct purchasing, but another function made possible by a mature warehouse system."],["content/c15.html#pdf-071",66,"BUYING BETTER IS NOT THE SAME AS MANAGING INVENTORY ALREADY ON HAND.","TRANSITION · FROM PURCHASING TO MANAGING INVENTORY ALREADY ON HAND BUYING BETTER IS NOT THE SAME AS MANAGING INVENTORY ALREADY ON HAND. Direct purchasing does not create excess inventory by itself. Demand keeps changing, and stock at an individual pharmacy can fall out of line with local demand. The warehouse already knows how to replenish a pharmacy. The next question is what to do with stock already purchased when local demand changes. PURCHASING Price, terms, and source are only the beginning. THE PRODUCT IS ALREADY IN THE SYSTEM Sales slowed. Demand shifted. Stock is now too high for that location. NEW CAPABILITY Treat inventory as one network-wide pool and move it to where it is needed. THE PRODUCT IS ALREADY IN THE NETWORK. THE QUESTION IS: WHERE IS IT NEEDED? PHARMACY A — EXCESS STOCK NETWORK-WIDE INVENTORY POOL PHARMACY B — NEEDS STOCK Redistribution (what U.S. retailers often call inventory rebalancing) starts not with another purchase, but with a network-wide view of inventory already on hand: where there is excess and where stock is needed now. The next stage is redistribution — managing excess stock as one network-wide inventory pool."],["content/c16.html#pdf-072",67,"Redistribution, managed inventory exit (Outlet), and expiration dates","PART II · STAGE 6 Redistribution, managed inventory exit (Outlet), and expiration dates When does a product become excess? At the moment of purchase? At the moment of delivery? Or when inventory no longer matches the current demand of a specific pharmacy? The product may have been needed by the pharmacy yesterday. Today, demand is different. PROMOTION — ended. SALES — slowed. DEMAND — changed. ASSORTMENT — revised. Excess inventory is therefore not necessarily a purchasing or allocation error. It is inventory that has become too large relative to current demand and the time remaining to sell it normally. Is this product excess for the chain — or only for one pharmacy? LOCAL EXCESS DOES NOT YET MEAN EXCESS FOR THE CHAIN PHARMACY A — demand has almost stopped. PHARMACY B — demand remains. PHARMACY C — demand remains. The chain sees not three separate inventory balances, but one network-wide resource. Sales may almost stop in one pharmacy. In another, the same product may continue selling normally. When the chain is viewed as a system, an opportunity appears. If this pharmacy cannot sell the product in time, can another one?"],["content/c16.html#pdf-073",68,"HOW DOES PRODUCT MOVE BETWEEN PHARMACIES?","HOW DOES PRODUCT MOVE BETWEEN PHARMACIES? Physically, in both modes the product usually passes through the warehouse. The difference lies elsewhere: What does the warehouse do with the box? Transfer or return to inventory? One warehouse — two different roles in redistribution. MODE 1 — transit through the warehouse: the box is already intended for Pharmacy B and does not enter warehouse inventory. MODE 2 — return to warehouse inventory: the next destination is determined by actual demand. When the recipient is already known and speed matters, transit can be simpler. When the chain wants to preserve flexibility over the next decision, the product returns to warehouse inventory and its destination is determined by the next actual demand."],["content/c16.html#pdf-074",69,"WHAT DOES REDISTRIBUTION PROVIDE?","WHAT DOES REDISTRIBUTION PROVIDE? Redistribution does not fix demand. It fixes how inventory already purchased is used. A product that sells slowly at one location gets another chance where demand is higher. reduce local excess inventory; reduce expiration risk; improve availability at another pharmacy; accelerate the return of capital tied up in slow-moving inventory. Redistribution does not create new inventory. It makes inventory already purchased work where demand exists. One of the author's projects 35 → 25 days of inventory before implementation after implementation Reduction in days of inventory of approximately 29% Not a universal benchmark — an illustration of how regular redistribution improves inventory utilization. Why does this mechanism often fail to operate regularly? The principle itself is simple: identify excess at one pharmacy and move it to where demand is higher. The problem begins at scale. In a chain of dozens or hundreds of pharmacies, inventory, demand, sales velocity, and transfer constraints must be continuously matched across thousands of items. Manually, this quickly becomes a difficult and expensive operation. Automation changes the economics of the process. What looks like an occasional 'major cleanup' of inventory when done manually can be performed regularly by a system. Excess inventory then starts moving earlier — while there is still demand for it, rather than when it has already become an obvious problem. But redistribution also has a limit. If a product is already excessive not in one pharmacy but across the entire chain, there is nowhere left to move it. What if there is nowhere left to move it? This leads to the next step: what to do when the internal possibilities for redistribution have been exhausted."],["content/c16.html#pdf-075",70,"WHEN REDISTRIBUTION IS NO LONGER ENOUGH","WHEN REDISTRIBUTION IS NO LONGER ENOUGH A new product may be ready for launch, but the old one is still occupying shelf space and tying up working capital. Until the old product exits, the assortment has not actually been renewed. New product is ready to launch Old product is still in inventory Shelf space, capital, and assortment capacity are occupied New product launch is blocked Assortment management is not only about introducing new products. It also requires the ability to remove old ones quickly. TWO MODES OF INVENTORY EXIT MANAGEMENT Outlet brings selected inventory to a defined target state. The target depends on the situation. Complete discontinuation For a product that must be fully discontinued across the chain. 0 inventory 0 by a specified date by the specified deadline Goal: free up space for the new product and release working capital. Return to a normal level For a product that remains in the assortment but whose inventory is above the acceptable level. target level Goal: reduce inventory to the target quantity and keep it there. The inventory target can be set for a specific date or maintained on an ongoing basis. HOW THE OUTLET SYSTEM WORKS The system tracks inventory and applies the right tools until the target is reached. Analysis of current inventory and deviation from target Selection of tools (redistribution, pricing, promotion, etc.) Monitoring the inventory trajectory Adjusting the intensity of actions Reaching the target: 0 or the target quantity An assortment is not renewed when the company decides to introduce a new product. It is renewed when the system can free the space needed for it. WHY THIS SHOULD WORK AUTOMATICALLY In a large pharmacy chain, managers cannot continuously track thousands of products that need to be discontinued or returned to normal stock levels. By the time the problem is noticed and a one-off action is launched, old inventory is still occupying space and delaying the new product. Automation turns inventory-exit management into an always-on operating capability rather than an occasional manual campaign."],["content/c16.html#pdf-076",71,"How much management time is a company prepared to spend on a product it has already decided to discontinue?","FIELD CASE · LAMEL, YEKATERINBURG How much management time is a company prepared to spend on a product it has already decided to discontinue? Around 2017, the owner of the Lamel cosmetics chain in Yekaterinburg asked me to visit. 120 stores 12 category managers 20–40% of their time As I recall, the chain had about 120 stores and approximately 12 category managers. The owner said he had been trying to solve one problem for two or three years—with consultants and his own IT team. Category managers were spending too much time manually clearing the remaining stock of discontinued products. MANUAL CYCLE AGAIN AND AGAIN review inventory; move stock between stores; decide what discount to offer; check what remains; adjust the decision again. Manual work is not a single decision but a repeating cycle of observation, movement, markdown, and rechecking. According to the owner's estimate and my recollection, this work consumed roughly 20–40% of category managers' time. The problem was not only the money recovered from the sell-through. The main question was how much management time the company was spending on a product it had already decided to discontinue. He wanted category managers to return to managing categories. Not toward manually selling through old product, but toward developing the assortment."],["content/c16.html#pdf-077",72,"FIELD CASE · LAMEL · OPERATING MODEL","FIELD CASE · LAMEL · YEKATERINBURG FIELD CASE · LAMEL · OPERATING MODEL The figures on this page relate only to this case: they are the author's recollection and the owner's estimate, not an industry benchmark. FROM A MANUAL CYCLE TO MANAGING THE TARGET INVENTORY STATE In the Lamel case, management attention returns to the category, while the routine inventory-exit cycle moves to the system. BEFORE The manager personally runs the inventory-exit cycle 1. REVIEW INVENTORY Where is the excess? 2. MOVE PRODUCT Where can it go? 3. DECIDE WHAT DISCOUNT TO OFFER How can the sale be accelerated? 4. CHECK WHAT REMAINS Did it work? 20–40% of category managers' time The cycle repeats again and again for different products. AFTER The manager sets the mode; the system moves inventory toward the target state 1. DISCONTINUE THE PRODUCT For a product that must leave completely and free up space for a new one. Goal: 0 inventory Deadline: when necessary, by a specific date 2. RETURN INVENTORY TO NORMAL For a product that remains in the assortment but whose inventory is above the acceptable level. Goal: target quantity Mode: reach it by a deadline or maintain it regularly THE SYSTEM MOVES INVENTORY TOWARD THE DEFINED STATE selects and applies tools, monitors the trajectory, and adjusts actions. THE DECISION REMAINS WITH THE CATEGORY TEAM · THE ROUTINE MOVES TO THE SYSTEM"],["content/c16.html#pdf-078",73,"WHAT EXACTLY SHOULD REMAIN WITH THE PERSON?","WHAT EXACTLY SHOULD REMAIN WITH THE PERSON? After automation, the person does not disappear from the process. They stop running the cycle and start managing the outcome. THE PERSON DEFINES Strategic product decision. 1 What should be done with the product? • discontinue it; • or return inventory to normal. 2 What is the target state? • 0 inventory; • or a target quantity. 3 Is there a deadline? • no hard date; • or by a specific date. THE SYSTEM RUNS THE OPERATIONAL WORK Executes the logic until the target is reached. 1 Tracks deviation from the target. 2 Redistributes product. 3 Applies the prescribed pricing actions. 4 Monitors inventory and the pace of inventory exit. 5 Adjusts the intensity of actions. 6 Returns only exceptions to the person, not every case. IN THIS CASE 10–15 MINUTES A DAY The person sets the target. The system manages the trajectory. Instead of a constant manual cycle. HOW THE HUMAN ROLE CHANGED BEFORE A person inside every decision Continuously review inventory, move product, change the price, and check the result. AFTER The person sets the rule and handles exceptions Defines the target, controls exceptions, and focuses on developing the category. Automation frees the person not from responsibility, but from repetitive work. The decision about what to discontinue remains with the category team. The work of bringing inventory to the target moves to the system. It was in this project that the principle finally crystallized for me: Outlet is not a discount campaign; it is management of the target, the deadline, and the inventory trajectory."],["content/c16.html#pdf-079",74,"EXPIRATION DATE: WHEN TIME BECOMES A CONSTRAINT","EXPIRATION DATE: WHEN TIME BECOMES A CONSTRAINT The expiration-date problem does not begin on the day the product expires. It begins earlier—when the time remaining becomes shorter than the time required to sell the current inventory normally. Outlet already manages toward a target state and, when needed, a deadline. The expiration date adds a hard external boundary that the company cannot move. If the product already has a fixed end date, how much time does the company have left to redistribute, reprice, or sell it? THE LESS TIME REMAINS, THE FEWER THE OPTIONS An expiration date turns time into a hard constraint. Enough time Limited time Critically little time Normal sale There is time to sell at the regular price. Redistribution Takes time to select locations, ship, and receive stock. Pricing actions and promotions Takes time to prepare and launch the pricing action. Return to warehouse inventory Also takes time for logistics and processing. THE RISK OF WRITE-OFF INCREASES EVERY DAY. Enough time remains Remaining time decreases Critical date (expiration date) WHAT HAPPENS WHEN TIME IS SHORT? The effectiveness of each tool declines. The number of options decreases, while the cost of using them increases. The probability of write-off increases. Even with active measures, there may not be enough time to reach the target state. Starting early becomes more important. The earlier the system sees the risk, the more options it has and the higher the probability of success. As the expiration date approaches, manual intervention becomes too slow. To act in time, the system must identify the risk early and automatically trigger the required actions."],["content/c16.html#pdf-080",75,"STAGE CHECK · FOR MANAGEMENT","STAGE CHECK · FOR MANAGEMENT Managing excess inventory is not about clearing stock. It is about how quickly the pharmacy chain responds when demand changes. Do we distinguish local excess from network-wide excess? Can the system see where the same product still has real demand? When do we move stock through the warehouse in transit, and when do we return it to warehouse inventory? How long does it take to move product after excess is identified? Where does redistribution end and Outlet begin? Does every Outlet product have a target and, when necessary, a deadline? How much time do category managers spend on repetitive operational work? How far ahead of the critical date does the system start acting? If these questions cannot be answered quickly with numbers and rules, excess inventory is not being managed systematically—it is still being handled manually, case by case. These questions show whether excess inventory is managed through a real system. As long as the answers still depend on who notices the problem and whether that person has time to address it, the process remains manual and inconsistent. A mature model turns recurring decisions into rules: the system detects the deviation, selects an approved action, and routes only exceptions to a person. The sooner a recurring decision becomes a rule, the less routine operational work remains for people."],["content/c16.html#pdf-081",76,"EXCESS CAN BE MANAGED QUICKLY. BUT SOME PROBLEMS BEGIN BEFORE THE ORDER.","TRANSITION · FROM CONSEQUENCE TO THE ASSORTMENT DECISION EXCESS CAN BE MANAGED QUICKLY. BUT SOME PROBLEMS BEGIN BEFORE THE ORDER. Inventory balancing has almost no direct effect on the company's purchasing power. It improves control over already-purchased inventory, capital, and response time. If demand changes, the network can respond faster with inventory it has already purchased. Capital and management attention become less dependent on a local change in demand at one location. AFTER PURCHASE Where should the excess already created be moved, and how quickly should the network respond? BUT EARLIER BEFORE THE ORDER. BEFORE THE WAREHOUSE. Which products should be in the assortment at all, what customer need does each one address, and how should they work together? If part of the problem begins before the order and the warehouse, is managing only its consequences enough? No. The next capability is to design the category, not individual stock positions. Do you have a set of products—or do you have a category?"],["content/c17.html#pdf-082",77,"Do you have several products—or do you have a category?","Do you have several products—or do you have a category? PART II · STAGE 7 A category begins not with the supplier, but with the reason the customer will return. In practice, assortment often grows in the opposite direction: a supplier offers attractive terms, a new SKU is added to the assortment, and the shelf becomes broader. Each decision looks reasonable on its own. But that does not yet mean the customer has gained a reason to return specifically to this chain. HOW THE SHELF IS OFTEN FORMED SUPPLIER attractive terms SKU new item SHELF became broader CUSTOMER why come back? A commercially justified shelf does not necessarily create a category in the customer's mind. The product reached the shelf. But did the customer gain a reason to come back here? SHELF AS A SET OF SKUs · suppliers · inventory · sales CATEGORY IN THE CUSTOMER'S MIND “I know where to go for choice” brand · price · option · trust A category does not exist only in the accounting system. It exists when the customer understands what need can be met here and why this is the place worth returning to for choice. A supplier can offer a product. But only the chain decides what the category should mean to the customer."],["content/c17.html#pdf-083",78,"REVERSING THE LOGIC","REVERSING THE LOGIC Start with the customer, not the supplier. Customers do not shop by your internal product classification. They come to solve a specific need: reduce a fever, choose vitamins, select personal care, or find the right brand, dosage form, or price point. THE RIGHT SEQUENCE NEED CHOICE OPTIONS SKU SUPPLIER From customer need to the right range of options, then to SKUs, and only then to the supplier. THREE QUESTIONS BEFORE NEGOTIATING WITH A SUPPLIER What should the customer be able to find in the category? What range of options do they actually need? Which SKUs best provide that choice? Only then should the discussion move to the supplier, price, and commercial terms. These help determine the best way to execute the category design; they should not define the category for the pharmacy chain. Assortment strategy starts with customer need. Purchasing follows. But how much choice is enough? There is no single right number across a pharmacy chain. Assortment depth should reflect demand volume and mix at each location."],["content/c17.html#pdf-084",79,"ASSORTMENT DEPTH","ASSORTMENT DEPTH How many vitamin D options should a pharmacy carry? In a large urban pharmacy, demand may justify broad choice. At a small location, the same depth can tie up capital without a noticeable increase in sales. The opposite extreme is also dangerous—too few options where demand can support deeper choice. How much choice is enough? One need—three different assortment depths. TOO NARROW — some meaningful demand goes unmet. ENOUGH — the main options are covered without unnecessary duplication. TOO DEEP — SKUs multiply faster than demand or incremental value. Assortment depth follows demand at the specific location. The next SKU should bring new value, not just new inventory. The right assortment is neither the maximum nor the minimum. It is the point beyond which the next SKU adds little incremental demand or customer value."],["content/c17.html#pdf-085",80,"SKU ROLES IN THE CATEGORY","SKU ROLES IN THE CATEGORY Not every SKU should do the same job Within a category, products can perform different roles. On a very fast-moving, well-known product, the chain may earn almost nothing. But if customers know its price well, that product can materially influence price perception of the entire pharmacy. Alongside it may be products with higher margins, including private-label products. Not every product has to generate margin. Some products have to win the customer. The mistake is to demand the same financial behavior from every SKU and fail to see its role in category economics. CATEGORY CHECK Every SKU should have a clear role—traffic, margin, or completeness of choice. If the role is unclear, the SKU requires review."],["content/c17.html#pdf-086",81,"DEMAND TRANSFER","DEMAND TRANSFER What happens to demand when an SKU appears or disappears? A new SKU may sell well and still add almost nothing to category sales. When a product is removed, the reverse question is just as important: will demand transfer to another SKU, or leave with the customer? Where does demand move? SKU sales alone are not the answer. The direction of the flow matters. ADDING A SKU What incremental demand did the category gain? Existing SKUs → NEW: transfer from existing SKUs. New demand from outside the category → NEW. REMOVING AN SKU What will the customer do when the product is gone? Removed SKU → another SKU: demand transfers within the category. Removed SKU → outside the chain: demand leaves the chain. Assortment optimization means reducing inventory without losing demand. Therefore, the introduction of a new SKU is evaluated by the change in the result of the category as a whole: did total demand, margin, and need coverage increase—or were sales simply redistributed among SKUs? AFTER AN ASSORTMENT CHANGE, CHECK CATEGORY DEMAND Did the category grow—or did sales simply transfer among SKUs? CATEGORY MARGIN Did total margin increase—or did only its source change? CUSTOMER NEED COVERAGE Was a new customer need met—or was another similar product simply added? AFTER REMOVAL Did demand transfer to another SKU—or leave the category / chain? If only the internal SKU mix changed, the assortment became different—but the category did not become stronger."],["content/c17.html#pdf-087",82,"TWO DECISIONS · ONE CRITERION","TWO DECISIONS · ONE CRITERION What does a new SKU add to the category—and where will demand go after it is removed? NEW SKU What does it add to the category? A supplier can offer a good product, a discount, and marketing support. But that alone is not a reason to introduce the item. A new item is justified if it adds: price point form or function in-demand brand customer segment role in traffic and margin What matters is the incremental gain to the category. SKU REMOVAL Where will demand transfer? The slowest-moving product is not always the best candidate for removal. It may meet a rare but unique need, support an important brand, or provide a necessary price point. What will the customer do when this product is no longer available? DEMAND STAYS IN THE CHAIN — the customer easily chooses another SKU. DEMAND LEAVES THE CHAIN — there is no acceptable substitute, or customer preference is strong. Assortment optimization is the ability to remove inventory without removing demand. In practice, an assortment is rarely damaged by one obviously bad decision. More often, a category gradually emerges from many locally reasonable actions: one SKU was added because of attractive terms, another was retained because of historical sales, a third was removed because of low velocity. Each decision can be explained on its own. Together, they can destroy the logic of the category. The same principle applies in both directions: improve the assortment without losing demand."],["content/c17.html#pdf-088",83,"WHO DESIGNS THE SHELF?","WHO DESIGNS THE SHELF? Who designs the shelf—the chain or the supplier? It is natural for a supplier to want more space for its products. It is natural for the chain to want attractive terms. The problem begins when commercial terms start determining the architecture of the category. ARCHITECTURE FIRST—THEN SUPPLIER SELECTION THE SHELF EMERGED FROM DEALS: Supplier A · Supplier B · Supplier C — duplication, gaps, arbitrary depth. The supplier competes for a role in the category. The category should not be built around the supplier. The right to design customer choice should remain with the retailer. Commercial terms help choose among good options, but they do not replace assortment strategy. First, the chain must determine which roles customers need within the category. Only then do suppliers compete for the right to fulfill those roles—through price, terms, quality, and service. If the process starts with supplier offers, the category architecture gradually becomes a record of negotiations rather than deliberate customer choice. A commercial offer should respond to the category architecture, not create it."],["content/c17.html#pdf-089",84,"TWO TESTS OF ASSORTMENT STRATEGY","TWO TESTS OF ASSORTMENT STRATEGY CAN A GOOD DISCOUNT END UP COSTING TOO MUCH? Imagine a fast-moving brand customers actively seek out. The retailer cannot agree on target terms with the supplier, so it delists the brand and replaces it with a higher-margin alternative. KNOWN BRAND → TERMS NOT AGREED → HIGHER-MARGIN SUBSTITUTE → LOST CUSTOMER TRAFFIC The decision may look attractive in a purchasing report. But if customers reject the substitute, procurement savings can be outweighed by lost traffic. If negotiations lead you to delist a product customers came to buy, the discount may cost more than it saves. THE MARKET CAN MOVE FASTER THAN THE ASSORTMENT PLAN A 100-store pharmacy chain launches a new product in 20 locations. Sales grow rapidly in those stores. 20 PILOT LOCATIONS → CONFIRMED DEMAND → SIMILAR LOCATIONS → SCALE IN STAGES, NOT ALL 80 AT ONCE Rolling the product out immediately to the remaining 80 pharmacies would be a mistake. Expansion should follow confirmed demand: pilot first, compare similar locations, then scale only after the results are validated. The market can change in a week. The assortment plan cannot take six months to catch up."],["content/c17.html#pdf-090",85,"CATEGORY MAP · STAGE CHECK","CATEGORY MAP · STAGE CHECK Category management becomes strategic when the chain itself designs customer choice, while suppliers and SKUs occupy roles within that architecture. DECISION MAP Where should choice be added, where should duplication be removed, where should depth change, and where should the product not be held locally at all? 1 What need does the category meet? 2 Where is the assortment too narrow or too deep? 3 What role does each SKU perform? 4 What did the new SKU add to the category as a whole? 5 Where does demand move after a removal? 6 Is the supplier designing the shelf instead of the chain? 7 How quickly does the chain detect confirmed new demand? 8 How much shelf space and capital does the category consume relative to its result? Evolution is not from fewer SKUs to more. It is from thinking in products to thinking in categories. Such a map is needed not only for periodic assortment reviews. It is a set of questions through which every significant decision to introduce, remove, or scale an SKU should pass. The clearer the answers, the less the assortment depends on historical happenstance and individual commercial offers."],["content/c17.html#pdf-091",86,"TRANSITION · FROM CATEGORY TO NETWORK ASSORTMENT","TRANSITION · FROM CATEGORY TO NETWORK ASSORTMENT Previously, the supplier could largely determine the shelf. Now a mature chain designs the category itself; the supplier competes for a role within the customer's decision. The assortment becomes the retailer's own asset and a driver of customer traffic. IF THE CHAIN HAS ALREADY DECIDED WHAT TO SELL, DOES IT HAVE TO KEEP ALL OF IT IN EVERY PHARMACY? LOCAL SHELF What is physically stored at this location. Limited by space and local inventory. NETWORK ASSORTMENT What the chain can actually make available to the customer. Central warehouse · other locations · order. The shelf remains local. The assortment becomes network-wide. Where should each product physically be held so that the chain's offering remains convenient for the customer?"],["content/c18.html#pdf-092",87,"Where should each product physically be held for the customer to still consider the chain's offer convenient?","PART II · STAGE 8 Online ordering, centralized inventory, and network assortment After category management, the chain already understands what choice it wants to offer the customer. Where should each product physically be held for the customer to still consider the chain's offer convenient? The answer depends not only on sales frequency. It depends on time. CUSTOMER PATIENCE TIME The maximum waiting time within which the customer is still willing to complete the purchase with this chain. For one type of demand, it is a few minutes. For another, a day. For a third, a week or more. That is why a broad assortment does not have to be fully duplicated in every pharmacy. The shorter the customer's patience time, the closer the inventory must be to the customer. The longer the patience time, the farther upstream the product can be held."],["content/c18.html#pdf-093",88,"Customer patience time determines inventory location","Not every product is needed immediately. For some demand, customer patience time is almost zero. For other demand, waiting is acceptable if the lead time is clear and reliably met. now → later customer wait → promised time → inventory location As acceptable customer wait time increases, inventory can be held further upstream — from pharmacy to central warehouse to supplier — reducing local stock without narrowing assortment."],["content/c18.html#pdf-094",89,"THE CUSTOMER SHOULD NOT HAVE TO SEARCH FOR THE CHAIN'S INVENTORY ON FOOT","THE CUSTOMER SHOULD NOT HAVE TO SEARCH FOR THE CHAIN'S INVENTORY ON FOOT When a person comes to a pharmacy and only at the counter—from the pharmacist—learns that the product is unavailable, the chain is already too late. The customer has spent time. Then they go to a competitor. Digital ordering changes when the customer gets the answer. In advance, the customer can: 1 find the product; 2 see where it is available; 3 reserve it; 4 choose a pickup location; 5 arrive to collect an order that is already prepared. The main value here is not necessarily home delivery. In many markets, remote sales of certain medicines are regulated differently. NOW the product is already available LATER the product will be available within the promised time Even a “find → order → pick up at the pharmacy” model turns Availability Level from an internal metric into a promise to the customer. The customer should not see the chain's warehouse structure. The customer should see a clear answer: “available now” or “available at the stated time.”"],["content/c18.html#pdf-095",90,"THE SHELF REMAINS LOCAL. THE ASSORTMENT BECOMES NETWORK-WIDE","THE SHELF REMAINS LOCAL. THE ASSORTMENT BECOMES NETWORK-WIDE Each pharmacy retains a local core assortment—products needed frequently, quickly, and specifically at that location. But the choice available to the customer can be much broader. U.S. retailers often call this the \"endless aisle\": the customer can order what is not on this particular shelf. LOCAL SHELF · NETWORK-WIDE CHOICE Through digital ordering, the customer sees the network assortment, selects a product and pickup location; the chain selects the inventory source. PHARMACY — FREQUENT DEMAND, AVAILABLE IMMEDIATELY CENTRAL WAREHOUSE — RARE DEMAND, AVAILABLE IN 1–2 DAYS SUPPLIER — ULTRA-RARE SKUs, AVAILABLE ON ORDER SMALL PHARMACY ≠ LIMITED CHOICE The local pharmacy serves demand with short customer patience time. The central warehouse covers rare and slow-moving demand. Suppliers can cover ultra-rare demand when the lead time is predictable and acceptable to the customer."],["content/c18.html#pdf-096",91,"WHY HOLD ONE RARE PRODUCT IN HUNDREDS OF PHARMACIES?","WHY HOLD ONE RARE PRODUCT IN HUNDREDS OF PHARMACIES? Imagine a chain of several hundred pharmacies. There is an expensive or rare item that does not sell every day. If one unit is held at every location, the chain finances hundreds of local inventory positions for a small number of actual sales. HUNDREDS OF LOCAL INVENTORY POSITIONS rare product duplicated across the chain AGGREGATED INVENTORY rare demand aggregated in one place A central warehouse changes the economics. The product is held where demand is aggregated. The customer places an order, chooses the pickup pharmacy, and the chain delivers the product along an existing route. If actual delivery time is shorter than the customer's patience time, local duplication of inventory becomes unnecessary. the customer gets a broader real choice; the chain does not multiply rare inventory by the number of pharmacies. Centralizing rare assortment expands choice without a proportional increase in inventory."],["content/c18.html#pdf-097",92,"WHAT CHANGES IN THE REGIONS?","WHAT CHANGES IN THE REGIONS? A small pharmacy has a physical constraint. But its available assortment does not have to be equally small. If the location is connected to a central warehouse, digital ordering, and a regular route, the customer gains access to products that are uneconomic to hold locally. This is especially important where a trip to a large city becomes part of the cost of the purchase itself. CONSTRAINTS MUST BE CHECKED SEPARATELY product type; prescription status; temperature requirements; legislation; available logistics. A strong chain extends the assortment advantage of central infrastructure to every local site."],["content/c18.html#pdf-098",93,"WHAT IF THE PRODUCT IS NOT EVEN AT THE CENTRAL WAREHOUSE?","WHAT IF THE PRODUCT IS NOT EVEN AT THE CENTRAL WAREHOUSE? Sometimes the best way to manage an ultra-rare product is not to hold it in your own inventory. But only on one condition: The chain must be able to make a reliable delivery-time promise. You need to know who will supply the product, how long it will take, how likely the promised time is to be met, and what constraints apply. Then the customer sees not simply “out of stock,” but: “Available on order. Expected pickup time: [stated time].” Physically, the product may belong to the supplier today. But the chain has another capability—the ability to organize reliable access. The assortment is defined not only by what is in stock. It is defined by what the chain can reliably provide to the customer within the promised time. THREE LEVELS OF AVAILABILITY IN THE PHARMACY immediately frequent demand; almost no patience CENTRAL WAREHOUSE with the next delivery route rare demand; willing to wait a day or two SPECIAL ORDER known, longer lead time ultra-rare demand; willing to wait longer The core formula: the promised time must fit within the customer's patience time—and the chain must be able to meet that promise."],["content/c18.html#pdf-099",94,"A BROAD ASSORTMENT IS NOT A CATALOG","A BROAD ASSORTMENT IS NOT A CATALOG Adding another ten thousand SKUs to a digital catalog is technically easier than guaranteeing fulfillment. The maturity of a network assortment is determined not by the number of items on the screen, but by the share of requests for which the chain can make a real, deliverable promise. Abandonment because of waiting time is especially important. It helps estimate actual customer patience time as observed behavior."],["content/c18.html#pdf-100",95,"A broad assortment does not begin with a catalog. It begins with a reliable promise.","A broad assortment does not begin with a catalog. It begins with a reliable promise. customer patience time increases → HOW MARKET POWER CHANGES The chain no longer competes only on what is physically stocked on the local shelf. It competes on its ability to offer broader choice and meet the promised time. Hundreds of pharmacies become access points to one network-wide assortment."],["content/c18.html#pdf-101",96,"TRANSITION · FROM INVENTORY LOCATION TO THE PHYSICS OF ITS MOVEMENT","TRANSITION · FROM INVENTORY LOCATION TO THE PHYSICS OF ITS MOVEMENT At this stage, the chain already knows which inventory should be local, which should be at the central warehouse, and which can be supplied on order. WE HAVE DECIDED WHERE INVENTORY SHOULD BE. BUT DOES A PERSON HAVE TO WALK TO THE PRODUCT? QUESTION 1 Where should inventory be held? Decided. NEW LIMIT Even a product we have decided to hold in this pharmacy—does it have to be stored next to the person? NEW CAPABILITY The system finds the required package itself and delivers it to the workstation. That changes the pharmacy's physical model. Even local inventory still requires physical movement. The issue is no longer where inventory is held, but the physics of its storage and movement within the location. The next stage is storage robotics: no longer the location of inventory, but the physics of how it is stored and moved within the location."],["content/c19.html#robotics-opening",97,"Should a person have to walk to the product?","Should a person have to walk to the product? PART II · STAGE 9 Storage robotics, space, inventory, and a new physical model Imagine one of the best pharmacy locations in the city. High traffic. Good visibility. Ground-floor space is expensive. The owner is happy with the location and does not want to move. A significant share of the expensive space is occupied by medicine storage cabinets. That is where the employee goes to retrieve the required package. The customer does not see most of this inventory. Why should a product the customer cannot see occupy the pharmacy's most expensive square meters? At the previous stage, we already separated the assortment from its storage location. Not every product has to be physically stocked in every pharmacy. But the next question arises. Even a product we have decided to keep in this pharmacy—does it have to be stored next to the person? If the system can find the required package itself and deliver it to the dispensing point, more than search speed changes. The physical economics of the pharmacy changes. This is the sense in which this chapter considers robotics. Not as equipment. Not as another expensive machine. But as a capability for removing constraints on the physical movement of products."],["content/c19.html#robotics-space",98,"FIRST CONSTRAINT · SPACE","FIRST CONSTRAINT · SPACE Storage and visible assortment no longer have to compete for the same square meter In a conventional pharmacy, layout often follows the physics of manual labor: cabinets and storage areas must be close enough for an employee to reach the required package quickly and return. As a result, storage quietly begins to consume some of the most expensive retail space. Automated storage breaks this link. Inventory can be deeper inside the premises, higher, lower, in a basement, on another floor, or in another technical area, provided the system reliably delivers the required package to the workstation. The assortment of a particular pharmacy does not have to occupy its most expensive space. For the owner of one strong pharmacy, this may mean being able to remain in the best location without buying or renting more expensive ground-floor space. For a chain, it means being able to design different formats: make storage more compact, allocate more expensive space to visible assortment—cosmetics, personal care, supplements, and other categories customers choose on the sales floor—and open more compact locations in expensive areas. A freed square meter matters only when it starts earning: expanding visible assortment, giving space to categories with better economics, or serving more customers without expanding the premises. Saving space alone is not yet a result. The result comes when an expensive square meter starts working for assortment, sales, and customer traffic."],["content/c19.html#robotics-person",99,"SECOND CONSTRAINT · HUMAN TRAVEL","SECOND CONSTRAINT · HUMAN TRAVEL Why is professional time spent walking to retrieve a package? Manual storage forces the person to move to the product. The customer names the medicine. The employee leaves the counter, searches for the right location, takes the package, and returns. One such operation seems small. But business consists of repetition. The constraint is especially visible not in the daily average but during peak hours. While one employee searches for a product, the next customer is already at the counter. If flow increases, the company responds in the usual way: adds a person, expands the back office, or accepts a queue. The automated model reverses the direction of movement: the person does not go to inventory; the product comes to the person. In logistics, this principle is often called goods-to-person. The manual model has another hidden cost: learning a new geography of inventory. A new employee must remember which cabinet, drawer, and zone contains each product and initially spends more time searching. In a goods-to-person model, this part of onboarding is reduced: the employee must learn the system and operating rules, but no longer needs to memorize the physical location of thousands of packs. The robot takes over movement of the package. It does not have to take over the professional decision. That is why value cannot be measured only by headcount reduction. For a small pharmacy, the capability of the existing team to serve greater flow, absorb sickness and vacations, depend less on one employee, and leave more time for consultation may be far more important. For a chain, the value is process repeatability and less dependence of productivity on each location's layout. If a highly skilled pharmacist is needed to speak with the patient, review an exception, and make a professional decision, should their attention be spent on a repetitive physical operation that a system can perform consistently hundreds of times?"],["content/c19.html#robotics-inbound",100,"Goods receiving: manual or automated intake?","GOODS RECEIVING · INBOUND FLOW Goods receiving: manual or automated intake? Automation removes repetitive physical work from inbound flow, but exceptions and control remain a management task. MANUAL RECEIVING AUTOMATED RECEIVING LESS MANUAL WORK repetitive scanning and put-away FASTER RECEIVING the product becomes available in the system faster THE SYSTEM PUTS THE PRODUCT AWAY AUTOMATICALLY Exception control remains with people. The same product. Less manual work. Robotics changes not only dispensing, but also goods receiving."],["content/c19.html#robotics-capacity",101,"NEW CONSTRAINT · ROBOT CAPACITY","NEW CONSTRAINT · ROBOT CAPACITY We removed the shelf constraint—and created a new constraint Automated storage also has a limit. Every additional position inside the system costs money. Poor inventory turnover therefore acquires another cost. Previously, excess inventory tied up capital and occupied space. Now it also requires more automated capacity. You can buy a large robot. Or you can first ask why you need such a large robot at all. Imagine two pharmacies with identical sales, assortment, and Availability Level. One holds 60 days of inventory; the other maintains the same service with about 40 days. All else equal: 60 / 40 = 1.5. This does not mean a particular robot will be exactly one and a half times larger: size depends on package sizes, assortment, and required throughput. But the economic logic is simple: more days of inventory requires more capacity. Poor inventory management can force a company to buy equipment to store its own inefficiency. Excess inventory simultaneously increases working capital, required space, and capital expenditure on automation."],["content/c19.html#robotics-small-pharmacy",102,"SMALL PHARMACY · DIFFERENT ECONOMICS","SMALL PHARMACY · DIFFERENT ECONOMICS Not the robot. The ability to remain in a strong location and serve greater flow with the same team For a single pharmacy, robotics makes sense when expensive space and manual logistics are already constraining sales or customer throughput. Expensive square meters → visible assortment and sales. Less walking and manual logistics → greater flow without proportional staff growth. Easier onboarding of new employees and less time spent on receiving and put-away. System size should follow the real business constraint, not the desire to buy the maximum amount of automation. PHARMACY CHAIN · WHERE SHOULD AUTOMATION BE LOCATED? A chain does not need the same robot at every location; it needs the right automation architecture At an expensive location, the robot frees retail space; with frequent, reliable replenishment, a compact local system is sufficient; elsewhere, central inventory and lower local capacity may be more economical. A mature chain standardizes not the size of the robot, but the method for calculating where automation creates maximum value."],["content/c19.html#robotics-warehouse",103,"DISTRIBUTOR / DISTRIBUTION CENTER","DISTRIBUTOR / DISTRIBUTION CENTER Where this kind of robot truly creates value For a distributor, this type of robot is especially valuable not in the fastest flow, but where manual picking becomes expensive, slow, or risky. Medium- and slow-moving assortment / long tail. Thousands of SKUs with low picking frequency are stored densely, while the product comes to the picker. Less walking, searching, and time spent assembling pharmacy orders. Temperature-controlled products. If the system is designed for the required temperature range, it reduces unnecessary manual movement and helps maintain a controlled environment and traceability. Narcotics and other controlled medicines. Restricted access, transaction verification, and a complete movement log simplify control and reduce the risk of errors. Returns, batches, expiration dates, and serialized packages. The system helps identify products, trace movement, return eligible packages to available inventory, and select the correct batch during picking. The value is not in automating the entire warehouse, but in removing manual search and improving control where the assortment is complex, long-tail, or requires special handling."],["content/c19.html#robotics-hospital",104,"HOSPITAL · MEDICINE CLOSER TO THE PATIENT","HOSPITAL · MEDICINE CLOSER TO THE PATIENT The medicine should wait for the patient, not the patient for the medicine In a hospital, robotics is needed for more than speed. It can reduce repetitive medication logistics, reduce storage space, and bring medicines closer to the point of care. Less manual work in dispensing and picking. The same workload requires less repetitive work in searching, dispensing, and replenishment; some staff additions can be avoided as volume grows, or staff can be shifted to clinical work. Less space for medicines. Automated storage can reduce central and local inventory areas and the number of fragmented storage locations. Refrigerators, controlled medicines, and exceptions may still remain separate. Fast access on the ward. An authorized employee obtains the medicine near the patient without going to the central pharmacy; the system sees local inventory and can generate replenishment demand. Control and safety. Individual access, transaction logs, and discrepancy control provide a complete movement trail for special medicines; patient-specific unit-dose can be traced from the hospital pharmacy to the point of administration. In a hospital, robotics reduces manual medication logistics, frees some storage space, and shortens the distance between medicine and patient."],["content/c19.html#robotics-regulation",105,"A NEW ACCESS CHANNEL TO THE PHARMACY","A NEW ACCESS CHANNEL TO THE PHARMACY The pharmacy can capture demand before the customer reaches the checkout Robotic storage and secure dispensing can operate not only as internal automation, but also as a separate access channel to the pharmacy. 24/7 access. The customer can collect a pre-prepared order outside normal opening hours—without keeping the entire pharmacy process and full staff operating around the clock. ePrescription and advance picking. The electronic prescription arrives in advance; after verification, the robot selects the required packages, while staff check and assemble the patient-specific order. By the time the patient arrives, the order is ready for dispensing. This is how the pharmacy begins capturing demand before the customer's physical visit. For prescription medicines, actual automated dispensing still depends on national legislation and mandatory pharmacist involvement."],["content/c19.html#robotics-installed-diagnostic",106,"The robot is already installed. How efficiently is it being used?","SUMMARY · EFFICIENCY OF THE INSTALLED ROBOT The robot is already installed. How efficiently is it being used? An installed robot alone does not guarantee efficient automation. 1 AVAILABILITY The required products are available; high availability is not being achieved through excessive days of inventory. 2 DAYS OF INVENTORY Optimum, not maximum; fast replenishment matters more than frozen cash. 3 CAPACITY UTILIZATION Priority assortment; capacity sized for the required inventory and peak load. Are we achieving the required availability with the minimum necessary inventory? EFFICIENCY MATRIX · AVAILABILITY × INVENTORY TURNOVER AVAILABILITY BOUGHT WITH INVENTORY Excess inventory. Frozen cash. TARGET ZONE The required products are available. Inventory is working. WORST ZONE Low availability and inefficient inventory. TOO LITTLE INVENTORY Risk of lost sales. EFFICIENT Availability without excess inventory; capacity occupied by priority assortment; peak flow passes without a bottleneck. INEFFICIENT Excessive days of inventory / slow SKUs; the robot is full but availability is low; expensive capacity with no measurable effect. A robot can be a productive asset or an expensive cabinet—it depends on the quality of inventory management. LOW INVENTORY TURNOVER HIGH INVENTORY TURNOVER HIGH AVAILABILITY LOW AVAILABILITY"],["content/c19.html#robotics-owner-test",107,"STAGE CHECK · FOR MANAGEMENT","STAGE CHECK · FOR MANAGEMENT First quantify the constraint. Then the robot. Which constraint is actually costing us money today? Expensive space, staff walking, receiving, peak-hour queues, lack of the required product, or limited access to the pharmacy? How much does this constraint cost today in measurable terms? Square meters, labor hours per day, additional staff, waiting time, lost sales, or product unavailability. What inventory is truly required for the target Availability Level—and what robot is needed for that inventory? Check days of inventory, ordering quality, replenishment frequency, required capacity, and peak throughput."],["content/c19.html#robotics-owner-test-cont",108,"STAGE CHECK · CONTINUED","What new capability will robotics create? More visible assortment, greater flow with the same team, faster receiving, 24/7 dispensing, ePrescription preparation, faster order picking, or access to medicine closer to the patient? Where should automation be located to create maximum value? In a specific pharmacy, at a distribution center, in a hospital ward—or not at this location at all? If these five questions do not have a numerical or operational answer, the company is still choosing equipment rather than designing a new capability. Robotics creates value when it removes a specific physical constraint. After deciding “what and where to store,” the next question is how the product should move between receiving, storage, and the dispensing point. If this capability works, the next question is: what exactly can we repeat at the next location? Next stage: what exactly are you going to scale?"],["content/c20.html#pdf-102",109,"Scale a working operating model.","STAGE 10 · SCALING THE OPERATING MODEL Location, franchise, broad format, and purchasing alliances You can open many pharmacies quickly—and multiply every existing problem just as quickly. Each new location adds inventory, a team, a location, a stream of decisions, and the risk of drifting away from the chain's standards. That is why the true object of scaling is not the number of pharmacies. Scale a working operating model. The robot itself does not scale If one pharmacy gained value from robotic storage, that does not mean the next location needs the same robot. What scales is not the device but the capability: inventory rules, integration, replenishment, performance requirements, the service model, and an understanding of which constraint is being removed in each format. Copying equipment is easier than reproducing the economic result. Once the key functions are managed as a system, three axes of growth emerge: more locations and access to new customer traffic; more business within the existing location; 3. greater purchasing power—even without full ownership consolidation."],["content/c20.html#pdf-103",110,"LOCATION AS ACCESS TO CUSTOMER TRAFFIC","LOCATION AS ACCESS TO CUSTOMER TRAFFIC Is a pharmacy a physical space, or a position within the movement of people? A strong location is defined not only by the price per square meter, but by the quality and stability of customer traffic: a clinic, shopping center, transport hub, parking, or an everyday route. A pharmacy may be inside such a facility, at its entrance, or on a person's natural path. The key is to be within the customer's movement. Evaluate access to sustainable customer traffic, not cheap square meters. THE CHEAP-RENT TRAP Cheap premises become expensive if the flow moves away. A new shopping center, parking facility, or another destination can change people's everyday routes; rent savings turn into weak revenue and the cost of closure. A cheap location becomes very expensive when customers move elsewhere. When opening a location, look not only at today's flow, but also at who is shaping tomorrow's flow."],["content/c20.html#pdf-104",111,"ONE INTERSECTION — TWO FLOWS","ONE INTERSECTION — TWO FLOWS Even the distance between two locations tells you little by itself. Different natural routes of people may exist at the same intersection. ONE INTERSECTION · TWO FLOWS Distance is not the same as flow: people follow natural routes. FLOW A → PHARMACY A → continuation of Flow A's natural route. FLOW B → PHARMACY B → continuation of Flow B's natural route. straight-line radius ≠ flow LOOK AT HOW PEOPLE MOVE — NOT ONLY AT THE RADIUS Two nearby pharmacies do not necessarily serve the same flow. Sometimes each sits on its own natural route."],["content/c20.html#pdf-105",112,"FRANCHISING AS ACCESS TO AN OTHERWISE INACCESSIBLE LOCATION","FRANCHISING AS ACCESS TO AN OTHERWISE INACCESSIBLE LOCATION What do you do if the best location is already occupied? An independent pharmacy may already operate at a strong location: the owner has the premises, the agreement, and a local business. The chain cannot simply take over that location. But it can offer a different model: brand, purchasing, central warehouse, network assortment, automation, marketing, and standards. If the chain cannot obtain the location directly, it can build a partnership with whoever controls that location. Ownership and management control are not the same thing. The chain gains the ability to grow faster, but accepts the risk of weak execution of standards and damage to the brand. A franchise scales an advantage only together with discipline."],["content/c20.html#pdf-106",113,"MORE BUSINESS WITHIN ONE LOCATION","MORE BUSINESS WITHIN ONE LOCATION Is growth only about more pharmacies? Not necessarily. The second axis is to generate more business from each existing location. The chain already has customer traffic, trust, logistics, a warehouse, inventory management, and category expertise. This platform can support a broader format: cosmetics, personal care, medical devices, children's products, and other categories aligned with customer behavior and market rules. Growth is not only more pharmacies. It is more business within each pharmacy. DO NOT EXPAND WITHOUT A SYSTEM But here it is especially easy to confuse expansion with scaling. For illustration, imagine three notional formats: 3–5 thousand active SKUs, 10–12 thousand, and about 20 thousand. These are not universal industry boundaries. At what point does “more” stop being the same pharmacy?"],["content/c20.html#pdf-107",114,"A broad format is not created by the number of shelves. It is created by a system capable of managing those shelves.","Rapid expansion introduces new categories, more slow-moving items, more capital, more complex replenishment, more redistribution, and more managed inventory exit. A broad format is not created by the number of shelves. It is created by a system capable of managing those shelves."],["content/c20.html#pdf-108",115,"PURCHASING SCALE WITHOUT A MERGER","PURCHASING SCALE WITHOUT A MERGER Do you have to become one company to buy like a large chain? No. Several independent chains can remain competitors for the customer while pooling volume in negotiations for selected purchases—where competition rules permit it. If one chain must take an excessive batch to obtain a discount, it can damage its own inventory turnover. If aggregate volume is formed by several participants, each can take a more economically reasonable share while preserving the combined purchasing weight. But such cooperation requires separate legal review. It must not turn into coordination of prices, assortment, or other aspects of retail competition."],["content/c20.html#pdf-109",116,"WHERE DOES SCALE BEGIN TO BREAK THE MODEL?","WHERE DOES SCALE BEGIN TO BREAK THE MODEL? Growth becomes dangerous not when there are many locations. It becomes dangerous when the chain stops reproducing the quality of its model. What became worse after growth? What stopped being repeatable? Where is inventory growing faster than sales? Where does local freedom undermine the chain's standards? Signals may appear in different places: a new location operates differently, a franchisee copies the brand without the discipline, the broad format outruns the management system, or purchasing scale forces excess buying. Scaling is a test of repeatability, not a competition in the number of openings."],["content/c20.html#pdf-110",117,"STAGE CHECK · FOR MANAGEMENT","STAGE CHECK · FOR MANAGEMENT Not “how many locations can we open?” but “what model can we repeat without losing quality?” Which processes are reproduced in the same way in a new and an established location? Are locations selected based on the movement of people or the price of the premises? Can we see distinct flows even within a single intersection? Does the franchisee receive a system or only a brand? Does the broad format remain the same model, or does it already require a new business? How quickly does a new location reach normal Availability Level, inventory turnover, and margin? Does scale strengthen purchasing economics or force the business to buy excess inventory? Does the quality of the model deteriorate as the chain grows? FLOW → SYSTEM → REPEATABILITY → SCALE"],["content/c20.html#pdf-111",118,"HOW MARKET POWER CHANGES","HOW MARKET POWER CHANGES When scale begins to strengthen market power An efficient model creates additional cash resources that can be directed to new locations and formats. More locations bring more flow, data, purchasing volume, and negotiating leverage. But poor execution can consume the economic value of that scale. First build an efficient machine. Then press the accelerator. WHAT COMES NEXT? When scale begins to strengthen the purchasing position, data, and the capability to grow further, the next question arises: What happens to the market when several large systems begin competing not for individual pharmacies, but for share of the entire market?"],["content/c21.html#pdf-112",119,"What happens when one chain systematically performs better than others?","What happens when one chain systematically performs better than others? STAGE 11 · EXPANSION, CONSOLIDATION, AND MIGRATION OF FUNCTIONS Expansion, consolidation, and migration of functions At first, the difference is visible inside the company: a higher Availability Level, faster capital turnover, ordering and replenishment operating under common rules, and an assortment that changes with demand. But what happens if these advantages operate simultaneously for many years? They begin to reinforce one another. Then the chain gains more than a better result at one pharmacy. It gains the ability to grow faster—provided that a new location does not undermine the quality of the model already built. SIZE IS NOT MARKET POWER Size alone does not guarantee durable market power. It can create negotiating leverage, but control of critical functions makes the position stronger and harder to replace. If two chains are equally large, but one controls warehousing, purchasing, assortment, and customer access while the other depends on external players—are they equally powerful?"],["content/c21.html#pdf-113",120,"THE ACCELERATION POINT","THE ACCELERATION POINT Build the machine first. Then step on the gas Before a system is in place, growth often increases the need for money and management attention. Once the system exists, growth can create resources for the next stage of growth. a high Availability Level retains customers; inventory turnover releases capital; automation reduces dependence on individual people; a central warehouse can partially pool local uncertainty; direct purchasing and private label can retain a larger share of margin within the chain; redistribution releases capital from excess or inefficiently positioned inventory; inefficiently positioned inventory; category management and network assortment meet more customer needs. Scale before efficiency multiplies problems. Scale after efficiency can multiply advantage. The key word is can. If the quality of the model declines, growth again becomes a source of complexity. HOW ADVANTAGE FUNDS THE NEXT STEP Efficient model → cash resources → next growth step When a chain grows faster, what is it actually buying: more locations—or more control?"],["content/c21.html#pdf-114",121,"TWO PATHS TO EXPANSION","TWO PATHS TO EXPANSION Build locations yourself or buy existing customer traffic? Organic growth builds new locations. An acquisition buys existing locations, staff, inventory, and customer traffic. Mergers and acquisitions (M&A) deliver scale faster, but they do not guarantee an advantage. An acquisition buys scale. The operating model turns it into results. Without a strong integration system, the buyer may acquire not market share, but someone else’s problems. What exactly do you acquire with a new chain: market share—or someone else’s operational complexity?"],["content/c21.html#pdf-115",122,"MIGRATION OF FUNCTIONS","MIGRATION OF FUNCTIONS As the market evolves, functions that were previously performed mainly by other players migrate into the chain. When a function moves to the chain, access to its economics becomes possible—if the chain performs that function competitively. Size can strengthen negotiating leverage. Control of functions makes that power more durable."],["content/c21.html#pdf-116",123,"When the customer begins to do your work","When the customer begins to do your work WHAT REMAINS FOR THE DISTRIBUTOR? A large chain may become progressively less dependent on the traditional distributor model: its own warehouse, internal logistics, direct contracts, and centralized inventory management change the role of the intermediary. large volume and route density may decline; part of the commercial margin may shrink; the role of a mandatory intermediary may disappear. But this is not a universal law. A distributor may move into retail, bring independent pharmacies together, become an infrastructure operator, or sell logistics, data, and specialized services. The distributor's risk is losing not only volume, but also its former role. HOW MANY BUYERS DETERMINE A MANUFACTURER’S ACCESS TO THE MARKET? After consolidation, a significant share of customer traffic may be concentrated among a limited number of large chains. They influence assortment, availability, promotional visibility, competition with private label, and data on actual sales. There may be many competitors on the manufacturing side. But access to the patient is often controlled by only a few large buyers. Scale thus becomes control of access—but only where the chain actually controls a meaningful share of customer traffic."],["content/c21.html#pdf-117",124,"Size or market power?","Size or market power? Two chains can be roughly the same size and still have completely different real power. Control does not necessarily mean ownership. It can be operational, contractual, infrastructural, or informational. What matters is where the critical bottlenecks are—and who has the capability to manage them."],["content/c21.html#pdf-118",125,"CONSOLIDATION IS PRESSURE, NOT DESTINY","CONSOLIDATION IS PRESSURE, NOT DESTINY Must the market inevitably converge on a few large chains? No. Economics can create pressure toward consolidation, but the final market structure is shaped by ownership rules, geography, reimbursement for medicines, concentration limits, the role of independent pharmacies, consumer culture, and public policy. LARGE CHAINS High concentration where scaling is permitted and efficient. REGIONAL SYSTEMS Consolidation proceeds by region and leaves several strong centers. INDEPENDENTS + INFRASTRUCTURE Ownership remains fragmented, while power concentrates in purchasing, logistics, or associations. No scenario is inevitable. In one market, independent pharmacies give way to chains; in another, the law limits concentration and strengthens distributors, purchasing groups, or associations. If a more efficient chain can scale while preserving the quality of its model, its advantage begins to reshape the market structure. Market structure depends not only on the efficiency of players, but also on the rules under which they are allowed to grow."],["content/c21.html#pdf-119",126,"Pharmacy chain evolution map","Pharmacy chain evolution map Evolution shows which next capability needs to be built. Market power explains why it matters. CAPABILITY ECONOMIC IMPACT WHAT IT ENABLES NEXT HOW POWER CHANGES Availability Level Retains demand and customers Stable flow Customer advantage Inventory turnover Releases capital Expansion Financial flexibility Order automation Scales rules A larger chain Management control Central warehouse Shortens replenishment time; can partially pool local risk Direct purchasing Supply chain control Can retain more purchasing margin within the chain Private label; a deeper commercial function Less dependence on an intermediary Private label / own brand Can retain a larger share of commercial margin within the chain Its own product function and differentiation Control of its own product economics Redistribution Releases capital from excess inventory A broader assortment Network inventory control Category management Designs customer choice Strong assortment architecture Shelf control Network assortment Expands access without duplicating inventory More customer needs met Control of access to the assortment Online ordering / digital channel Adds a digital sales channel and directs demand to the pharmacy Reservation, ordering, and pickup through the chain's pharmacies Control of digital access to customer traffic Automated storage / picking Reduces manual labor and errors; increases speed Scalable order fulfillment Cost and service advantage at scale Location / format / franchise Creates new flows and formats Expanded presence Control of access to customer traffic Scalable model Replicates the system without loss of quality Locations, formats, alliances Negotiating power Not a maturity ladder: no row is mandatory for every market. Management checklist: what works, what to strengthen, what to build next."],["content/c21.html#pdf-120",127,"Retail section check","Retail section check Which capability is most lacking today for the company’s next stage of development? CAPABILITY NEEDED NOW? CURRENT STATE MAIN CONSTRAINT NEXT DECISION Availability Level and missed sales Yes No Inventory turnover and capital Automated ordering Central warehouse and replenishment Direct purchasing and economics Redistribution and inventory removal Category management Digital ordering and network assortment Location, franchise, and format Scaling without loss of quality Which critical function are we still leaving to another player—and what does that mean for our economics and power?"],["content/c21.html#b057-kpi-page-01",128,"Eight KPIs. One management system","Eight KPIs. One management system An executive does not need eight separate reports . What is needed is one system of questions: result → cost of the result → quality of capital → decision. 52-week margin Lost margin Days of inventory GMROI Own-financing days Inventory age Expiration risk Unwanted overstock Eight numbers—eight management questions 1 Is a full year of business becoming stronger or weaker? 2 How much result did we leave to competitors because products were unavailable? 3 How much capital is tied up in inventory today? 4 How much margin does each €1 of average inventory generate? 5 Is this inventory financed by the supplier or already by the company? 6 How long has money been tied up in this specific inventory? 7 Where is tomorrow’s write-off risk forming today? 8 Where is inaction already unacceptable? Open the relevant KPI directly in the meeting . Every page follows the same logic: what it shows → how to read it → what to view alongside it → what to ask → typical mistake → action."],["content/c21.html#b057-kpi-page-02",129,"52-week margin","52-week margin One month can easily be mistaken for seasonality. A rolling 52 weeks answers a more useful question: is a full year of business generating more gross margin—or less? +12% directional illustration -6 -5 -4 -3 -2 -1 now How to read it Look at the direction of the curve, not a single month. Separate the effect of scale—new locations, prices, market—from improvements in the existing system. Growth in absolute margin alone does not prove that management has become more efficient. What to view alongside it Lost margin —are we losing results? Days of inventory —how much capital did growth require? GMROI —is capital working better? What to ask in the meeting 1 What explains the change over the last 52 weeks? 2 Which categories made the largest contribution? 3 Is this growth in system quality or only in scale? 4 How much capital did this result require?"],["content/c21.html#b057-kpi-page-03",130,"Lost margin","Lost margin A stockout is not just an operational problem. It is money the system could have earned but did not. 0.5% lost margin illustration 8.1% 4.2% 2.1% m−3 m−2 m−1 now How to read it Look at both the amount of the loss and the percentage. The trend matters more than a single period. The company-wide average hides the source of the problem—you need to drill down to specific items. Key pair Lost margin × days of inventory. If the loss falls while inventory rises sharply, service may have been bought with excess capital. What to ask in the meeting 1 Which 10 items created the largest loss? 2 Are these recurring or new problems? 3 Where is the cause: forecast, order, lead time, or allocation? 4 What rule do we change so the problem does not recur?"],["content/c21.html#b057-kpi-page-04",131,"Days of inventory","Days of inventory Days of inventory is a simple way to express capital intensity . It shows how many days of current sales the average inventory can support. 54 days of inventory illustration 72 68 62 58 56 80 60 40 20 Jan Feb Mar Apr May Jun How to read it A reduction is useful only if lost margin does not rise alongside it. Otherwise, capital was released at the customer’s expense. An increase is not always bad: a seasonal peak or supply risk may require a deliberate buffer. The question is whether we are managing it. What to view alongside it Lost margin —did we lose sales? GMROI —does the inventory pay off? Age —is all inventory moving? Financing —who pays for the cycle? What to ask in the meeting 1 Where did days of inventory increase the most? 2 What is the cause: demand, MOQ, discount, lead time, or replenishment? 3 Do high inventory and high loss occur at the same time? 4 Which items can be reduced without worsening service?"],["content/c21.html#b057-kpi-page-05",132,"GMROI — margin on inventory invested","GMROI — margin on inventory invested Two categories can generate the same margin while requiring very different amounts of capital . GMROI shows how much gross margin each €1 of average inventory generates. 230% GMROI = 2.3× · illustration 100% 145% 160% 178% 195% 215% 250% 200% Jan Feb Mar Apr May Jun More margin with the same average inventory. The same result with lower average inventory. High GMROI achieved through chronic stockouts. Where it is especially useful Category comparisons; large purchases “for the discount”; assortment decisions; supplier negotiations; slow-moving items. What to ask in the meeting 1 Where does capital generate the least margin? 2 Is the cause low margin or excessive inventory? 3 Which discounts reduced GMROI because of purchase volume? 4 What will happen to availability if this inventory is reduced?"],["content/c21.html#b057-kpi-page-06",133,"Own-financing days","Own-financing days Two suppliers with the same price can have different costs for the business . This KPI shows how many days of the operating cycle the company finances with its own money. 22 days financed by us illustration 68 days of inventory · retail example where DSO ≈ 0 DIO — days inventory outstanding · DSO — days sales outstanding (time waiting for customer payment) · DPO — days payable outstanding (supplier payment terms actually used). In finance, this is known as the cash conversion cycle (CCC). 0 days 34 31 29 27 24 40 20 0 Jan Feb Mar Apr May Jun How to read it Review it by supplier, category, and channel. The more positive days, the longer the company’s own capital remains tied up before returning as cash. A reduction is good only if it was not bought by worsening availability, margin, or supply terms. GMROI × financing Do not mix the formulas: GMROI shows margin per €1 of average inventory; this KPI shows how many days the money is actually ours. What to ask in the meeting 1 Where do we finance the cycle for the longest? 2 What creates the days: DIO, DSO, or weak DPO? 3 Do margin and GMROI compensate for this capital requirement? 4 What is more profitable to change: batch size, frequency, customer payment, or supplier payment terms?"],["content/c21.html#b057-kpi-page-07",134,"Inventory age","Inventory age Average inventory turnover can look normal while a layer of older batches is growing underneath it. Inventory age shows how long capital remains tied up in specific products and where old inventory is accumulating. Trend in inventory age structure 0 20 40 60 80 100 18% 20% 23% 27% 25% 21% Jan Feb Mar Apr May Jun -4 pp vs. May 180+ days 121–180 days 91–120 days 61–90 days 31–60 days 0–30 days share of inventory aged 90+ days How to read it Look not only at the share of old inventory today, but also at how it changes from month to month. The key is to understand whether the old layer is shrinking—and whether new old inventory is being created. What to view it with Own-financing days · expiration risk · unwanted overstock · actual demand. What to ask at the meeting 1 Where is the 90+ day layer growing? 2 Which categories are creating new old inventory? 3 What is not selling: is it a demand, price, or purchasing problem? 4 What is the exit plan, and who is responsible?"],["content/c21.html#b057-kpi-page-08",135,"Expiration risk","Expiration risk Inventory age looks backward. Shelf-life risk looks forward: can we turn the product back into cash before it can no longer be sold? The key question is what share of inventory will not sell through at current demand. What will expire in the next 6 months 0 50 100 150 200 90 160 180 120 70 Oct Nov Dec Jan Feb Mar expected to sell before expiration at risk of not selling illustration Risk trend ≤ 90 days 310 280 250 220 184 Apr May Jun Jul Aug Sep Response windows 180+ daysmonitoring 91–180 prevention 31–90 active action ≤30 urgent decision How to read it Look not only at the volume of short-dated batches, but at the volume that will not sell through before expiration at current demand. Focus on the value and share of inventory at risk, as well as the months in which the risk is concentrated. What to view it with FEFO—sell the batch with the nearest expiration date first. Also review: actual demand · days of inventory · promotion plan · returns · redistribution across locations. What to ask at the meeting 1 Which batches and SKUs contain the risk? 2 Why is the risk higher in specific months? 3 What can be done: price, promotion, redistribution, return? 4 How much risk will we eliminate in the next 30 days?"],["content/c21.html#b057-kpi-page-09",136,"Unwanted overstock","Unwanted overstock Unwanted overstock is any quantity above what a location should hold, whether because demand has fallen, the product is being discontinued, or its expiration date leaves too little time to sell it. For this inventory, inaction is no longer acceptable : every item needs a specific decision, owner, and deadline. €2.4 million · illustration Trend in unwanted overstock, % 58.6% 50.2% 42.7% 35.2% 8% / €190 thousand — still no decision How to define it The criterion must be formalized: inventory above the defined limit; no movement; age above the threshold; expiration risk; discontinued product; inventory does not match demand. The rule must be reproducible rather than dependent on a manager's subjective judgment. What must be done 1 Assign an owner. 2 Open a mini-project. 3 Choose an exit scenario. 4 Track monthly. What to track By supplier · by category · by manager · by location. Separately track new unwanted overstock created during the period."],["content/c21.html#b057-kpi-page-10",137,"Not eight reports. One management conversation","Not eight reports. One management conversation The eight metrics are not there to produce eight reports. They should provide one coherent view of the business—and lead to one type of decision: how to turn inventory into profit rather than a problem. What did the business earn—and what did it lose? 52-week margin Lost margin How much capital was required to produce this result? Days of inventory GMROI Own-financing days Is invested capital turning into a future loss? Inventory age Expiration risk Unwanted overstock The same conversation for every deviation 1 NUMBER What changed? 2 TREND Is it better or worse? 3 WHERE? SKU, category, supplier, location 4 WHY? Main cause 5 OWNER Who is responsible for the decision? 6 ACTION What are we doing? 7 DEADLINE By when? 8 CHECK How will we know it worked? Three mandatory linkages Are we buying service with excess capital—and are we saving capital at the customer's expense? How much margin does €1 of inventory generate—and how many days of this cycle do we finance with our own money? Where does aging inventory turn into a future loss—and where should inaction already trigger a mandatory mini-project? The dashboard and the largest exceptions are distributed in advance . The meeting is not used to search for new numbers. Management time is spent on causes, owners, and process changes."],["content/c21.html#v2-kpi-page-05-owner-pack",138,"One product flow should have one end-to-end owner","ONE SUPPLY CHAIN One product flow should have one end-to-end owner The supplier, central warehouse, distribution, and pharmacy may sit in different departments. But for the customer, there is only one outcome—the product is either available or it is not. If everyone is responsible only for their own segment, the cause of the loss falls between departments. PRODUCT MOVES THROUGH THE ORGANIZATION WHO SHOULD SEE THIS ENTIRE CHAIN? ONE SUPPLY CHAIN MANAGEMENT FUNCTION The name may differ: supply chain, inventory and replenishment management, planning and logistics. The point is not the name, but end-to-end responsibility for planning, ordering, replenishment, distribution, inventory movement, and Availability Level—the ability to ensure that the customer has the required product when it is needed—from supplier to point of sale. CATEGORY MANAGER Assortment, commercial terms, price, the role of the category, and the decision about what the company wants to sell. END-TO-END SUPPLY CHAIN FUNCTION To ensure that the selected assortment is physically available: the right inventory, in the right place, at the right time, with a clear cause identified for every failure. THE SAME FUNCTION ALSO PREPARES THE MONTHLY MANAGEMENT PACK 1 · THE BIG PICTURE 8 KPIs on one page: value, trend, variance. 2 · EXCEPTIONS ONLY The following pages appear only where a metric has deteriorated. 3 · DOWN TO THE SPECIFIC CAUSE Category → category manager → supplier → location → item → owner of the cause. The report is sent to the executive in advance. The meeting is not spent looking for numbers—it is spent analyzing causes and changing the process."],["content/c21.html#v2-kpi-page-06-operating-rhythm",139,"Do not search for the problem in reports. Analyze the largest causes","MONTHLY MEETING Do not search for the problem in reports. Analyze the largest causes A KPI shows where to look. Management begins when the aggregate number becomes a short list of specific items that caused the deterioration. TOP 10 FOR THE MONTH What created the most lost margin? Product item · location · category · supplier · amount of loss TOP 10 NEW PROBLEMS Where did the largest unwanted overstock position emerge? Product item · where it is held · age · capital · owner FOR EACH ITEM—THE SAME CONVERSATION EXAMPLE · LOST MARGIN The product was unavailable. Why? The delivery arrived late. Why? The order was triggered late. Why? The replenishment parameter did not reflect the actual lead time. Decision: change the replenishment rule and parameter controls—not simply 'ask the supplier to be more attentive.' EXAMPLE · UNWANTED OVERSTOCK Inventory rose sharply. Why? Volume was purchased to obtain a discount. Why? Actual demand was not a constraint on the decision. Why? There is no rule that stops purchasing above the permitted inventory level. Decision: change the purchasing rule and limit—not merely try to sell off the excess already created. END OF EVERY REVIEW: what we change in the process → who owns it → deadline → how we will verify the result next month DAILY · OPERATIONS TEAM SKU, OOS, order, excess, expiration, transfer, and return. MONTHLY · MANAGEMENT Largest exceptions, 'why?', root cause, and process change."],["content/c21.html#pdf-121",140,"Evolution of the Pharmacy Chain","Evolution of the Pharmacy Chain The pharmacy chain began as a point of sale. It then learned to manage availability, capital, ordering, warehousing, purchasing, excess inventory, assortment, digital access, and scale. As a result, it began performing an increasing share of the work that had previously given other players volume, margin, and influence. THE FUNCTION MOVES IN-HOUSE—THE ECONOMICS FOLLOW CHAIN CAPABILITY THE FUNCTION MOVES IN-HOUSE PART OF THE ECONOMIC VALUE SHIFTS TO THE CHAIN DEPENDENCE DECLINES MARKET POWER SHIFTS Warehouse + replenishment inventory and logistics management availability · capital · dependence Direct purchasing + categories part of the trading function margin · terms · shelf control Data + digital access + scale information and access to the customer bargaining power · control of demand FUNCTION MIGRATION CHANGES WHERE MARGIN, CAPITAL, DATA, AND CONTROL REMAIN. NOW THE CENTRAL QUESTION CHANGES If your largest customers are gradually building their own warehouses, logistics, purchasing, and direct relationships with manufacturers—what should your business become? This is no longer the next stage of the pharmacy chain. This is the beginning of a new part. NEXT PART: THE DISTRIBUTOR'S CHOICE"],["content/c22.html#pdf-122",141,"When do customers become competitors?","When do customers become competitors? A distributor's move into retail and the change of mindset A distributor historically creates real value. It aggregates purchases from manufacturers, breaks down large lots, holds a broad assortment, provides trade credit, manages the warehouse, and delivers products to many pharmacies. But what happens when the largest customers begin building part of this infrastructure in-house? A pharmacy chain builds its own warehouse. Accelerates replenishment. Aggregates demand. Begins purchasing directly. Creates its own system for data, category management, and logistics. The customer remains a customer of the distributor. But at the same time, it begins competing with the distributor for the function. When a customer learns to perform part of your work, it becomes a competitor for the economics of that function. It is important to see that competition begins even before a direct clash in the retail market. The chain may continue buying from the distributor while simultaneously taking over the warehousing function, purchasing negotiations, data, or part of logistics. The volume of the relationship remains, while the distributor's role is already narrowing. Therefore, the owner's question is not only 'Are we losing the customer?' but also 'Which of our functions has the customer already learned to perform without us?' If the chain takes functions upstream, shouldn't the distributor itself move downstream—into retail? Perhaps it should. But buying pharmacies does not mean the company has become a retailer."],["content/c22.html#pdf-123",142,"Can you buy a pharmacy chain and continue thinking like a distributor?","Can you buy a pharmacy chain and continue thinking like a distributor? CHANGE OF MINDSET In wholesale, the natural logic looks like this: where to buy more cheaply; what volume will secure the best price; how to distribute product faster; how to utilize the warehouse and routes; how to sell the volume already purchased. In distribution, this logic is rational. In retail, it becomes dangerous if it remains the primary decision-making logic. Retail must start with different questions: Why should the customer come here specifically? What need are they trying to meet? What should they find in the pharmacy? How does customer traffic flow? Why should the customer come back? A distributor primarily optimizes the movement of products among market participants. Retail connects the customer, the customer's need, and the point of sale. Retail does not start with cheaper purchasing. It starts with the customer."],["content/c22.html#pdf-124",143,"Who actually makes the decision?","Who actually makes the decision? MANAGEMENT MECHANISM The old management system does not disappear The challenge is that after pharmacies are acquired, the old management system does not disappear. The same buyers see the discount. The same warehouses see the volume. The same financial metrics reward turnover. And if retail does not gain its own decision rights, wholesale logic begins to govern the retail location almost invisibly. A change of mindset must not be a declaration but a change in the management process. You need to define who shapes the assortment, who has the right to reject volume, who is responsible for customer traffic, and who is accountable for the performance of a specific pharmacy. This is where acquiring pharmacies becomes either a genuine retail business or simply another channel for familiar wholesale logic. Question for the owner In a dispute, who has the final say: purchasing, because the price is good, or retail, because it does not need the volume? If there is no answer, the organizational structure has formally changed, but what is being managed has not."],["content/c22.html#pdf-125",144,"MENTAL MODEL","MENTAL MODEL Wholesale logic is rational within distribution. The mistake begins when product logic becomes the primary logic of a retail decision."],["content/c22.html#pdf-126",145,"We bought it cheaper. Now someone has to sell it?","We bought it cheaper. Now someone has to sell it? THINKING TRAP Imagine a product that sells about once a year in a particular pharmacy. An opportunity appears to buy it very cheaply. Wholesale logic says: The price is good. We should buy more. And twenty units arrive at the pharmacy. The seller is then told: 'Now sell them.' ≈ 1 sale per year → 20 units in the pharmacy But demand has not changed. There are no more customers. The need has not increased. The product has simply moved from the warehouse to the pharmacy. You cannot buy a product first and then order retail to create a customer for it. A discount can increase inventory. It does not automatically create demand. If the product is rarely needed, twenty cheap units may turn out to be frozen capital rather than a good purchase. Did the discount create demand—or only inventory?"],["content/c22.html#pdf-127",146,"Is a pharmacy a warehouse with a door for the customer?","Is a pharmacy a warehouse with a door for the customer? RETAIL SPACE The same mistake appears in the retail space. If a company is used to managing boxes, it can easily begin to believe that a good pharmacy is one where it has managed to place more product. A pharmacy is not a warehouse that customers have been allowed into. A retail location should be organized around customer behavior: is it clear how to enter, easy to navigate, is the required category visible, is it easy to choose, comfortable to spend time there, and is there a reason to return? Robotic storage makes it possible to hold inventory at the location without occupying expensive retail space. A specific chain must understand its own audience. If the data show that women make up the primary purchasing audience, this should be reflected in navigation, assortment, space, and service—not as a stereotype, but as a consequence of actual customer behavior. Retail space should be designed around the customer, not around the convenience of storing products. Logistics, inventory density, and merchandising efficiency remain important, but they become constraints within the customer model rather than the model itself."],["content/c22.html#pdf-128",147,"MANAGEMENT SEPARATION","MANAGEMENT SEPARATION What should the owner change? A retail location must maintain two disciplines at once: the product must be available, and buying must be clear and convenient for the customer. If one suppresses the other, the owner gets either an attractive but operationally weak pharmacy or an efficient warehouse with a checkout. If a distributor decides to build its own retail business, changing the ownership structure is not enough. The two management logics must be separated. DISTRIBUTION OPTIMIZES RETAIL OPTIMIZES purchase price customer traffic volume Availability Level warehouse assortment logistics space and service credit repeat visits serving many customers economics of a specific location Distribution can provide retail with infrastructure. But it must not make retail's key decisions on its behalf. Even their metrics differ. For distribution, a strong result may be a better purchase price, route utilization, or high warehouse inventory turnover. For retail, that is not enough: the product must be available exactly where demand exists; the assortment must match the need; and the location must retain the customer. If the same management system evaluates both businesses only through purchasing savings and volume, conflict is almost inevitable. A strong distributor can provide retail with infrastructure. Retail capability still has to be built separately."],["content/c22.html#pdf-129",148,"What should be checked before considering the move into retail complete?","What should be checked before considering the move into retail complete? STAGE CHECK · FOR THE OWNER Does a pharmacy decision start with a purchasing opportunity or with a customer need? Are we buying volume because retail needs it, or because the supplier offered a good price? Who is responsible for customer traffic and the reasons customers choose our location? Who designs the assortment and space—retail or purchasing? Does the retail business have the right to tell distribution: 'This volume we do not need'? Are we really building a retail business—or merely creating our own channel for wholesale product? Owned retail is one possible response to a changing market. But it is not the only one. How do you determine which path actually fits the structure of a particular market?"],["content/c23.html#pdf-130",149,"Do not start with the question: 'What can we do?' Start with: 'What is the market becoming?'","Owned retail, an independent pharmacy ecosystem, or third-party logistics There is no single right future for a distributor. There are at least three fundamentally different roles: Become the owner of a retail chain. Build an ecosystem around independent pharmacies. Become an operator of third-party logistics and infrastructure (3PL). We have already examined the first path: buying pharmacies is not enough—you have to learn to think like a retailer. An owned chain provides direct access to customer traffic and the ability to control retail decisions. But with that, the distributor takes on the full complexity of the retail business—locations, assortment, staff, customer experience, and the economics of each location. Therefore, owned retail is not automatically the 'next level.' It is one of three different responses to a changing market. Do not start with the question: 'What can we do?' Start with: 'What is the market becoming?' If chains are free to consolidate, owned retail may become more logical. If independent pharmacies are resilient, they can be strengthened through shared infrastructure. If chains already control commercial decisions but do not want to own all physical logistics, space opens up for 3PL. The question is not which path is more prestigious. The question is which function will remain paid for and difficult to replace in the future market structure. Regulation also changes the available roles: it can preserve room for independent ownership or accelerate consolidation."],["content/c23.html#pdf-131",150,"STRATEGIC FIELD","STRATEGIC FIELD First determine where the market structure is heading. Only then choose the business model."],["content/c23.html#pdf-132",151,"Can you build a chain without buying pharmacies?","Can you build a chain without buying pharmacies? INDEPENDENT PHARMACY ECOSYSTEM Yes—if independent pharmacies receive some of the advantages of a chain through a single organizer. The distributor can combine for them: purchasing; warehouse and frequent replenishment; trade credit; IT and automated ordering; private label; redistribution; assortment management services. Legally, the pharmacies remain independent. Economically, they begin to operate as a larger system. You can build strong infrastructure around a pharmacy and still not own the pharmacy itself. If the owner of a location can sell it to another chain tomorrow, the distributor's influence is not the same as ownership. Therefore, the distributor is not building its own retail business here, but a system of dependence on shared infrastructure: the more value the pharmacy receives through it, the more expensive it becomes to switch partners. How speed, credit, IT, exclusivity, pooled purchasing, and switching costs shape the economics of this path is the subject of the next chapter."],["content/c23.html#pdf-133",152,"If the commercial decision already belongs to the chain—what remains for the distributor?","If the commercial decision already belongs to the chain—what remains for the distributor? THIRD-PARTY LOGISTICS / 3PL If the chain already decides what to buy, from whom, how much, and what assortment to build, the distributor may still retain a strong role—infrastructure. The decision remains with the chain. Execution belongs to the operator. This is no longer classic trading distribution. The source of value changes. Previously, the distributor earned money in part from the trading function: it bought, financed, built the assortment, and resold. In the 3PL model, the customer takes the commercial decision in-house, and the operator must prove the value of execution. But this can be a sustainable business if the operator's infrastructure is cheaper, more reliable, or more flexible than the customer's warehouse and logistics. The greater the route density, process quality, and capability to serve several chains on one infrastructure, the stronger this position can be. The chain keeps the brain. The distributor rents out the body. The metaphor is sharp, but the distinction is precise: one side determines the commercial decision, while the other is responsible for physical execution."],["content/c23.html#pdf-134",153,"Which path fits your market?","Which path fits your market? STAGE CHECK · FOR THE OWNER WHAT IS HAPPENING IN THE MARKET? WHICH PATH IS MORE LOGICAL? WHAT NEEDS TO BE PROVEN? Chains are free to consolidate Owned retail We know how to attract customers, not only supply a location Independent pharmacies are resilient Independent pharmacy ecosystem We create advantages that a standalone pharmacy cannot obtain on its own Chains control commercial decisions Third-party logistics / 3PL Execution is better or cheaper than using the customer's own infrastructure. Three questions remain before a major investment: Who will control customer traffic in five to ten years? Which function will remain genuinely valuable for the distributor? Are we becoming ever better at a business model for which the market is leaving less and less room?"],["content/c23.html#pdf-135",154,"What do we want to control in five to ten years?","What do we want to control in five to ten years? CRITICAL FUNCTION This is more important than the current profitability of an individual business line. A company can earn well today from a model for which demand is structurally shrinking, while simultaneously underinvesting in the function that will become its main source of power tomorrow. Long-term advantage is often determined not by the number of functions, but by control of the function that is critical to this particular market structure. Therefore, the owner's central question is: Which critical function do we want to control in five to ten years? If the independent pharmacy ecosystem becomes that path, the next question is no longer strategic but operational. How do we turn thousands of independent pharmacies into a system that a competitor cannot replace with a single price list?"],["content/c24.html#pdf-136",155,"TIME AND UNIQUENESS","Speed, credit, exclusivity, IT, and purchasing groups Thousands of independent pharmacies do not necessarily mean that market power is fragmented. Pharmacies may have different owners yet depend on a small number of distributors — through assortment, replenishment time, trade credit, IT, exclusivities, and purchasing groups. A pharmacy can be independently owned while remaining dependent on shared infrastructure. If switching distributors means losing credit, special terms, access to assortment, and daily logistics, the pharmacy's operating freedom narrows sharply. It is around this infrastructure that a strong distributor builds a switching barrier. TIME AND UNIQUENESS What exactly does an independent pharmacy buy from a distributor? Time. In some fragmented markets I have seen replenishment take roughly two to four hours from order to delivery. For a small pharmacy, this means that a significant share of inventory is effectively held by the distributor. The pharmacy keeps less capital on its own shelves while serving broader demand. The distributor becomes the independent pharmacy's external warehouse. Uniqueness. If the law and the market allow exclusive distribution, a particular product may be available only through one supplier. Competitors can replicate speed. Exclusive access is much harder to replicate. Fast replenishment wins on service. Exclusivity protects part of demand."],["content/c24.html#pdf-137",156,"Why is a distributor like a bank?","Why is a distributor like a bank? The manufacturer wants to receive payment sooner. The pharmacy wants to pay later. The distributor finances the difference. UP THE CHAIN purchase volume faster payment better purchasing terms DOWN THE CHAIN lower working-capital requirement The distributor sells time to the pharmacy and buys price from the manufacturer with its own capital. But this mechanism creates receivables risk. Capital becomes power only when a company knows how to manage liquidity, customer concentration, and defaults."],["content/c24.html#pdf-138",157,"How do you make an independent pharmacy part of a common system?","How do you make an independent pharmacy part of a common system? AN INDEPENDENT PHARMACY WITHIN SHARED INFRASTRUCTURE For a small pharmacy, IT, integrations, upgrades, and support are often a separate headache. A distributor can remove that burden by embedding the service into the customer's daily process. The next step is a purchasing association or buying group. Independent pharmacies pool demand and receive better terms, a shared warehouse, private label, redistribution, IT, and marketing support. Service creates dependency more effectively than pressure. At the same time, pharmacies' independence in pricing and the requirements of competition law must remain transparent. The ecosystem must not turn into hidden coordination among competitors."],["content/c24.html#pdf-139",158,"Can a pharmacy be financed without owning it?","Can a pharmacy be financed without owning it? In markets with ownership restrictions, a distributor may finance a pharmacist who opens or acquires a pharmacy. In return, the agreement may require a major share of purchases to be made from the financing distributor. Economically, this creates a long-term customer. Legally, the boundary is fundamental. LAWFUL FINANCING REGULATORY RISK The pharmacist genuinely owns and manages the pharmacy The owner exists only formally The terms are transparent and proportionate Debt is used as hidden control Professional independence is preserved The model effectively circumvents ownership restrictions Capital can create loyalty without formal ownership. But hidden control is no longer a strategy; it is a legal risk. What matters in this book is the mechanism of power itself, not instructions for circumventing the law: whoever finances market infrastructure often gains influence over future purchasing flows."],["content/c24.html#pdf-140",159,"Can a market be legally open but economically closed?","Can a market be legally open but economically closed? Yes. A strong distributor combines several advantages into one system. the stronger the system, the easier it is to attract the next pharmacy THE SYSTEM DOES NOT WIN A SINGLE DELIVERY — IT STRENGTHENS ITSELF Thus, a legally fragmented market may in practice be controlled by a small number of infrastructure players."],["content/c24.html#pdf-141",160,"Stage check: ecosystem of independent pharmacies","Stage check: ecosystem of independent pharmacies A function becomes strategic when the distributor creates value for the pharmacy that a competitor cannot replace with a price list alone. POSITION LEVEL ☐ One of the suppliers ☐ Preferred supplier ☐ Operating platform ☐ Dominant ecosystem THE OWNER MUST SEE Share of the customer's purchases; customer churn; cost of credit; route density; share of pharmacies on the common IT platform / in the association; exclusive assortment; switching cost. SIGNS OF WEAKNESS The customer chooses only on price; there is no control over service; credit is not linked to customer quality; IT exists separately from the commercial model; the association creates no real economic value. TRAP Create dependency without sufficient value. Such a system generates resistance and collapses when the first strong alternative appears. HOW MARKET POWER CHANGES Control of infrastructure concentrates economic power even without owning pharmacies. WHAT NEXT? If the channel already controls replenishment, assortment, and access to the patient, how free is the manufacturer to sell its own product? Fragmented ownership does not guarantee fragmented power."],["content/c24.html#pdf-142",161,"Where is your distribution business positioned?","Where is your distribution business positioned? Which functions will still be ours when the market completes the next stage of consolidation? For each area, mark its relevance to the future market, current position, principal risk, and next management decision."],["content/c24.html#pdf-143",162,"What happens to the manufacturer when the path to the patient is already controlled by others?","What happens to the manufacturer when the path to the patient is already controlled by others? A manufacturer can create a product. But between the factory and the patient there are already other centers of control: a distributor that controls independent pharmacies; a pharmacy chain that controls the shelf and the pharmacy counter; the state, which defines the rules of access and reimbursement; the digital infrastructure through which data and orders flow. The more consolidated the channel, the fewer independent routes the manufacturer has to market. In generics, the problem becomes especially severe: manufacturing capacity may be abundant, while the number of patients and prescriptions remains limited. Being able to manufacture a medicine does not yet mean being able to sell it. FACTORY DISTRIBUTOR RETAIL PATIENT The next part begins where the factory ends. NEXT — THE MANUFACTURER BEYOND THE FACTORY"],["content/c25.html#pdf-144",163,"The manufacturer beyond the factory","The manufacturer beyond the factory Chapter 18 · Supply grows faster than demand Imagine that the same generic can be produced by ten qualified manufacturers. Do patients need ten times more treatment because of this? No. The number of patients, diagnoses, prescriptions, and treatment courses does not increase simply because new manufacturing capacity appears. Pharmaceutical manufacturing requires registration, good manufacturing practice, quality, validation, and a reliable supply chain. But once the molecule is known and exclusivity barriers have ended, many manufacturers can compete for the same limited demand. This creates a structural gap: supply can be scaled much faster than patient need. Capacity can be increased. Patient demand does not grow on command. This part of the book begins where the factory ends. The manufacturer's key question is no longer only, 'How much can we produce?' but, 'How will the product we manufacture gain access to existing demand?'"],["content/c25.html#pdf-145",164,"Capacity can be increased. Patient demand does not grow on command.","Capacity can be increased. Patient demand does not grow on command. In generics, competition is not only for the capability to manufacture. It is for a share of end demand that already exists. If total demand grows slowly, an additional share within the existing market mainly means redistribution among competing products. Additional absolute volume can also come from broader access, diagnosis, therapy adherence, new channels, or changes in therapy. Within a limited pool of demand, an additional share for one player usually means a loss of share for another."],["content/c25.html#pdf-146",165,"Competition is for access","Competition is for access The product must pass through several decisions made by others The manufacturer can increase The market cannot create on command Number of lines and shifts Additional patients Production batch size New diagnoses simply because capacity exists Number of suppliers and sites More prescriptions for the same clinical need Output volume of an SKU Unlimited consumption of one medicine After leaving the factory, the medicine still has to obtain: regulatory access; access to funded demand; access to the distributor or direct purchasing by the chain; assortment listing and presence; Availability Level at the moment of demand; prescription, recommendation, or patient choice. Manufacturing creates supply. Market access turns it into sales. OWNER LEVEL · STAGE CHECK THE MANAGER MUST SEE The size of real demand by molecule; the number of significant buyers; the share of sales by channel; dependence on a single player controlling access; Availability Level at the manufacturer and in the channel. SIGNS OF WEAKNESS Capacity grows faster than sales; large batches become inventory; success is measured by shipment into the channel; losing one chain closes off a significant share of the market. TRAP Assume that a good product, low cost, and spare capacity automatically create market share. WHAT NEXT? Who controls the path from finished product to the patient?"],["content/c26.html#pdf-147",166,"Who controls market access?","Who controls market access? Assortment listing, private label, and access to the shelf Who does a manufacturer usually consider its main competitor? Another manufacturer with the same molecule, a similar price, and comparable quality. But in a consolidated market, the strongest constraint may not be the neighboring factory. It may be the buyer who decides whether the patient sees your product at all. In the extreme configuration, a manufacturer may have dozens of competitors — and only a few buyers who actually determine market access. FOUR LEVELS OF ACCESS LEVEL WHAT IT MEANS 1. Regulatory The product is registered and may legally be marketed 2. Financial The patient, the state, or another payer can afford the treatment 3. Retail The product is listed, distributed, and actually available in the pharmacy 4. Access to recommendation The patient sees or receives a relevant alternative Assortment listing is not Availability Level. Availability is not a recommendation. A recommendation is not yet a purchase."],["content/c26.html#pdf-148",167,"Who controls market access?","For the manufacturer, each level requires a separate mechanism. You can win registration and reimbursement yet fail to enter a chain's centralized assortment. You can enter the chain's assortment but remain present in only some pharmacies. You can be in the pharmacy but still not be visible to the patient. Market access cannot be treated as a single checkbox. It is a chain of decisions owned by different players."],["content/c26.html#pdf-149",168,"When the buyer becomes an economic competitor","When the buyer becomes an economic competitor A strong pharmacy chain earns additional margin from its private label. It controls the assortment, shelf, data, promotions, and staff incentives. Private label is not a problem in itself. For the chain, it is a rational tool for profit and differentiation. But for an external manufacturer, it changes the structure of negotiations: the chain compares its offer not only with another brand, but also with a product whose economics partly accrue to the chain itself. Fragmented retail Consolidated retail Many buyers and local decisions A few centralized purchasing centers Rejection by one pharmacy has almost no effect on access Failure in one chain can close off a significant share of demand No single buyer controls the entire path The chain increases its power through assortment, data, and private label The fewer routes to the patient, the greater the power of whoever controls those routes. For the manufacturer, this means that retail concentration must be viewed not only as a channel story, but also as a change in its own future negotiating position."],["content/c26.html#pdf-150",169,"Do not wait until consolidation is complete","Do not wait until consolidation is complete While the market is fragmented, rejection by one pharmacy or one group does not close off a significant share of demand for the manufacturer. Many independent routes to market remain. As chains grow larger, the situation changes: many independent buyers → a few centralized purchasing centers → every rejection becomes more costly → channel bargaining power rises → for the manufacturer it becomes harder to secure inclusion in the chain's assortment and preserve product economics. Therefore, at the early stages of consolidation, a manufacturer can lawfully participate in public discussion of market rules and support a competitive environment that prevents excessive concentration from closing independent routes to the patient. The argument should be broader than one company's commercial interest: competition, patient access, and supply resilience. Once the market is already concentrated, the manufacturer negotiates with a strong channel. While the market is still consolidating, it can still participate in shaping the rules under which that channel will become strong. ACCESS CAN BE RESTRICTED WITHOUT A DIRECT REFUSAL Assortment listing may be agreed, but the product may appear in only some pharmacies; Availability Level may remain low; the product may not enter priority promotions; the assortment plan may be updated slowly; staff may be incentivized to offer other products. The manufacturer may win the right to be on the shelf and still lose the right to be chosen. STAGE CHECK THE MANAGER MUST SEE Market share by key purchasing groups; presence; Availability Level by chain; retail sell-out; share of dependent volume; cost of access to each chain. TRAP Treating inclusion in the chain's centralized assortment as the final victory and failing to measure what happens in each pharmacy and at the pharmacy counter. WHAT NEXT? Build a portfolio of independent routes to the patient."],["content/c27.html#pdf-151",170,"Routes to the patient","Routes to the patient Government rules, created demand, agreements, risk, and data What should a manufacturer do if one strong player can block a significant part of the path to the patient? The weakest option is to keep trying to open the same door with ever larger discounts. A stronger approach is to build several independent routes. Route What it changes 1. Government rules Preserve or open access to therapy 2. Created demand Creates demand before the patient enters the pharmacy 3. Agreement with the channel Links commercial terms to actual execution 4. Data Show what happens after the product is shipped Market access is not one door. It is a system of routes. ROUTE 1 · GOVERNMENT Limits on excessive concentration preserve several independent routes; mandatory assortment rules affect physical availability; reimbursement affects the patient's ability to pay for treatment; substitution rules and transparency of alternatives affect choice. Government can open a route to therapy without guaranteeing a route to a particular brand. ROUTE 2 · CREATE DEMAND BEFORE THE PHARMACY If the patient comes in and asks for a specific product, a stockout becomes a problem not only for the manufacturer. Where permitted, demand can be created through advertising, digital channels, brand awareness, work with physicians, and scientific and medical communication. The more demand is created before the patient reaches the pharmacy counter, the less freedom pharmacy staff have to ignore the product entirely."],["content/c27.html#pdf-152",171,"ROUTE 3 · GET INTO THE ASSORTMENT—AND ENSURE EXECUTION","ROUTE 3 · GET INTO THE ASSORTMENT—AND ENSURE EXECUTION Commercial terms can be tied to what the chain actually delivers: the number of pharmacies carrying the product, the target Availability Level, the agreed volume, promotional activities, timelines, and execution standards. Agreed inclusion in the assortment is only the beginning. The main question is: what will happen tomorrow morning in five hundred pharmacies? If the chain does not want to finance the risk of a new product, the manufacturer can take part of that risk back: provide the product on consignment, allow payment as the product is sold, or agree on returns of unsold inventory. Associations of independent pharmacies can be another route if large chains are too expensive or closed to entry. Scope of applicability. Such terms are applicable only where permitted by law and internal compliance rules; restrictions may be substantially tighter for prescription and reimbursed medicines."],["content/c27.html#pdf-153",172,"Data must return to the factory","Data must return to the factory How much product did the manufacturer ship to the chain? That still does not answer how much was actually sold through retail and reached the patient. Shipment shows product entering the chain. Retail sell-out shows confirmed demand. SIGNAL WHAT IT MAY MEAN Sales are high, then inventory falls to zero Demand exists, replenishment is not keeping up Inventory is high, sales are weak The location is overstocked Sold in some pharmacies, out of stock in others Presence problem Shipments are high, end sales are weak The channel bought the product, but patient demand was not confirmed PRACTICAL TECHNIQUE In my practice, a simple mechanism worked: an additional commercial term was included in the draft agreement from the outset, for example an extra 1–2% discount in exchange for regular provision of agreed sales and inventory data. Sometimes separate negotiations about data were not needed at all after that. It is not always necessary to persuade a partner to provide data. Sometimes it is enough to create an economic incentive that makes the partner want to meet the condition. The 1–2% figure is the author's practical example, not a universal norm. Data sharing must be lawful and must not require unnecessary patient personal data. No marketing can sell a product that the manufacturer failed to produce on time."],["content/c27.html#pdf-154",173,"The manufacturer after the factory","The manufacturer after the factory Seven functions that turn manufactured product into patient access STEP FUNCTION MANAGEMENT QUESTION 1 See limited demand What real demand exists, and who already controls it? 2 Ensure lawful access What rules on mandatory access, reimbursement, and substitution apply? 3 Create demand before the pharmacy Can patient demand or physician prescribing be created before the patient enters the pharmacy? 4 Activate the channel What commitments on assortment listing, availability, and execution are needed? 5 Take on part of the channel's risk Is inventory financing, consignment, or an agreed return mechanism needed? 6 Obtain end-sales data What is actually selling, where is the product out of stock, and where is there excess inventory? 7 Feed demand back into manufacturing How do retail sell-out data change batch timing and the manufacturer's Availability Level? The manufacturer begins where the factory ends. Not every mechanism applies in every market. The manager's task is not to collect the maximum number of tools, but to remove dangerous dependency on one route and one player controlling access."],["content/c27.html#pdf-155",174,"Through which routes does your product actually reach the patient?","Through which routes does your product actually reach the patient? Do not ask only, 'Do we have registration and an agreement with the chain?' Check the entire access chain. FUNCTION CURRENT POSITION MAIN GAP / NEXT DECISION Limited demand □ We do not quantify it □ We see market size □ We manage share by route Government access □ No strategy □ Isolated actions □ Systematic access management Patient / physician demand □ None □ Separate campaigns □ Demand is linked to availability Retail execution □ Listed in assortment □ Presence □ Availability and end sales are controlled Risk sharing □ The channel finances everything □ Separate terms □ Risk is managed by SKU End-sales data □ Shipments only □ Periodic data □ Daily by SKU × pharmacy Manufacturing response □ Planning is separate □ Forecast reflects the market □ End sales change production timing A company can become better and better at manufacturing a product that gets through to the patient worse and worse. THE CHAIN IS COMPLETE. NOW — THE LAYER ABOVE IT We have covered the three main commercial links in the pharmaceutical market: the pharmacy chain, the distributor, and the manufacturer. Now we need to move above the chain. Who controls the infrastructure and the rules on which the entire chain operates? IT penetrates pharmacy chains and distribution particularly deeply: ordering, inventory, replenishment, integrations, and data. Government is especially visible from the manufacturer's side: registration, reimbursement, mandatory access, competition, and concentration — but its decisions change the entire chain. We have completed the chain 'pharmacy chain — distributor — manufacturer.' Now we look at the forces that define the infrastructure and rules of the game for all three. Next part: IT AND GOVERNMENT"],["content/c28.html#pdf-156",175,"The layer above the chain","The layer above the chain Chapter 21 · IT as market infrastructure We have completed the three main commercial links in the pharmaceutical market: the pharmacy chain, the distributor, and the manufacturer. Now we need to move one level higher. Some forces are not the next link in the chain; they cut across several links at once. We have completed the chain. Now we look at the forces that define infrastructure and rules for all its links. The first layer is IT. For a pharmacy chain and a distributor, digital infrastructure determines who sees the data, who can connect a new technology, how quickly the company can implement a specialized solution, and whether an advantage it creates can remain its own advantage. A company can win within the market and still depend on whoever controls the digital entry point into its business. CHAPTER 21 ERP/POS, vendor dependency, integrations, and specialized technologies. What legal and contractual rights does the company have to its operational data? The legal regime depends on the type of data and the jurisdiction. But even when such rights exist, another question remains: who controls access in practice?"],["content/c28.html#pdf-157",176,"Who controls the digital entry point?","Who controls the digital entry point? WHO CONTROLS THE DIGITAL ENTRY POINT? The ERP/POS system begins to determine not only transactions, but also which technologies can be integrated into the business's operating environment at all. When platform dependency is high and switching is expensive, control of the interface can become a source of market power."],["content/c28.html#pdf-158",177,"Why the choice of ERP/POS for a pharmacy is often narrow","Why the choice of ERP/POS for a pharmacy is often narrow Pharmaceutical retail has requirements that ordinary retail does not In many markets, a pharmacy system needs batch tracking, expiration dates, traceability, prescriptions, reimbursement, fiscal requirements, and other industry-specific functions. Without them, the system may be practically unusable. Function Why it increases dependency Batch tracking and expiration dates Not every general-purpose retail system supports the required depth and workflow Prescriptions and reimbursement Local integrations and compliance with market-specific rules are required Regulatory traceability Changes in legislation require continuous adaptation of the core system Sales, inventory, and accounting Replacing the ERP/POS system affects almost the entire operating model at once As a result, only a few mature vendors may remain in a country — and sometimes effectively one dominant player. The narrower the choice, the higher the cost of switching systems and the stronger the vendor's negotiating position. Specialization first makes an ERP/POS system valuable. Concentration can make it practically irreplaceable."],["content/c28.html#pdf-159",178,"Formal freedom of choice does not always mean practical freedom","Formal freedom of choice does not always mean practical freedom After several years, the ERP/POS system is already linked to sales, purchasing, inventory, accounting, fiscal processes, prescriptions, reimbursement, and historical data. It can be replaced. But the cost, risk, and duration of the project can become so high that switching is no longer a routine vendor decision. If the vendor also restricts the programming interface or delays integrations, the business faces two uncomfortable questions: Can we actually choose the best specialized tool? If we create an advantage, how long will it remain ours alone? A market can be open in ownership and closed in software. In this configuration, the core system controls more than record-keeping. It affects the speed at which innovation reaches the business. That is why digital architecture becomes a strategic owner-level issue, not merely a convenience for the IT department."],["content/c28.html#pdf-160",179,"When one interface becomes the entry point to almost the entire market","When one interface becomes the entry point to almost the entire market Legal and contractual rights to data do not yet guarantee practical control over it. If exports, the API (application programming interface for data exchange), integrations, and migration depend on the platform, then the platform determines how freely the business can use its data. Control begins with the capability to receive, transmit, migrate, and use data without an intermediary's permission."],["content/c28.html#pdf-161",180,"The chain creates an idea. The platform turns it into a standard.","The chain creates an idea. The platform turns it into a standard. For a software platform vendor, reusing a standard function is normal product economics. For the chain, it is important to define in advance the rights to data, developed functionality, and its reuse. The question is no longer only what data rights are secured for the chain, but also who controls the advantage that has been created. THREE QUESTIONS FOR THE CONTRACT What data rights are contractually secured for the company? Who owns the functionality created and the right to reuse it? Who controls the API, integrations, and operational access?"],["content/c28.html#pdf-162",181,"Neither monolith nor chaos","Neither monolith nor chaos Changing ERP/POS does not mean changing an architectural mistake NEITHER MONOLITH NOR CHAOS Architecture separates the standard foundation from the areas of real advantage. REPLENISHMENT ASSORTMENT REDISTRIBUTION ANALYTICS CORE ERP/POS TRANSACTIONS · RECORD-KEEPING · MANDATORY PROCESSES A large chain can switch from one ERP/POS system to another and remain in the same problem for years if it keeps believing that one system should be the best expert in every business function at once. The alternative to a monolith is not an uncontrolled 'zoo' of systems. What is needed is architecture: a standard foundation, managed integrations, and specialization where it creates a measurable economic effect. Standardize where standardization is useful. Specialize where measurable advantage is created."],["content/c28.html#b063-it-183",182,"When IT becomes a hidden constraint on growth","CHAPTER 21 · INTERNAL DEPENDENCY When IT becomes a hidden constraint on growth External lock-in is visible in contracts and interfaces. Internal lock-in arises when the capability to make a change depends on one person, an overloaded function, or access that cannot be bypassed. 1 · ON-DEMAND SERVICE ERP/POS works. Changes are made by the vendor or an external specialist. Risk: the business has grown, but the interaction model has remained the same. 2 · BOTTLENECK One person or a small team manages ERP, infrastructure, integrations, incidents, and projects. Consequence: the speed of strategy is determined by IT's available time. 3 · HIDDEN CENTER OF CONTROL Critical access rights, knowledge, and integration logic are concentrated in one point. Formally, the owner makes the decision. The practical right to implement the change sits somewhere else. FIELD SIGNAL In a chain of more than 100 pharmacies, the owner supported a new project. After the meeting, the IT manager told the external team: no changes would be made without his approval. The formal decision belonged to the owner; the practical ability to implement it was elsewhere. FORMAL DECISION RIGHT ≠ PRACTICAL CONTROL When they diverge, the company develops a hidden center of control."],["content/c28.html#b063-it-184",183,"Good IT increases company speed","CHAPTER 21 · IT AS A CAPABILITY Good IT increases company speed Department size is secondary. For the owner, what matters is not the volume of IT activity, but whether the next change becomes faster, safer, and less dependent on specific people. 1 · THE CORE IS STABLE ERP/POS, network infrastructure, access rights, and critical integrations operate predictably. 2 · NEW SOLUTIONS CAN BE CONNECTED Documented interfaces and access rights make it possible to connect something new without rebuilding the core. 3 · CHANGE HAS RESOURCES New tasks receive priority, resources, and a deadline. Strategy does not wait for an open slot. 4 · KNOWLEDGE STAYS IN THE COMPANY Knowledge is not locked in one person: backup coverage and documentation exist. COST OF THE NEXT CHANGE TIME ↓ RISK ↓ DEPENDENCE ON PEOPLE ↓ CAPABILITY TO CHANGE ↑ THE ROLE OF IT IN CHANGE The business owns the outcome and the meaning of the data. IT provides technical feasibility, architecture, integrations, and security. In a cross-functional project, IT is a key participant, but not the sole owner of the change. IT should reduce the cost of the next change — in time, risk, and dependence on specific people."],["content/c28.html#pdf-163",184,"Digital freedom of the business","Digital freedom of the business Owner level · stage check IT architecture becomes a source of market power when a company controls its own data, understands the cost of switching the core system, and can connect specialized solutions without losing control of its data. DATA CONTROL Only through the vendor Exports are available Managed interfaces and a data layer INTEGRATION SPEED Years / impossible Manual projects Standard connection COST OF ACCESS Reasonable Significant, but justified Formally available, but the price blocks access COST OF SWITCHING ERP/POS We do not know Partially assessed A realistic migration scenario exists SPECIALIZATION Everything inside ERP/POS Separate systems exist Specialization creates advantage ARCHITECTURAL RISK Not managed Depends on people Rules exist for integrations, security, and accountability COMPETITIVE ADVANTAGE Ideas quickly become standard Partially protected We understand what should remain proprietary Digital freedom is determined not only by whether data can be obtained or another system connected. What matters is whether this can be done technically, quickly, and at an economically reasonable cost. The next layer is government."],["content/c29.html#pdf-164",185,"Yes. If the government changes the rules faster than the company can adapt.","Ownership, reimbursement, mandatory access, and political risk Can you build the strongest pharmacy chain, the best warehouse, and an efficient vertical—and still lose your strategic position? Yes. If the government changes the rules faster than the company can adapt. Government is not merely an external backdrop to the market. It defines the boundaries within which evolution is possible at all. Government can permit consolidation or stop it; define rules for ownership, reimbursement, mandatory access, and substitution; and influence prices and licensing. In an extreme scenario, the issue may extend to the security of ownership itself. FOUR ROLES OF GOVERNMENT Mode What changes Enabling market growth Consolidation and vertical integration gain more room Preserving the structure Ownership / location constraints maintain fragmentation Regulating access Reimbursement, mandatory assortment, substitution, and prices redistribute power Interventionist / high-risk Political power may outweigh economic efficiency"],["content/c29.html#pdf-165",186,"The same capabilities. Different rules. Different power.","A market may be liberal on ownership and strict on prices—or preserve independent pharmacies while deeply regulating funded demand. Strategy must therefore fit not only the current law, but also the direction in which the government model is moving."],["content/c29.html#pdf-166",187,"One law can constrain one player and protect another","One law can constrain one player and protect another The economics of a large pharmacy chain push the market toward scale: warehouse operations, direct purchasing, automation, capital productivity, and acquisitions reinforce one another. But ownership constraints, licensing, and merger control can alter the trajectory. For a chain, a constraint may close the path to consolidation. For a distributor, it may preserve a fragmented customer base. For a manufacturer, it may preserve more independent routes to the shelf. ACCESS TO MEDICINES Mechanism What question does it answer? Mandatory assortment / access What must a pharmacy stock or provide to the patient? Reimbursement What part of treatment is paid for by the government or another payer? Substitution rules What alternatives may or must be offered? Price and alternative transparency What does the patient see at the pharmacy counter? Mandatory assortment opens access. Reimbursement opens payment. These are different doors. The specific effect depends on the country, the International Nonproprietary Name (INN), substitution rules, and the financing model. But the mechanism is the same: government can redistribute power among the chain, manufacturer, physician, patient, and payer."],["content/c29.html#pdf-167",188,"Do not copy another country's strategy without understanding its rules","Do not copy another country's strategy without understanding its rules Imagine two identical companies. The same capital, technology, and management quality. In one market, the company can rapidly build a chain, integrate distribution, and develop private label. In another, the same model may be impossible or economically weak. There is no universally correct strategy. There is a strategy that fits a specific configuration of rules and power. The same applies to a distributor: an ecosystem of independent pharmacies may be sustainable where independence is protected, but in a more open market the best participants may be acquired by a large chain. Regulatory analysis must answer not only “What is permitted today?” but at least three more questions: 1 What political and economic logic underlies the current rules? 2 In what direction might they change? 3 Which part of our strategy would break first under such a change? A company's strategy must fit not only the current law, but also the trajectory of the government model."],["content/c29.html#pdf-168",189,"When regulation becomes an ownership risk","When regulation becomes an ownership risk In my practice, I have observed markets where intervention by the government or politically connected players went far beyond ordinary industry regulation. In some cases, large vertically integrated groups lost control of key assets. Commercial power matters only as long as ownership rights are protected and the rules are observed. Sometimes the strongest player in the market is not a chain, distributor, or manufacturer. It is the government. AREA WHAT TO CHECK WHAT MAY CHANGE Market structure acquisitions and vertical integration? Changes in ownership, licensing, or merger control Access What is mandatory, reimbursed, or subject to substitution? New access / financing / transparency rules Economics What does the government control in pricing, margins, advertising, and financing? Pricing, payer, or tax reform Protection of ownership How predictable are rule enforcement and asset protection? Political pressure or forced restructuring"],["content/c30.html#pdf-169",190,"What must remain under our control so that this advantage does not disappear as we scale?","How to scale the advantage without losing it in the market's next transition By this point, management should already know two things: what competitive advantage has been proven today and what measurable economic result it creates. Now a third question appears: What must remain under our control so that this advantage does not disappear as we scale? Revenue, number of pharmacies, and warehouse size show the position already achieved. Migration of functions shows the process that creates that position. When a pharmacy chain builds its own warehouse and begins buying directly, the shift in power starts before the distributor sees a decline in revenue. When a distributor creates an ERP/POS system, credit, and an alliance of independent pharmacies, pharmacy dependence grows before it becomes visible in the ownership structure. When government prepares new rules, business economics may change even before they formally take effect. The migration of functions often becomes one of the early signals of the market's future structure. Four rules for the next move: 1 follow functions, not labels; 2 distinguish influence from ownership; 3 treat data and government as independent centers of power; 4 and prove the mechanism of advantage before aggressive scaling. There is no universal next step. There is the next step for this market—at this stage of its evolution."],["content/c30.html#pdf-170",191,"Where is control moving?","Where is control moving? MOVEMENT OF FUNCTIONS = MOVEMENT OF POWER Transfer of functions and external rules are different mechanisms. They must be viewed separately. MIGRATION OF FUNCTIONS DISTRIBUTOR → PHARMACY CHAIN: warehouse / replenishment DISTRIBUTOR → PHARMACY CHAIN: credit / data DISTRIBUTOR → MANUFACTURER: patient access WHOEVER TAKES OVER THE FUNCTION STRENGTHENS THEIR POSITION. RULES DEFINE THE BOUNDARIES. Watch more than market share. Watch who gains the warehouse, credit, data, and patient access; who controls integration; and who shapes the rules. A new dependency appears before a new leader becomes obvious."],["content/c31.html#std12-p187",192,"The Last Bottleneck","The Last Bottleneck CHAPTER 24 · MANAGEMENT ATTENTION Management attention as a growth constraint What is the scarcest resource in a growing company? Money? People? Technology? For many companies, the answer is less obvious: the time of key executives. Money can be raised. Longer payment terms can be negotiated with a supplier. You can find a partner, hire a specialist, take out a loan, or change the capital structure. But the hours of people who understand the business, can make key decisions, and move the company forward cannot be increased at the same rate. A company can exhaust management attention before it exhausts money. This constraint rarely looks like a strategic problem. It usually looks like ordinary work: calls, meetings, urgent exceptions, and daily decisions that rise to the top again and again. WHAT THE ATTENTION “TAX” LOOKS LIKE CALLS MEETINGS EXCEPTIONS DAILY DECISIONS"],["content/c31.html#std12-p188",193,"Automating a process is not enough","CHAPTER 24 · AUTOMATION Automating a process is not enough Recurring management decisions must be removed Automation itself guarantees nothing. You can automate a weak process—and problems will still rise to the top. You can generate a bad order faster, automatically create excess inventory, or build a system with hundreds of alerts, each of which still has to be reviewed by a manager. OPERATION AUTOMATION Performs the action faster, but may leave the same flow of exceptions and decisions moving upward. THE RIGHT AUTOMATION Formalizes recurring decisions, handles standard situations itself, distinguishes normal conditions from genuine exceptions, and escalates only what truly requires a manager's decision. WHAT CUSTOMERS SAW Several clients told me that the value of the project was not only high Availability Level, lower inventory, and automatic replenishment. Key people in the company stopped constantly discussing the same product problems: stockouts, excess inventory, expiration dates, transfers, and warehouse exceptions. The system freed not only capital tied up in inventory. It freed the time of the people who must create the company's next stage. IN 2-3 YEARS After automation, does the number of recurring decisions requiring management attention decrease?"],["content/c31.html#std12-p189",194,"Operations move upward","If not, the operation has been automated—but the management problem has not. One of the owners responsible for business development described the time freed up for key people as one of the project's largest effects. CHAPTER 24 · MANAGEMENT ATTENTION TAX Operations move upward The main cost of a recurring problem is not only money, but also the attention it consumes WHERE DOES MANAGEMENT ATTENTION GO? Recurring exceptions can quietly turn top management into a manual dispatch center. WEAK SYSTEM TOP-LEVEL ATTENTION OWNER DIRECTOR MANAGER EMPLOYEE OUT OF STOCK · EXCESS · EXPIRATION · TRANSFER · SUPPLIER The same issues keep rising to the top. MATURE SYSTEM ONLY GENUINE EXCEPTIONS AUTOMATION Management sees only what genuinely requires a decision."],["content/c31.html#std12-p190",195,"What happens if the business doubles?","THE RIGHT AUTOMATION REDUCES THE FLOW OF RECURRING DECISIONS TO THE TOP A mature system does not have to eliminate every exception. It must reduce the number of recurring decisions that require top-level attention. If an executive solves the same class of problems again every month, that is not a system but an expensive manual mechanism compensating for its weakness. CHAPTER 24 · MANAGEMENT SCALABILITY What happens if the business doubles? A test that quickly shows whether the system is scaling—or only the operating workload NON-SCALABLE MODEL BUSINESS ×2 QUESTIONS TO THE TOP ×2 THE MANAGER BECOMES THE BOTTLENECK SCALABLE MODEL BUSINESS ×2 RECURRING ESCALATIONS GROW MORE SLOWLY TOP MANAGEMENT FOCUSES ON DEVELOPMENT If the business doubles and the number of issues that must reach the owner also doubles, the system is not scaling—the operating workload is."],["content/c31.html#std12-p191",196,"Freeing time is not enough—it must be reinvested","Freeing time is not enough—it must be reinvested FEWER EXCEPTIONS FREE ATTENTION DEVELOPMENT NEW CAPABILITY GROWTH CHAPTER 24 · OWNER AS A CONSTRAINT When the owner's strength becomes a constraint Freeing management attention is not enough. The next question is whether the company can make decisions without everything converging again on one person. In a small company, a strong owner is often an advantage: the owner sees the whole picture and makes decisions quickly. But business scale grows faster than one person's capacity. FIELD CASE · ONE DECISION CENTER In one large pharmaceutical group, the owner managed a pharmacy chain of more than one hundred locations, manufacturing, and several other businesses. During our meeting, she was approving orders and answering operational questions at the same time. She suggested we continue the presentation: “I can hear everything.” Assistants were around her, but decisions still went through her. The company had grown, while the management system remained built around the capacity of one person. 100+ LOCATIONS AND SEVERAL BUSINESSES 1 CENTER FOR KEY DECISIONS"],["content/c31.html#std12-p192",197,"When an advantage becomes a bottleneck","SCALE GREW · DECISION ARCHITECTURE DID NOT CHANGE The constraint was already visible: a significant share of the owner's attention was going not to choosing the next move, but to a stream of daily decisions. CHAPTER 24 · OWNER AS A CONSTRAINT When an advantage becomes a bottleneck Scale grows—the decision center remains one EMPLOYEES issues, tasks CUSTOMERS requests, terms SUPPLIERS deadlines, prices URGENT ISSUES failures, risks INVENTORY shortages, logistics APPROVALS documents, decisions CONTROL KPIs, quality ECONOMICS payments, capital Assistants can increase the number of assignments completed. But if only the owner determines what to do, in what sequence, and how to resolve every exception, the company's management capability does not increase."],["content/c31.html#std12-p193",198,"People in the structure do not yet make a team","When critical decisions, control, and communication converge on one person, growth creates a new constraint—overloading this concentration point for management attention. CHAPTER 24 · MANAGEMENT TEAM People in the structure do not yet make a team A team is not defined by the number of positions. The main question is whether people can take on a task, coordinate with one another, and carry a change through to results without constant manual management by the owner. THREE MODELS—THREE DIFFERENT SPEEDS ASSISTANTS wait for instructions escalate issues upward the owner holds responsibility for the result FORMAL TEAM roles are assigned decisions are delayed accountability is blurred MANAGEMENT TEAM one owner of the result authority is in place issues are resolved In one company, managers had been appointed, but issues stalled and departments explained problems by pointing to one another's actions. Delegation existed in the structure, but it did not create shared accountability or speed. QUICK TEST: IS THIS ALREADY A TEAM OR NOT YET? Work does not stop if the owner temporarily steps out of the operating loop. Decisions are made according to a shared logic, not only through personal approval from the top. People understand not only their own tasks, but also the overall result the system is working toward."],["content/c31.html#std12-p194",199,"A team turns a decision into a result","Errors and disruptions do not break collaboration: the team can adapt and keep moving. Key functions do not depend on one person without a replacement, support, or a clear process. If most of these points are not yet true, this is not yet a team as a management system, but a group of people within an organizational structure. CHAPTER 24 · MANAGEMENT TEAM A team turns a decision into a result We already encountered this pharmacy chain in the chapter on working capital. There, the financial effect mattered. Here, another element of the same growth matters. The owner set the direction and made the key decision, while a management team of about five people resolved implementation issues between checkpoints. The chain later grew from roughly 70 to 1,000 pharmacies. Such growth cannot be explained by one factor. But the team made it possible not to return every decision to the owner and to turn the chosen direction into working changes faster. NEXT LEVEL · CHANGE TEAM In a more mature model, a new capability is created by a temporary cross-functional change team. It brings together the necessary departments and external specialists, tests the solution, and then hands it over to the operations team that will use the new capability continuously. DIRECTION CHANGE TEAM RECEIVING OPERATIONS TEAM"],["content/c31.html#std12-p195",200,"Where does management time go?","The owner sets the direction. The team turns it into a result. If every decision stops when the owner is absent, the company has employees, but not yet a management team. CHAPTER 24 · DESK CHECK Where does management time go? Complete this based on the last 4-8 weeks, not from memory 1 · RECURRING ISSUE HOW OFTEN? HOW HIGH DOES IT ESCALATE? HOURS OF KEY PEOPLE WHY DOESN'T THE SYSTEM SOLVE IT ITSELF? WHAT SHOULD CHANGE? 2 · RECURRING ISSUE If these hours are freed up for management, what exactly will we invest them in? NEW MARKET · FINANCING · ACQUISITION · NEW CAPABILITY · TECHNOLOGY · NEW FORMAT · NEXT LAYER OF LEADERS Do not write “work on development.” Name the specific decision. Otherwise, the freed-up time will dissolve into operations again."],["content/c32.html#std12-p196",201,"The Final Growth Check","The Final Growth Check FINALE In this book, we discussed availability, inventory turnover, capital, assortment, purchasing, warehouses, distribution, manufacturer access to the patient, IT, and government. Each next capability requires more than the right decision. It requires management attention and a team capable of turning that decision into a working system. Do the people who must build the company's next stage have the time, authority, and capability to carry the change through to results? A strong company is not one where the owner and the best executives heroically solve more problems. A strong company is one where recurring decisions stay within the system, while top-level attention is directed toward what the company does not yet know how to do. The main output of a mature management system is attention and accountability directed toward building the next capability. What is our proven competitive advantage? What measurable economic result does it create? If we want to grow, what constrains the next scale-up? What capability must we build next? Do the key people have the time to build it?"],["content/c32.html#std12-p197",202,"Four actions after the check","FINALE · NEXT MOVE Four actions after the check These are not four independent recommendations, but a sequence within one management cycle. Create an advantage. The capability must be visible to the customer and difficult for a competitor to replicate. Prove the economic result. Show the effect in sales, margin, capital, resilience, or customer outcomes. If growth is chosen, prove the scaling mechanism. Verify that the advantage survives growth and does not create a new constraint faster than the business grows. Free up attention for the next capability. The system should give key people back time for development, not just for handling current exceptions. Growth becomes manageable when advantage, economic result, scale, and attention work as one system."],["content/c33.html#pdf-179",203,"Where is your market now?","Where is your market now? Diagnosis of competitive advantage, configuration of power, and the next strategic move We have examined the pharmacy chain, distributor, manufacturer, IT infrastructure, and government. But none of these players is always and everywhere the strongest. In one market, power is concentrated among several pharmacy chains. In another, it belongs to a distributor that has built infrastructure around independent pharmacies. In a third, critical access is controlled by the government or a digital platform. Market power often concentrates where a company controls something important to the system and difficult for other participants to replace. 1. What is our real competitive advantage? The difference must be visible not only inside the company, but also relative to the strongest alternative. 2. What measurable economic result does it create? Sales, margin, capital, resilience, risk, or customer outcomes. 3. Who or what can constrain this advantage? Rules, access, data, ownership, channel dependence, or the next migration of a function. Only then does it make sense to choose the next strategic move. Three questions frame the entire practicum: advantage → result → constraint."],["content/c33.html#pdf-180",204,"What could devalue all the company's other capabilities?","What could devalue all the company's other capabilities? Executive Desk Check—IT and Government We have already seen how a company gains advantages through availability, inventory turnover, automation, warehousing, purchasing power, assortment, scale, infrastructure, and patient access. But above them are constraints of another level. CONSTRAINT CURRENT POSITION NEXT DECISION DATA CONTROL Supplier decides / Exports available / Managed access layer INTEGRATION SPEED Years / impossible / Manual projects / Standard connection TECHNOLOGY DIFFERENTIATION Standard ERP/POS only / Specialized systems / Measurable advantage REGULATORY TRAJECTORY Not tracked / Legal monitoring / Scenario-based strategy MARKET ACCESS DEPENDENCY One mechanism / payer / Partly diversified / Multiple routes PROTECTION OF OWNERSHIP Not assessed / Risk understood / Structure and contingency scenarios prepared Capability answers the question “what can we do?” Constraint asks, “will the environment allow us to turn that capability into power?” The next question: what configuration of power exists here and now?"],["content/c33.html#pdf-181",205,"Competitive advantage must create a measurable economic result","Competitive advantage must create a measurable economic result Growth makes sense only when the advantage is proven and withstands scale. A capability by itself is not yet a competitive advantage. If a competitor can do the same thing with a comparable result, it is a necessary level of play, not an advantage. GROWTH CREATES A NEW CONSTRAINT. THE NEXT CAPABILITY REMOVES IT."],["content/c33.html#pdf-182",206,"Two proofs of advantage—and a separate growth check","Two proofs of advantage—and a separate growth check The three checks are best read separately: differentiation, economic result, and the capability to withstand the chosen scale. WE REALLY DO THIS BETTER THAN OUR COMPETITORS. Not an internal belief, but a measurable difference: higher availability, lower inventory at the same Service Level, faster replenishment, better purchasing terms, lower cost to serve, or faster technology implementation. THE ADVANTAGE CREATES A MEASURABLE RESULT. More sales, higher margin or return on capital, less capital and lower costs, higher customer retention, lower risk, or greater resilience. IF GROWTH IS CHOSEN—WILL THE ADVANTAGE WITHSTAND SCALE? The expansion mechanism must be clear: capital for new locations, margin for development, customer base, scaling speed, or reduced dependence on a single route. IF GROWTH IS CHOSEN—WILL THE ADVANTAGE WITHSTAND SCALE? An advantage can be real even without growth. Growth is a separate strategic decision. If growth is chosen, the advantage must withstand scale: a manual service may disappear when the business doubles. If part of the advantage emerges only at scale, first test the basic unit economics and the mechanism for improving it."],["content/c33.html#pdf-183",207,"Start by choosing three advantages","Start by choosing three advantages Do not fill in everything. Choose three differences that can genuinely change your company's economics. Our advantage · Where are we better than the market? · What proves it? · Economic effect · How do you use it / what will change? WHERE DIFFERENT PARTICIPANTS SHOULD START PHARMACY CHAIN availability · inventory · assortment · scale DISTRIBUTOR service · capital · logistics · ecosystem MANUFACTURER market access · route to the patient · channel dependence EVERYONE data · integrations · regulation · control of functions The advantage must produce a measurable economic result. Growth is one possible next move."],["content/c33.html#pdf-184",208,"Three tests that are best viewed together","Three tests that are best viewed together These questions are connected: high service without capital, a cash cycle without growth, and a broad assortment without customers do not create a sustainable advantage. CHAIN / DISTRIBUTOR Availability Level + inventory What is our Availability Level—and that of the strongest competitor? How many days of inventory do we and they require at a comparable Service Level? CHECK: lower inventory at the same or better Service Level = capital released for growth. CHAIN / DISTRIBUTOR What is our Availability Level—and that of the strongest competitor? How many days of inventory do we and they require at a comparable Service Level? CHECK: lower inventory at the same or better Service Level = capital released for growth. EVERYONE Cash cycle Look beyond inventory turnover. Own-financing days = days of inventory + days waiting for cash − supplier payment terms. MAIN QUESTION: if sales grow by 30%, how much additional working capital will be required? EVERYONE Look beyond inventory turnover. Own-financing days = days of inventory + days waiting for cash − supplier payment terms. MAIN QUESTION: if sales grow by 30%, how much additional working capital will be required? CHAIN / DISTRIBUTOR Assortment and customer patience time What need can the customer meet immediately, within 2–4 hours, tomorrow, in 3–4 days, or in two weeks? Virtual assortment works only within the customer's time horizon. CHECK: do more customers come to us because the market considers our assortment more reliable? What need can the customer meet immediately, within 2–4 hours, tomorrow, in 3–4 days, or in two weeks? Virtual assortment works only within the customer's time horizon. CHECK: do more customers come to us because the market considers our assortment more reliable? ONE COMMON QUESTION: does our operating model allow us to keep growing without a disproportionate increase in costs and capital?"],["content/c33.html#pdf-185",209,"When an advantage becomes a condition for staying in the game","When an advantage becomes a condition for staying in the game One owner of a large pharmacy chain asked me a very precise question: “You work with many of my competitors. We have all improved availability and inventory turnover. If they get the same thing—where is my competitive advantage?” Me “Look at a particular intersection. There are three or four pharmacies there today. All of them work with us.” OWNER “Exactly. All of them.” “Yes. But there used to be other pharmacies there too. They are gone now. If you had not changed your inventory management system along with the strong players—are you sure your pharmacy would still be at that intersection today?” He thought for a moment. Then he said, “Yes. That is very valuable.” Yesterday, a new capability helped you win. Today, without it, it is already difficult to stay in the game. A strong capability first creates a gap. When the market adopts it, it may stop differentiating the leaders—but it becomes the price of entry. AUTHOR'S PRACTICAL GUIDE < 90% —a large gap can still be created through availability alone. 98–99% among leaders—the next frontier is capital, assortment, data, speed, and access. The threshold depends on the calculation method, category, and market; it is not a universal standard. CAPABILITY ADVANTAGE → MARKET STANDARD → NEXT CAPABILITY"],["content/c33.html#pdf-186",210,"What can protect or devalue the advantage?","What can protect or devalue the advantage? After operational economics, check how long the advantage will remain yours. EVERYONE · 1. Data and innovation speed. Can a new technology be connected quickly? How fast is the path: idea → pilot → working function → scale? Who has the right to replicate the function created for competitors? EVERYONE · 2. Rules and ownership. What part of the advantage was created by the company, and what part exists only because of current regulation, licensing, access rules, or ownership rules? What would break first if one key rule changed? EVERYONE · 3. Migration of functions. Chain: warehouse, purchasing, data. Distributor: credit, IT, ecosystem. Manufacturer: access, route to the patient, data. Migration of functions helps assess resilience and the likely longevity of today's position. UNIQUE TO US · AVAILABLE TO COMPETITORS · MARKET STANDARD ADVANTAGE WINDOW is the period during which a difference creates economic value before competitors replicate it and it becomes a market standard."],["content/c33.html#pdf-187",211,"Our result versus the strongest competitor","Our result versus the strongest competitor Complete only the rows relevant to your model. What matters here is not the absolute number, but the difference versus the best available alternative. CHECKPOINT · US · STRONGEST COMPETITOR / MARKET · DIFFERENCE · EFFECT / NEXT MOVE. Rows: availability / service; inventory / turnover; cash cycle; assortment / patience time; integration speed; dependence on rules; functions moving to us; functions moving away from us. AFTER THE COMPARISON: 1. Who controls the critical function? 2. Who can block it? 3. Where is it migrating? 4. What capability must be built next? Improvement versus your own past performance does not yet prove a competitive advantage."],["content/c33.html#pdf-188",212,"Where does power lie in this market specifically?","Where does power lie in this market specifically? Find the configuration that best describes the current system—and the dependency that will change the economics of the next decision. FRAGMENTED RETAIL / STRONG DISTRIBUTOR. Power: warehouse, speed, credit, ERP/POS, pharmacy alliance. Can the distributor retain the ecosystem? CONSOLIDATING CHAINS. Power: own warehouse, direct purchasing, analytics, customer traffic. How can they scale without weakening their economics? VERTICALLY INTEGRATED GROUP. Power: retail + distribution + sometimes manufacturing. Which critical function is still outside its control? GOVERNMENT-CONSTRAINED MARKET. Power: ownership, licensing, regulation, mandatory access. What changes if one key rule changes? DIGITALLY CLOSED MARKET. Power: the ERP/POS platform controls data and integration. Who actually controls technology choices? TRANSITIONAL CONTEST MARKET. Functions are actively migrating and stable dominance has not yet emerged. Which function must be captured before the market stabilizes? Do not classify the market for the sake of an elegant framework. Find the dependency that will change the economics of the next decision."],["content/c33.html#pdf-189",213,"First the advantage. Then the economic result.","First the advantage. Then the economic result. Seven steps in the final check—from proven advantage to the next management move. NAME THE ADVANTAGE. What do we do better than the market—and what proves it? SHOW THE ECONOMIC RESULT. How does the advantage create sales, margin, returns, resilience, or lower risk? BREAK THE MARKET DOWN INTO FUNCTIONS. Who actually controls what? IDENTIFY THE CONTROLLERS. Where does formal ownership differ from actual control? IDENTIFY CONCENTRATION AND CONSTRAINTS. Which routes are irreplaceable? What rules and digital constraints sit above them? SEE THE MIGRATION. Who is gaining functions, data, capital, and decision rights? DECIDE WHAT TO SCALE. What strengthens the advantage, removes the constraint, or protects the advantage from being devalued? First the advantage. Then the economic result. If growth is chosen—the scaling mechanism. Then the dependency map and the next move."],["content/c34.html#notes-214",214,"NOTES","NOTES For working notes on the book"],["content/c34.html#notes-215",215,"NOTES","NOTES For working notes on the book"],["content/c34.html#notes-216",216,"NOTES","NOTES For working notes on the book"],["content/c34.html#notes-217",217,"NOTES","NOTES For working notes on the book"],["content/c34.html#notes-218",218,"NOTES","NOTES For working notes on the book"],["content/c34.html#notes-219",219,"NOTES","NOTES For working notes on the book"],["content/c35.html#gutsaga-tech-220",220,"GUTSAGA TECHNOLOGIES","TECHNOLOGY SYSTEM GUTSAGA TECHNOLOGIES From raw materials and production to the final point of sale Over more than twenty years of practical work, inventory, replenishment, and production management challenges have evolved into an integrated system of technologies. Recurring decisions are converted into managed rules, leaving people to set objectives and handle exceptions.  Raw materials Production Finished goods Central warehouse Regional warehouse Store / pharmacy Horizon — Adequate Inventory what inventory should be Zones & Decisions which decision matches the item's current inventory zone GAPS where a future gap will emerge Outlet how to bring excess inventory to the target Production Flow a single production priority The bands show the logic for applying the technologies, not rigid product boundaries. NOT FIVE SEPARATE DECISIONS. ONE FLOW MANAGEMENT SYSTEM. Inventory · State · Future risk · Excess · Production priority"],["content/c36.html#gutsaga-tech-221",221,"FIVE TECHNOLOGIES. ONE SYSTEM.","GUTSAGA SYSTEM FIVE TECHNOLOGIES. ONE SYSTEM. Each technology has its own management task, its own logic, and a separate path to detailed material.  HORIZON — ADEQUATE INVENTORY Manages inventory based on its actual state. The target level changes dynamically, maintaining availability without excess capital. WHAT INVENTORY SHOULD BE. ZONES & DECISIONS Every product at every location sits in one of the inventory zones. Management sets the boundaries and rules; the system applies standard decisions. DO NOT MANAGE EVERY SKU. MANAGE THE RULES. GAPS Looks ahead across the replenishment horizon and identifies in advance the period when inventory will run out before the next delivery. SEE THE SHORTAGE BEFORE IT HAPPENS. OUTLET Brings excess inventory to a defined state in a controlled way. Redistribution and price changes move inventory along a defined trajectory—to zero when a product is being discontinued, or to the target quantity. MANAGE THE INVENTORY TRAJECTORY TO THE TARGET. PRODUCTION FLOW Combines make-to-stock (MTS) and make-to-order (MTO) production in a single priority system and propagates the state of final buffers upstream through connected production stages. DIFFERENT ORDER TYPES. ONE PRIORITY LANGUAGE."],["content/c37.html#pdf-190",222,"About the Author","About the Author Serzas Gutsaga Entrepreneur, consultant, and developer of management technologies For more than twenty years, he has worked with systems for managing inventory, flow, and operational decisions. He has worked with manufacturers, distributors, and retail chains; since 2012, his primary focus has been the pharmaceutical market. His professional journey began with programming. That experience shaped his approach to business as a system: seeing cause-and-effect relationships and translating management logic into solutions that can be scaled. Since 2005, he has worked with Theory of Constraints technologies and Inherent Simplicity / Symphony. Later, practical experience led to the creation of proprietary methods and technologies. GUTSAGA — two complementary areas Gutsaga Consulting Diagnosis of business systems, development of competitive advantages, and process transformation. Gutsaga Technology Development of technologies for inventory, flow, and daily operational decision management."],["content/c37.html#pdf-191",223,"Professional Journey","Professional Journey From Programming to Management Technologies This was not a move from one company to another. It was a consistent progression: from software development to flow management, and from practice to a proprietary system of methods and technologies. 2001–2004 · FROM CODE TO FLOW Founding Euritecha and working with operational systems. In 2004—the first proprietary inventory management project and the start of DBM development. 2005–2018 · FROM FUNCTIONS TO A SYSTEM Projects based on Theory of Constraints principles, collaboration with Inherent Simplicity / Symphony, and development of proprietary solutions. In 2008—the first project in a pharmacy chain. 2012–2018 · PHARMA AS THE PRIMARY FIELD The pharmaceutical market becomes the primary focus. Practice scales into systematic work with inventory, promotions, shortages, and flow balancing. 2019–2024 · FROM METHODS TO A PLATFORM The decision to build Horizon from scratch—an independent inventory and flow management platform that brought years of practice together in a scalable technology. 2025 · CONSULTING AND TECHNOLOGIES Founding Gutsaga Consulting and establishing professional consulting as a separate business. Gutsaga Technology continues to develop management technologies and Horizon. 20+ YEARS OF SYSTEMATIC PRACTICE 28 MANUFACTURING FACILITIES 10,000+ PHARMACIES Find the constraint. Understand the system. Turn management logic into technology that can be scaled. GUTSAGA"],["content/c38.html#pdf-192",224,"After the Book","After the Book Let's Continue the Conversation If you would like to discuss the ideas in this book, test them in your own company, or challenge them, there are several ways to continue the conversation. A Book for the Team Additional copies for the management team and a discussion of the book: where the company stands today, what its advantage is, and which constraint will come next. Open a Conference The talk “Evolution of the Pharmaceutical Market” opens a conference. A few questions engage the audience in a conversation about the market, money, and competitive power—and establish the framework for the program. Look at Your Own Company Work with the management team to identify the current constraint, the real advantage, and the next step. If needed, continue the analysis using the company’s actual data. Disagree? Write to Us A counterexample, a different logic of market development, or a missing stage is especially valuable for the next edition. The book does not end the conversation—it gives you a reason to continue it using your own material. Next Step Scan the QR code to continue exploring the book’s ideas using your own material. Company diagnostics, a book for the team, a conference, materials, and feedback. gutsaga.tech/en/evolution info@gutsaga.tech GUTSAGA"]];
